    faq: [
      { q: "What is Princess Mary about?", a: "Princess Mary is the longest story in A Hero of Our Time and is told in Pechorin's own journal — a series of diary entries covering several months in Pyatigorsk and then Kislovodsk, Caucasian spa towns frequented by Russian high society. Pechorin arrives and meets Grushnitsky, a young cadet whom he despises and from whom he is separated by everything that matters — Grushnitsky is romantic, sentimental, posturing, desperately wanting to be admired. Princess Mary Ligovsky, a young society woman, is impressed by Grushnitsky and inclined toward him. Pechorin immediately decides to take her from Grushnitsky, not because he wants her but because he cannot leave things as they are. He also meets Princess Vera, a married woman he has loved before, who is the only person who really knows him. He seduces Mary — Mary falls genuinely in love with him — and simultaneously engineers a situation in which Grushnitsky humiliates himself. Grushnitsky, discovering what Pechorin has done, challenges him to a duel and arranges for Pechorin's pistol to be unloaded. Pechorin finds out, tells no one, and kills Grushnitsky. He then tells Mary he doesn't love her. He is sent away to a fort." },
      { q: "What makes Princess Mary the psychological masterpiece of A Hero of Our Time?", a: "Princess Mary is the psychological masterpiece of A Hero of Our Time because it is the longest and most sustained example of Pechorin's self-analysis — the journal format gives us direct access to his consciousness as events unfold, and what we find there is extraordinary. Pechorin does not simply describe what he does; he analyzes his own motives with a precision and honesty that would be admirable if the motives were better. He knows why he destroys things — not out of malice exactly, but out of an inability to leave the status quo undisturbed, out of a need to test everything to its limit. He knows that his treatment of Mary is cruel. He knows that the duel with Grushnitsky is not about honor. He knows that he is fundamentally incapable of the simple happiness that people around him achieve. The self-knowledge changes nothing, which is the point: Pechorin is the prototype of the character whose consciousness is perfectly developed and whose will is perfectly impotent." },
      { q: "What is the role of Princess Vera in Princess Mary?", a: "Princess Vera is the most interesting female character in A Hero of Our Time and, in some ways, the most important. She is a married woman who has loved Pechorin for years and who continues to love him with full knowledge of what he is. Unlike Mary, who falls for a performance, Vera sees Pechorin clearly — she knows his cruelty, his inability to love in any normal sense, his fundamental destructiveness — and she loves him anyway, or despite it, or because of it. The scene in which Pechorin chases her carriage when she leaves, then falls from his exhausted horse on the road and weeps — the one moment of genuine, unguarded feeling in the entire novel — is moving precisely because it comes after so much calculated detachment. He cannot catch her. He does not know if he would have known what to say if he had. Vera is the only person Pechorin is authentic with, and he loses her through the same process that loses him everything else." },
      { q: "What is Grushnitsky's role in Princess Mary?", a: "Grushnitsky is one of the most carefully constructed minor characters in Russian fiction — a young man who is everything Pechorin despises, partly because everything Grushnitsky is, Pechorin might have become if he had less intelligence and more illusions. Grushnitsky is romantic, posturing, full of borrowed sentiment and theatrical gestures — he wears a private's cloak to make himself look dramatically downtrodden, he speaks in the borrowed language of the Romantic hero, he imagines himself interesting. He is not villainous — he is simply a fool who has cast himself as a hero. Pechorin's destruction of him is therefore both contemptible (Pechorin is stronger, cleverer, and is doing it partly out of boredom) and comprehensible (Grushnitsky is himself not entirely honest — when he discovers what Pechorin has done, he agrees to have Pechorin's pistol unloaded, which is attempted murder). The duel is the inevitable conclusion of their relationship." },
      { q: "What should I read after Princess Mary?", a: "The complete A Hero of Our Time — particularly Taman, The Fatalist, and Bela — gives the full Pechorin portrait of which Princess Mary is the central panel. Eugene Onegin by Pushkin (1833) is the essential predecessor — Onegin's treatment of Tatyana as context for Pechorin's treatment of Princess Mary. Notes from Underground by Dostoevsky (1864) is the most intense response to the Pechorin type. Les Liaisons Dangereuses by Laclos (1782) is the essential European predecessor in the mode of calculated seduction. Rudin by Turgenev (1856) is the essential successor in the superfluous man tradition. For the question of the Romantic anti-hero in European fiction: Adolphe by Benjamin Constant (1816) and Manfred by Byron (1817) give the European context." },
    ],
