    slug: 'ward-no-6-chekhov',
    intro: "Ward No. 6 (1892) is Anton Chekhov's most celebrated short novel — the story of Dr Ragin, the superintendent of a provincial hospital, who withdraws from the miseries of hospital life into a comfortable philosophical detachment, and who is eventually committed to his own ward. The patient Ivan Gromov, who is obsessed with the fear of being wrongfully arrested, becomes Ragin's philosophical interlocutor. The story is Chekhov's most explicit treatment of the question of whether philosophical detachment — stoic acceptance, the refusal to be disturbed — is wisdom or cowardice.",
    startWith: "Ward No. 6",
