    faq: [
      { q: "What is A Dreary Story about?", a: "A Dreary Story (1889) is narrated by Nikolai Stepanovich, a celebrated professor of medicine who is in his sixties and is dying — of an unnamed disease, very slowly. The professor is intelligent, widely respected, genuinely distinguished in his field. As he is dying he begins to examine his life and his relationships: his wife, whom he has long since stopped loving but continues to live with; his daughter Liza, who has made an unfortunate engagement; his ward Katya, a young woman he raised who has tried to be an actress, failed, and now wanders from city to city in despair. The professor's confession is that he has no 'general idea' — no central organizing principle that holds his life together and gives it meaning. He has his work, he has his habits, he has his relationships, but when Katya comes to him in genuine distress and asks what to do, he cannot answer. He has nothing to give her. 'I do not know,' he says, and she leaves, and he understands this to be his failure." },
      { q: "What is the 'general idea' that the professor lacks in A Dreary Story?", a: "The professor's confession that he lacks a 'general idea' is the story's central philosophical statement. What he means is something like: a principle of life, a sense of what everything is for, a belief that could support him when his science and his work and his relationships are stripped away. He is a scientist; he believes in science; but his belief in science is a professional commitment, not an answer to the question of what life is for or how to face death. When Katya — a young woman he loves genuinely, who has nothing — comes to him and asks for help, his science cannot help her. His experience cannot help her. His intelligence cannot help her. The professor understands that the thing she needs — and the thing he needs — is something he does not have and cannot offer. The story is Chekhov's most explicit statement of the problem of the secular intellectual who has given his life to work without having developed the inner resources to face what work cannot address." },
      { q: "How does A Dreary Story compare to Tolstoy's The Death of Ivan Ilyich?", a: "A Dreary Story (1889) and The Death of Ivan Ilyich (1886) are the two supreme works in Russian literature about the intelligent man facing death and finding his life insufficient. The comparison is instructive. Tolstoy's story moves toward a conclusion — Ivan Ilyich, in the final days of his death, experiences a spiritual conversion that resolves his terror and gives him peace. The ending is consolatory. Chekhov's story has no such resolution: Nikolai Stepanovich dies without finding the 'general idea,' without being able to help Katya, without the conversion that would make his story comfortable. Tolstoy was a religious writer by the time he wrote Ivan Ilyich; Chekhov was not, and A Dreary Story is, among other things, a response to Tolstoy's consolation. The story presents what happens when the consolation of a spiritual resolution is not available." },
      { q: "Who is Katya in A Dreary Story?", a: "Katya is the professor's ward — a young woman he has raised and for whom he has genuine feeling. She tried to become an actress, failed, had an affair with a man who left her when she had a miscarriage, and has since wandered from city to city in a state of depression and purposelessness. Her situation and her character are counterpoints to the professor's: he has had a life of work and accomplishment and is dying; she has had nothing and is living. Both of them lack the 'general idea.' What makes Katya central to the story is the moment when she comes to the dying professor and asks him what to do — what she should do with her life. She has come to the person she loves most in the world, the only person she trusts, and she is asking in genuine despair. He has no answer. This failure — the failure of intelligence and experience and care to produce any answer to the question of what to do — is the story's most devastating moment." },
      { q: "What should I read after A Dreary Story?", a: "Ward No. 6 (1892) is the essential companion — the hospital and the question of detachment. The Lady with the Little Dog (1899) is his finest story. The Black Monk (1894) is the companion in the theme of a life approaching its end. The Duel (1891) is the most complex long story. For the tradition: The Death of Ivan Ilyich by Tolstoy (1886) is the essential companion — the same subject with a different resolution. Notes from Underground by Dostoevsky (1864) is the essential companion in the first-person confession tradition. Ivanov by Chekhov (1887) is the essential theatrical companion. For the broader Chekhov drama: Uncle Vanya and Three Sisters are the essential companions in the question of what to do with a life that hasn't worked out." },
    ],
