    intro: "The Kreutzer Sonata (1889) is Leo Tolstoy's most disturbing long story — a man on a train who confesses to a fellow traveler that he has murdered his wife. The confession unfolds the narrator Pozdnyshev's obsessive, misogynistic, and self-lacerating account of his marriage, his jealousy of a violinist who played Beethoven's Kreutzer Sonata with his wife, and his eventual murder of her. The novella is both a moral tract — Tolstoy's argument that sexual desire is the root of all evil — and a devastatingly observed psychological portrait.",
