    intro: "Our Friends in the North (1996) — produced by Tony Garnett — is a BBC television drama series examining what the relationship between four ordinary friends — Nicky, Geordie, Mary, and Tosker — and the specific social and political world of Britain from 1964 to 1995 — a world that is simultaneously transforming and being transformed by the specific social and political forces of housing corruption, organized crime, police corruption, the sexual revolution, the rise of Thatcherism, and the collapse of the political left — looks like from inside the four friends inside it across three decades. The series is characteristic of the Garnett tradition of examining what the relationship between ordinary people and the specific social and political worlds that are surrounding and defining them looks like from inside the ordinary people inside those worlds, applied to the specific social and political world of Britain from the Wilson era to the Blair era. These books share its interest in what the relationship between ordinary friends and the specific social and political world of Britain from the 1960s to the 1990s looks like from inside the ordinary friends inside that world as it is simultaneously transforming around them.",
    startWith: "Cathy Come Home (1966) by Jeremy Sandford — produced by Tony Garnett — the companion in his career — the BBC Wednesday Play examining what the relationship between an ordinary working-class family and the social and institutional world that is dismantling their family around them looks like from inside the ordinary family inside that relationship — the companion in his sustained examination of what the relationship between ordinary people and the specific social and political worlds that are surrounding and defining them looks like from inside the ordinary people inside those worlds.",
