    intro: "Bar Mitzvah Boy (1978) as a published text by Jack Rosenthal — the published version of the celebrated BBC Play for Today, examining what the relationship between a thirteen-year-old Jewish boy, his approaching bar mitzvah, and the family and social world that are all expecting him to go through with it looks like from inside the boy inside that relationship — represents the companion in the published tradition to the television play that established Rosenthal's reputation as one of the most important television writers of his generation. The play — in both its television and its published form — is notable as an examination of what the relationship between a young person and a specific social and religious ritual that they have been told they must go through, and the social and domestic world of the Jewish family that is surrounding and expecting them to go through it, looks like from inside the young person inside all three of those things. These books share its interest in what the relationship between a young person and a specific social or religious ritual that they are being expected to go through, and the family and social world that is surrounding and expecting them to go through it, looks like from inside the young person inside it.",
    startWith: "Bar Mitzvah Boy (1976) by Jack Rosenthal — the companion in his career — the BBC Play for Today in its original television form, examining what the relationship between a thirteen-year-old Jewish boy, his approaching bar mitzvah, and the family and social world that are all expecting him to go through with it looks like from inside the boy inside that relationship — the companion in his sustained examination of what the relationship between ordinary people and the social and religious worlds they are inside looks like from inside the ordinary people inside it.",
