    intro: "Made in Britain (1982) by Tony Garnett is a BBC television film produced by Tony Garnett and directed by Alan Clarke — one of the most celebrated works of the Garnett-Clarke collaboration — examining what the relationship between a sixteen-year-old skinhead with a National Front tattoo, the social and institutional world of the welfare state and the youth offending system that is trying to process and to manage him, and the social world that has produced him looks like from inside the young man inside all three of those things. The film is notable as an early performance by Tim Roth and as an examination of what the relationship between the social and institutional world of the British welfare state — specifically, the social services and the youth justice system — and the young people inside that world looks like from inside the young person who is refusing to be processed inside it. These books share its interest in what the relationship between a young person who is refusing to be processed inside the social and institutional world that is surrounding them and that social and institutional world looks like from inside the young person inside it.",
