    intro: "Between the Lines (1992) by Tony Garnett is a BBC television police drama series produced by Tony Garnett — one of his most celebrated productions in his role as an independent producer — examining what the relationship between a group of police officers working in the internal affairs department of the Metropolitan Police, the social and institutional world of the police force they are inside, and the moral and political world of police corruption and accountability that they are navigating looks like from inside the different police officers inside all three of those things. The series is notable as one of the most sophisticated and morally serious British police dramas of its era and as an example of Garnett's sustained commitment to examining the relationship between the social and institutional world of British institutions and the ordinary people inside those institutions. These books share its interest in what the relationship between the people inside an institution, the institution they are inside, and the moral and political world of accountability and corruption that surrounds that institution looks like from inside the people inside it.",
    startWith: "Cathy Come Home (1966) by Jeremy Sandford — the companion in his career — the BBC Wednesday Play that Garnett produced examining what the relationship between an ordinary working-class family and the social and institutional world that is dismantling their family around them looks like from inside the ordinary family inside that relationship — the companion in his sustained examination of what the relationship between the social and institutional world of British institutions and the ordinary people inside and around those institutions looks like from inside the ordinary people inside it.",
