    intro: "Boys from the Blackstuff: The Novel (1982) by Alan Bleasdale is the novelisation of Alan Bleasdale's acclaimed BBC television series — the book that accompanied the broadcast of the series and that made the characters and the world of the series available to readers who had not seen the television production. The novel is notable not as a simple transcription of the television scripts but as a reworking of the material into a different form — the form of the novel — that brings out different aspects of the characters and their world than the television version. The television series — which examined what the relationship between a group of unemployed men in Liverpool, the social and economic world that has destroyed their working lives, and the different ways they are each trying to survive inside that destruction looks like from inside the different men inside that relationship — is one of the most celebrated works of British television. The novel form allows a deeper examination of what is inside the different men inside the story than the television version can provide. These books share its interest in what the relationship between working-class people, the social and economic world that is destroying their working lives and their dignity, and the different ways they are each trying to survive inside that destruction looks like from inside the different people inside it.",
