    intro: "Are You Lonesome Tonight? (1985) by Alan Bleasdale is a stage play about Elvis Presley — or rather, about a man who believes he is Elvis Presley, or who is playing Elvis Presley, or who has been so consumed by the phenomenon of Elvis Presley that the distinction between the man and the performance has collapsed — examining what the relationship between a man, the social and commercial world that has turned him into a phenomenon, and the interior life that he is still living inside that social and commercial world looks like from inside the man inside all three of those things. The play is notable as one of Bleasdale's most theatrically inventive works and as an examination of what the relationship between a person, the celebrity and the social world that celebrity creates around and inside a person, and what is left of the person inside that celebrity looks like from inside the person inside it. These books share its interest in what the relationship between a person, the social and commercial world that has turned them into something larger than themselves, and the interior life that they are still living inside that social and commercial world looks like from inside the person inside it.",
    startWith: "Boys from the Blackstuff (1982) by Alan Bleasdale — the companion in his career — the television series examining what the relationship between a group of unemployed men in Liverpool, the social and economic world that has destroyed their working lives, and the different ways they are each trying to survive inside that destruction looks like from inside the different men inside that relationship — the companion in his sustained examination of what the relationship between ordinary people inside extraordinary pressures and what that relationship reveals about both the ordinary people and the extraordinary pressures looks like from inside the people inside it.",
