    intro: "The Forgotten Faces (1961) by Peter Watkins is an early BBC television documentary drama about the 1956 Hungarian Revolution — a pseudo-documentary that simulates the conventions of news footage to present a dramatisation of what the revolution looked like from inside the ordinary Hungarian people inside it. The film is notable as an early example of Watkins's pseudo-documentary approach — the same approach he would develop in The War Game (1965) and Culloden (1964) — and as an examination of what political revolution looks like from inside the ordinary people inside it. These books share its interest in what political revolution and the relationship between ordinary people and the extraordinary political events around them look like from inside the people inside both.",
