    intro: "Shoot to Kill (1990) by Peter Kosminsky is a television drama examining the events of 1982 and 1984 in which members of the Royal Ulster Constabulary are alleged to have operated a shoot-to-kill policy against suspected IRA members in Northern Ireland — examining the subsequent investigation by Deputy Chief Constable John Stalker into those shootings and the political pressure that was brought to bear to limit and ultimately terminate that investigation. The drama examines what the relationship between the state's use of lethal force outside the law and the state's subsequent attempt to conceal and suppress the evidence of that use of lethal force looks like from inside the different people inside that relationship. These books share its interest in what state violence and the suppression of accountability for that violence look like from inside the people inside them.",
