    intro: "Narrow Road to the Deep North (1968) by Edward Bond is a play in two parts set in Japan over several centuries. In the prologue, the poet Basho passes a starving baby on his journey to seek enlightenment and does not stop to help the child. The play then follows the child — who survives and becomes a tyrant — and the consequences of Basho's decision across the subsequent history of a Japanese city occupied by colonial forces. It is Bond's most direct examination of the relationship between art and political responsibility and what a great artist's choice to prioritise art over life costs the people around him. These books share its interest in what it means to choose aesthetic distance over political commitment and what that choice creates.",
