    intro: "The Cosmonaut's Last Message to the Woman He Once Loved in the Former Soviet Union (1999) by David Greig is one of the most ambitious plays of recent Scottish theatre. The play follows a Soviet cosmonaut, Oleg, who is orbiting the earth in a space station that can no longer communicate with the ground — the Soviet Union has dissolved and no one has come to bring him back — while on earth the lives of a series of people who are connected to him (or to each other) are followed: his ex-wife, a man who intercepts communications, a young woman, a diplomat. The play is about disconnection, missed connection, what a country that no longer exists means for the people inside it, and what love is when you can no longer reach the person you love. These books share its interest in what the fall of the Soviet Union meant and what disconnection looks like.",
    startWith: "Tinker Tailor Soldier Spy (1974) by John le Carré — the companion in fiction about the Cold War world that the Soviet cosmonaut comes from: le Carré's account of Soviet moles and the intelligence world that is the backdrop to what has been done to Oleg's life.",
