    intro: "Ines de Castro (1989) by Jo Clifford is a play about Inês de Castro, the Spanish noblewoman who became the secret wife of Dom Pedro, heir to the Portuguese throne, in the fourteenth century. When Dom Pedro's father, the King, had her murdered in 1355 for political reasons, Pedro vowed revenge, and when he became king he exhumed her body and had her crowned queen posthumously. Clifford's play examines the tragedy of a woman destroyed by the political forces around her and what it means to love someone at a cost to the state. These books share its interest in what the Inês de Castro story means and what historical tragedies of desire and power look like.",
    startWith: "A History of Portugal (1947) by A.H. de Oliveira Marques — the companion in historical context that is the best general history of Portugal for an English-speaking reader and the context for understanding what the court of Dom Alfonso IV was and what the Inês de Castro affair meant politically.",
