    intro: "The Love of the Nightingale (1988) by Timberlake Wertenbaker is a retelling of the myth of Philomela and Procne. Philomela is the sister of Procne, who has married King Tereus of Thrace. When Tereus is sent to Athens to bring Philomela to join her sister, he rapes Philomela on the way and cuts out her tongue to prevent her from telling anyone. Philomela finds a way to tell her story through a tapestry. The play is concerned with voice and silence — with the ways in which women are silenced, what they do to break that silence, and what the recovery of speech means. These books share its interest in what the myth means and what Wertenbaker was doing with classical material in her career.",
