    intro: "Woman in Mind (1985) by Alan Ayckbourn is one of his most formally ambitious plays. Susan, a vicar's wife, has been knocked unconscious by a garden rake; when she comes to, she is surrounded by her ideal family — an attentive husband, a beautiful daughter, a charismatic brother — who are a fantasy of her own creation, the family she wishes she had. As the play proceeds, Susan's real family (the actually rather awful vicar Gerald, his unpleasant sister Muriel, their alienated son Rick) intrudes on her fantasy world, and the fantasy becomes harder to maintain. The play is told from Susan's perspective — we see what she sees, including the fantasy — and it is one of the most exact portraits of a person's breakdown in British drama. These books share its interest in what fantasy and breakdown look like from the inside.",
