    intro: "Benefactors (1984) by Michael Frayn follows two couples across twenty years: David, an idealistic architect who is commissioned to redevelop a South London slum into a tower-block estate; Jane, his wife; and their neighbours Colin and Sheila. The play is narrated by all four characters simultaneously — a Frayn device that means we hear each person's account of the same events, each radically different from the others. It is a play about idealism and its costs: about what happens when you try to do good for people, what they want you to do for them, and what the difference is. These books share its interest in what urban redevelopment was in 1960s Britain, what idealism looks like in practice, and what Frayn's theatre was doing in this period.",
