    intro: "Donkeys' Years (1976) by Michael Frayn is set at a college reunion at an Oxbridge-style college — a group of middle-aged men returning after twenty-five years to the college where they were students. The comedy is about the gap between who they were then and who they have become, what they pretend to be and what they actually are, and the way that the college environment instantly reduces them all to their younger selves. It is one of the funniest British comedies of the second half of the twentieth century and also one of the most exact about the English class structure. These books share its interest in what university reunions are, what the relationship between past and present selves looks like, and what Frayn's comedy was doing.",
