    startWith: "Woyzeck",
    books: [
      { title: "Woyzeck", author: "Jack Thorne after Georg Büchner", year: 2017, note: "Start here — Woyzeck in contemporary London and the Royal British Legion as the examination of what systems do to the people at the bottom of them; the Old Vic production with John Boyega as one of the most significant Büchner adaptations in recent British theatre" },
      { title: "Woyzeck", author: "Georg Büchner", year: 1879, note: "The essential companion — the source; Büchner's original unfinished play as the companion to Thorne's adaptation and as one of the founding texts of modern drama — the first play to make a working-class man the tragic protagonist" },
      { title: "Hope", author: "Jack Thorne", year: 2013, note: "The essential companion — the companion in Thorne's career; the Labour council and the cuts as the companion in his examination of what systems do to people" },
      { title: "Junkyard", author: "Jack Thorne", year: 2017, note: "The essential companion — the companion in Thorne's career from the same year; the children at the adventure playground as the companion in his examination of what communities and systems can do for people when they choose to" },
      { title: "Catch-22", author: "Joseph Heller", year: 1961, note: "The essential companion in fiction — Heller's examination of what military institutions do to the men inside them as the companion to Woyzeck's examination of what the army and its systems do to its soldier" },
      { title: "The Outsider", author: "Albert Camus", year: 1942, note: "The essential companion in fiction — Camus's examination of a man who commits an act of violence and who cannot feel what he is supposed to feel as the companion to Woyzeck's examination of a man who is destroyed and what he does in his destruction" },
      { title: "The Road", author: "Cormac McCarthy", year: 2006, note: "The essential companion in fiction — McCarthy's examination of a man who will do anything to protect the person he loves in a world that is entirely against him as the companion to Woyzeck" },
      { title: "Kitchen", author: "Arnold Wesker", year: 1957, note: "The essential companion in drama — Wesker's examination of what a kitchen full of workers reveals about the class system and what it does to the people at the bottom as the companion to Woyzeck's examination of the same" },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: "What is Woyzeck by Jack Thorne?", a: "Woyzeck (2017) is Jack Thorne's adaptation of Georg Büchner's unfinished nineteenth-century play, transposed from its original early nineteenth-century German setting to contemporary London. Thorne sets his adaptation on Remembrance Sunday at a Royal British Legion club, with the soldier Woyzeck as a man who is being slowly destroyed by everything around him: his captain demeans and bullies him, a doctor conducts dietary experiments on him, his partner Marie is unfaithful with a drummer, and the community around him offers no real support. The play examines what happens to a man when every system and institution he lives inside grinds him down, and what he does when he reaches breaking point. The Old Vic production starred John Boyega as Woyzeck." },
      { q: "What should I read after Woyzeck by Jack Thorne?", a: "After Woyzeck, read the original Woyzeck by Georg Büchner (the source — one of the founding texts of modern drama), Hope by Jack Thorne (the companion in his career — what systems do to people), Catch-22 by Joseph Heller (the fiction companion to what military institutions do to the men inside them), The Outsider by Albert Camus (the fiction companion to a man who commits violence and cannot feel what he is supposed to feel), Kitchen by Arnold Wesker (the drama companion to what the class system does to people at the bottom), and Junkyard by Jack Thorne (the companion in his career from the same year). The Road by Cormac McCarthy is the fiction companion to a man who will do anything to protect what he loves in a world entirely against him." },
      { q: "What is Georg Büchner's original Woyzeck?", a: "Georg Büchner's Woyzeck was written in 1836 and 1837 but left unfinished when Büchner died of typhus in 1837 at the age of twenty-three. It was not performed until 1913, seventy-six years after it was written. The play is based on the real case of Johann Christian Woyzeck, a soldier who murdered his girlfriend in Leipzig in 1821 and was executed for it. Büchner's play depicts Woyzeck as a victim of the social and military systems he lives inside: he is bullied by his captain, used as an experimental subject by a doctor, and driven to distraction by his girlfriend's infidelity. Woyzeck is considered a founding text of modern drama — the first play to make a working-class man the central tragic figure rather than a prince or nobleman, and a major influence on expressionist and modern theatre. The play has been adapted many times, including by Alban Berg as an opera (Wozzeck, 1925)." },
      { q: "Why does Jack Thorne set Woyzeck in contemporary London?", a: "Jack Thorne's decision to transpose Büchner's play to contemporary London on Remembrance Sunday is a deliberate political choice. By setting the play among veterans and working-class people gathered at a Royal British Legion club, Thorne is asking what Woyzeck's story means in contemporary Britain: what the military does to the men and women inside it, what happens to veterans when they return from active service, and what working-class communities look like when the institutions that were supposed to support them have been eroded. The Remembrance Sunday setting gives the play's examination of what the army does to Woyzeck a specific contemporary resonance: a day when the nation is supposed to honor its soldiers, while the play examines what the nation actually does to them. The choice connects Büchner's nineteenth-century examination of class and institutional violence to contemporary debates about austerity, veterans' welfare, and what the state owes its people." },
      { q: "Who played Woyzeck in the Old Vic production?", a: "The Old Vic production of Jack Thorne's Woyzeck in 2017 starred John Boyega as Woyzeck. Boyega, who had achieved international fame for his role as Finn in Star Wars: The Force Awakens (2015), brought extraordinary intensity and physicality to the role. The production was directed by Joe Murphy and was one of the most acclaimed theatrical events of 2017. Boyega's performance was widely praised for its emotional power and for the way it made Woyzeck's gradual destruction feel viscerally real. The casting of Boyega also added a layer of social commentary to Thorne's already politically charged adaptation: a Black British actor in the role of a soldier being destroyed by the systems around him made explicit some of the racial dimensions of class and military service in contemporary Britain." },
    ],
