    faq: [
      { q: "What is Labour of Love by James Graham?", a: "Labour of Love (2017) by James Graham follows David, a Labour MP who has held a marginal constituency in the English Midlands since 1990, and Jean, who has been his constituency agent for the same period. The play is structured in two acts: the first act moves forward in time from 1990 to 2017, and the second act moves backward in time from 2017 to 1990. This unusual structure allows Graham to show both the arc of what happened to Labour over these years — from John Smith to Tony Blair to Gordon Brown to Ed Miliband to Jeremy Corbyn — and what happened to David and Jean's personal and professional relationship. The play is simultaneously a political history of the Labour Party and an examination of what two people who care deeply about politics and about each other do when they disagree about what their party should be." },
      { q: "What should I read after Labour of Love by James Graham?", a: "After Labour of Love, read This House by James Graham (the companion in Graham's career — the Labour whips of 1974 to 1979 and how the party works), The Blair Years by Alastair Campbell (the memoir companion to what the Labour Party became under New Labour), The End of the Party by Andrew Rawnsley (the nonfiction companion to the Blair and Brown years and how Labour lost power), A Very British Coup by Chris Mullin (the fiction companion to what a left-wing Labour government looks like), and Ink by Graham (the companion from the same year — the Sun and what the media and politics produce together). The Thick of It by Armando Iannucci is the television companion to what political culture looks like from inside." },
      { q: "What period does Labour of Love cover?", a: "Labour of Love covers the period from 1990 to 2017 — essentially the entire period from just before the death of Labour leader John Smith through the leadership of Tony Blair, Gordon Brown, Ed Miliband, and Jeremy Corbyn. 1990 is significant in the play because it was before John Smith became Labour leader in 1992 (after Neil Kinnock resigned following the 1992 election defeat), and the play's starting point allows Graham to examine what the Labour Party looked like before New Labour transformed it in the 1990s. The ending in 2017 means that the play covers the Jeremy Corbyn era and the transformation of Labour's politics under his leadership — a transformation that David and Jean, who represent different views of what Labour should be, have very different feelings about." },
      { q: "What is the unusual structure of Labour of Love?", a: "Labour of Love has an unusual two-act structure in which the first act moves forward in time from 1990 to 2017 and the second act moves backward in time from 2017 to 1990. The play shows the same relationship — David and Jean's partnership in the constituency — from two temporal directions, and the effect is to allow the audience to see both the accumulation of what has happened (as time moves forward in the first act) and the origins of what we already know has happened (as time moves backward in the second act). This structure reflects the play's examination of memory, loyalty, and what people carry from their past into their present: by moving backward in the second act, Graham allows the audience to see David and Jean at the beginning of their partnership with the knowledge of where they will end up, which changes the meaning of what they say and do." },
      { q: "Was Labour of Love a commercial success in the West End?", a: "Labour of Love was a major commercial success in the West End, running at the Noël Coward Theatre for several months in 2017 and 2018. The production starred Martin Freeman as David and Tamsin Greig as Jean, and the casting of two well-known actors was significant in attracting a larger audience than political plays typically reach. The play received very strong reviews and was one of the talked-about theatrical events of the 2017 season. Its commercial success demonstrated that James Graham's approach to political theatre — making political history personal and accessible through specific characters and relationships — could find a large audience, and it confirmed him as one of the most commercially as well as critically successful playwrights of his generation." },
    ],
