    faq: [
      { q: "What is Rabbit by Nina Raine?", a: "Rabbit (2006) by Nina Raine is set at a dinner party for Bella, who is turning thirty. She has gathered her friends: Richard, who is in love with her; Tom, who works in finance and is increasingly disconnected from what he once wanted; Emily, who is practical and somewhat judgmental; and Sandy, her boyfriend. Over the course of the evening, what each of them wants — from each other, from their lives, from what they had expected their thirties to look like — becomes increasingly apparent, and the social surface of the dinner party is gradually stripped away. The play is noted for its wit and its ear for the way educated people in contemporary London talk, and it established Raine's reputation as an acutely observant writer of social comedy that also examines deeper questions about what people want and cannot say." },
      { q: "What should I read after Rabbit by Nina Raine?", a: "After Rabbit, read Tribes by Nina Raine (the companion in Raine's career — Billy and the family and what communication costs), Consent by Nina Raine (the later companion in her career — two couples and what consent means in law and in life), Who's Afraid of Virginia Woolf? by Edward Albee (the companion to what a social gathering reveals about what people actually want from and do to each other), Normal People by Sally Rooney (the fiction companion to two people who want each other and cannot say so directly), and Conversations with Friends by Rooney (the fiction companion to what young people do with each other in the contemporary world). About a Boy by Nick Hornby is the fiction companion to an educated urban man and what he actually wants." },
      { q: "Why is Nina Raine's play called Rabbit?", a: "The title Rabbit refers to a game that the characters play during the dinner party — a game in which each player tries to keep saying the word 'rabbit' in conversation without anyone noticing. The game is a metaphor for the social dynamic of the play: the characters are all trying to do or get something — Richard is trying to get close to Bella, Tom is trying to maintain the surface of his life, Bella is trying to understand what she wants — without being seen to be doing it. The social surface of the dinner party is a kind of performance in which everyone is playing the rabbit game: saying one thing while trying to do another, maintaining the surface of wit and friendship while underneath something else entirely is happening. The title also invokes the image of a rabbit frozen in headlights, which is how several of the characters relate to their lives: unable to move, unable to change, caught in the beam of what they have become." },
      { q: "Where was Rabbit by Nina Raine first performed?", a: "Rabbit was first performed at the Old Red Lion Theatre in Islington, London, in 2006 — at a pub theatre, the kind of fringe venue where many significant British playwrights have had their debuts. The production was directed by Nina Raine herself, who is also a director as well as a playwright. The play subsequently transferred to the Hampstead Theatre, which gave it a larger audience and more critical attention. The Hampstead Theatre is known for producing new work and has a reputation for nurturing emerging playwrights, and the transfer there was an indication that Rabbit was considered one of the more significant debut plays of the season." },
      { q: "What themes does Rabbit share with Nina Raine's later work?", a: "Rabbit establishes several of the themes and preoccupations that run through Nina Raine's subsequent plays. The examination of what educated, middle-class urban people want from each other and cannot say directly — the gap between social surface and emotional reality — is central to Rabbit and runs through both Tribes and Consent. The specific milieu of educated London professional life appears in all three plays. The interest in what communication is and what it costs — in Rabbit, the dinner party conversation that conceals more than it reveals; in Tribes, the question of which language Billy belongs in; in Consent, the question of what the legal concept of consent and the lived experience of consent have to do with each other — is a through-line. And the form of drawing-room social drama, used in Rabbit and in Consent, shows Raine's debt to earlier British dramatists while also marking what she does differently with the form." },
    ],
