    slug: 'debbie-tucker-green-random',
    intro: "Random (2008) by debbie tucker green is a short play — it can be performed in under thirty minutes — in which a sister tells the story of a day in her south London family's life: her mother arguing with her brother at breakfast, her father going to work, herself going to school; and then she tells what happened to her brother that day, that he was randomly stabbed on the street; the play is performed by a single actress playing all the family members.",
    startWith: "Random",
    books: [
      { title: "Random", author: "debbie tucker green", year: 2008, note: "Start here — the sister telling the story of her brother's death as the examination of what random street violence does to a family in south London; the Royal Court production as one of the defining plays of early twenty-first century British drama about race, grief, and what communities absorb" },
      { title: "Stoning Mary", author: "debbie tucker green", year: 2005, note: "The essential companion — the companion in debbie tucker green's career; the European family fighting over an AIDS drug as the companion in her examination of race, violence, and what the world does to Black families" },
      { title: "Generations", author: "debbie tucker green", year: 2005, note: "The essential companion — the companion in debbie tucker green's career; the African family killed by AIDS as the companion in her examination of what violence does to families across generations" },
      { title: "The Bluest Eye", author: "Toni Morrison", year: 1970, note: "The essential companion in fiction — Morrison's examination of what racism and violence do to a Black family as the companion to Random's examination of what random violence does to a south London family" },
      { title: "Brother, I'm Dying", author: "Edwidge Danticat", year: 2007, note: "The essential companion in memoir — Danticat's examination of violence, immigration, and what it does to a family as the companion to Random's examination of what violence does to the family at the center of the play" },
      { title: "The Lonely Londoners", author: "Sam Selvon", year: 1956, note: "The essential companion in fiction — Selvon's examination of the experience of Black Caribbean immigrants in London as the companion to Random's south London setting and its examination of what that world is" },
      { title: "Small Island", author: "Andrea Levy", year: 2004, note: "The essential companion in fiction — Levy's examination of what it means to be Black and in Britain as the companion to Random's examination of a south London Black family and what happens to them" },
      { title: "Giovanni's Room", author: "James Baldwin", year: 1956, note: "The essential companion in fiction — Baldwin's examination of what it costs to be who you are in a world that doesn't want you as the companion to Random's examination of a family who absorb a loss the world doesn't much care about" },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: "What is Random by debbie tucker green?", a: "Random (2008) by debbie tucker green is a short play performed by a single actress who plays all the members of a south London family: the sister who narrates, her mother, her father, and her brother. The play begins as a morning in the family's life — the mother arguing with the brother about whether he is wearing the right things for the day, the father leaving for work, the sister going to school. Then the sister tells what happened to her brother that day: he was randomly stabbed on the street and died. The play examines what this does to the family, to the sister who has to carry the story, and to the south London community in which this kind of random violence is not unusual. The play is noted for debbie tucker green's distinctive rhythmic, fragmented dialogue style and for the virtuosic demands it places on the single performer." },
      { q: "What should I read after Random by debbie tucker green?", a: "After Random, read Stoning Mary by debbie tucker green (the companion in debbie tucker green's career — the examination of race, violence, and what the world does to Black families), Generations by debbie tucker green (the companion to what violence does to families across generations), The Bluest Eye by Toni Morrison (the fiction companion to what racism and violence do to a Black family), Small Island by Andrea Levy (the fiction companion to what it means to be Black and in Britain), and Brother, I'm Dying by Edwidge Danticat (the memoir companion to violence, immigration, and what it does to a family). The Lonely Londoners by Sam Selvon is the fiction companion to the experience of Black people in London." },
      { q: "What is debbie tucker green's writing style like?", a: "debbie tucker green's writing style is highly distinctive and unlike almost any other playwright working in British theatre. Her dialogue is rhythmic, fragmented, and often leaves out the words that standard dramatic dialogue would include — articles, prepositions, connecting clauses — creating a compressed, musical language that has to be performed rather than simply read. Her stage directions are minimal or absent. Her plays often don't use standard punctuation conventions, and they often use spacing and line breaks on the page to indicate rhythm rather than simply indicating who speaks when. This style has been compared to poetry, and debbie tucker green has spoken about the influence of music — particularly hip hop and R&B — on the way she structures dialogue. The style is demanding for performers but allows debbie tucker green to convey very specific rhythms of speech and thought." },
      { q: "Is 'debbie tucker green' correctly written in lowercase?", a: "Yes — debbie tucker green writes her name entirely in lowercase and without capitals, and this is not an error but a deliberate stylistic and political choice. Productions and publications of her work use this lowercase styling. The lowercase name is part of her practice as a writer: her work consistently refuses easy categorization and conventional formatting, and the lowercase name is one aspect of that. When referring to her in writing, the lowercase styling should be respected rather than corrected." },
      { q: "What is the significance of the 'random' of the title in debbie tucker green's play?", a: "The word 'random' in the title of debbie tucker green's play has multiple resonances. Most directly, it refers to the randomness of the violence: the brother was not targeted specifically, he was simply in the wrong place, and any young Black man in south London could have been stabbed that day. But 'random' in British slang (especially as used by young people in London in the 2000s) also means arbitrary, weird, or out of place — and the play is interested in the randomness of who gets to mourn, whose lives are treated as important, whose deaths register in the news and whose don't. The title also points to the play's formal randomness: the way the sister moves between different family members, different moments in the day, creates a kind of formal randomness that mirrors the arbitrary way the violence arrives." },
    ],
