    faq: [
      { q: "What is Trade by debbie tucker green?", a: "Trade (2005) by debbie tucker green is a short play set in the Caribbean. Three British Black women — Older Woman, Middle Woman, and Younger Woman — are on holiday, and they encounter young local Black men who approach them offering sexual services. The play examines what happens in these encounters and the dynamics they produce: who has power, who is being exploited, who is doing the exploiting, and what everyone involved is telling themselves about what they are doing. The play refuses comfortable positions: the women are not simply villains (they are Black British women in a world that often exploits them) and the men are not simply victims (they are agents making choices in a constrained economy). Trade is a work about the intersection of race, gender, class, and global inequality and what those intersections produce when they meet in a tourist economy." },
      { q: "What should I read after Trade by debbie tucker green?", a: "After Trade, read A Small Place by Jamaica Kincaid (the direct nonfiction companion to what tourism does to a Caribbean island), Lucy by Jamaica Kincaid (the fiction companion to what the relationship between the Caribbean and the First World looks like from the Caribbean side), Random by debbie tucker green (the companion in debbie tucker green's career), Girl, Woman, Other by Bernardine Evaristo (the fiction companion to what it means to be Black and female in Britain and the wider world), and The Brief Wondrous Life of Oscar Wao by Junot Díaz (the fiction companion to what the Dominican Republic is from inside and from outside). Stoning Mary by debbie tucker green is the companion from the same year." },
      { q: "How does Trade examine power and exploitation?", a: "Trade is remarkable for the complexity with which it examines who has power and who is being exploited in the encounters it depicts. The obvious reading is that the British women are exploiting the local men — they are tourists with money in a poor economy, and the transactions they enter into are shaped by that inequality. But debbie tucker green also examines what the men are doing and thinking: they are agents who have chosen this work, who have their own assessments of the women, and who are not simply passive objects of exploitation. The women, meanwhile, are Black British women who are themselves in a complicated position in relation to power in Britain and globally. Trade refuses to simplify these dynamics into a straightforward exploitation narrative and instead holds the complexity in view, making everyone in the play a person with agency and everyone in the play a person constrained by larger forces." },
      { q: "How does Trade relate to Jamaica Kincaid's A Small Place?", a: "A Small Place (1988) by Jamaica Kincaid is one of the most important essays about tourism and the Caribbean, and it is the direct nonfiction companion to Trade. Kincaid's essay is addressed to the tourist — to the person who comes to Antigua and sees beauty and pleasure without understanding what their presence means to the people who live there, without understanding that the Antigua they see is a product of colonialism and poverty. Kincaid is angry and direct: the tourist's pleasure is the local person's humiliation, and the beautiful island the tourist experiences is the impoverished country the Antiguans actually live in. Trade shares Kincaid's anger and her refusal to comfort the audience; the play puts the tourist encounter on stage and refuses to allow the British women (or the audience) to enjoy the fantasy of the uncomplicated Caribbean holiday." },
      { q: "Where was Trade first performed?", a: "Trade (2005) was first performed at the Royal Shakespeare Company's Other Place in Stratford-upon-Avon, in a production directed by Sacha Wares. debbie tucker green's work has been associated primarily with the Royal Court Theatre, but Trade was part of a series of short plays commissioned by the RSC. Like many of debbie tucker green's plays, Trade is very short — it can be performed in well under an hour — and the RSC production was part of a double or triple bill. The play was subsequently revived and has been widely produced at theatre festivals and by theatre companies in Britain and internationally." },
    ],
