    slug: 'caryl-churchill-love-and-information',
    intro: "Love and Information (2012) by Caryl Churchill consists of fifty-seven short scenes — some lasting only seconds, some several minutes — organized into seven 'random' sequences, plus a 'coda' section; each scene examines a different relationship between people and information, or between people and love, or both, and the play asks what knowledge is and what connection is in a world saturated with data.",
    startWith: "Love and Information",
    books: [
      { title: "Love and Information", author: "Caryl Churchill", year: 2012, note: "Start here — the fifty-seven scenes and seven sections as the examination of what information and love are and how they relate to each other; the Royal Court production as Churchill's most formally radical work since The Skriker and as one of the defining British plays of the twenty-first century" },
      { title: "Far Away", author: "Caryl Churchill", year: 2000, note: "The essential companion — the companion in Churchill's career; the world at war as the companion in Churchill's examination of moral frameworks and what becomes of them" },
      { title: "A Number", author: "Caryl Churchill", year: 2002, note: "The essential companion — the companion in Churchill's career; cloning and identity as the companion in Churchill's examination of what makes a person who they are" },
      { title: "The Information", author: "Martin Amis", year: 1995, note: "The essential companion in fiction — Amis's examination of what information does to relationships and to the people who carry it as the companion to Love and Information" },
      { title: "The Shallows", author: "Nicholas Carr", year: 2010, note: "The essential companion in nonfiction — Carr's examination of what the internet and information abundance is doing to human cognition as the companion to Love and Information's examination of what information saturation means for human connection" },
      { title: "Never Let Me Go", author: "Kazuo Ishiguro", year: 2005, note: "The essential companion in fiction — Ishiguro's examination of what love means and what memory means as the companion to Love and Information's examination of the same" },
      { title: "Oryx and Crake", author: "Margaret Atwood", year: 2003, note: "The essential companion in fiction — Atwood's examination of what happens to humanity in a world shaped by information and genetic technology as the companion to Love and Information" },
      { title: "The Remains of the Day", author: "Kazuo Ishiguro", year: 1989, note: "The essential companion in fiction — Ishiguro's examination of what a person does with information they have suppressed as the companion to Love and Information's examination of what people do with what they know and don't know" },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: "What is Love and Information by Caryl Churchill?", a: "Love and Information (2012) consists of fifty-seven short scenes — some of them only a line or two, some lasting several minutes — organized into seven sections plus a coda. The scenes are written to be produced in any order within each section (with the exception of the final scenes of each section, which are marked as fixed). Each scene examines a relationship between people that involves either information, love, or both: a couple arguing about whether one person told the other something important; a person receiving a diagnosis; a person who has forgotten everything; two people in a relationship where one person knows something the other doesn't. The play does not have recurring characters or a narrative; it is a formal and philosophical examination of what information and love are and how they interact." },
      { q: "What should I read after Love and Information by Caryl Churchill?", a: "After Love and Information, read The Shallows by Nicholas Carr (the nonfiction companion to what information abundance is doing to human cognition and connection), The Information by Martin Amis (the fiction companion to what information does to relationships), A Number by Caryl Churchill (the companion in Churchill's career — identity and what makes a person who they are), Far Away by Churchill (the companion in Churchill's career — moral frameworks and what becomes of them), and Never Let Me Go by Kazuo Ishiguro (the fiction companion to what love means and what memory means). Oryx and Crake by Margaret Atwood is the fiction companion to what happens to humanity in a world shaped by information and technology." },
      { q: "What does Love and Information say about knowledge?", a: "Love and Information is interested in the relationship between what we know and what we are — between information and identity, information and intimacy, information and power. Many of the scenes examine situations in which one person has information the other doesn't, or in which information changes the nature of a relationship, or in which the absence or presence of information determines what is possible between people. The play is also interested in what it means to live in a world where information is abundant — where we have access to more knowledge than we can absorb, where facts accumulate without necessarily producing understanding, and where connection between people is increasingly mediated by information technologies. Love and Information asks whether love and information are in tension with each other, whether too much information makes intimacy harder, and whether the desire for connection and the desire for knowledge point in the same direction or different ones." },
      { q: "How should Love and Information be performed?", a: "Churchill's published text of Love and Information includes notes on how the play should be performed. The fifty-seven scenes can be performed in any order within each section, except for the final scenes of each section which are fixed in place. Different productions have made different choices about this flexibility, and the play has been performed with varying running times and in varying configurations. The Royal Court's original production used a large cast of actors, each of whom appeared in multiple scenes, and the production emphasized the rapid movement between scenes and the variety of tones — some scenes are funny, some are tragic, some are philosophical, and they sit side by side without explanation or transition. The play asks that no scene be cut; the accumulation of the whole is what creates the total effect." },
      { q: "What is the relationship between love and information in Churchill's play?", a: "Love and Information does not offer a simple answer to the question implied by its title. What it suggests, through the accumulation of fifty-seven scenes, is that love and information are entangled in ways that are neither simply harmonious nor simply opposed. Information can be a form of love — knowing someone deeply is a form of caring about them. Information can also be a form of power over someone — knowing something they don't know, or withholding information. Love can require information — 'I love you' is an act of revealing information. Love can also require not knowing — some things about a beloved are better unknown. The play holds these entanglements in view without resolving them, and the accumulation of scenes creates a kind of experience of what it is like to live in a world where love and information are constantly interacting." },
    ],
