    faq: [
      { q: "What is Mojo by Jez Butterworth?", a: "Mojo (1995) is set in a Soho nightclub called the Atlantic Club on two nights in the summer of 1958. The club's owner, Baby, has discovered and is managing a teenage rock-and-roll singer called Silver Johnny. Various criminal interests — including a man called Sam Ross — want to control Silver Johnny's contract and his potential earnings. On the first night, Baby is murdered. The rest of the play follows the people left in the club — Mickey, Skinny, Potts, Sweets, and Baby's son Ezra — as they try to figure out what happened, who is coming for Silver Johnny, and what they are going to do. The play is simultaneously menacing, funny, and saturated in the language and music of early rock and roll." },
      { q: "What should I read after Mojo by Jez Butterworth?", a: "After Mojo, read Absolute Beginners by Colin MacInnes (the direct fiction companion to Soho and British youth culture in 1958), The Birthday Party by Harold Pinter (the theatrical companion — menace and comedy in the same year Mojo is set), Jerusalem by Jez Butterworth (the companion in Butterworth's career — Johnny Byron and English myth and violence), The Ferryman by Jez Butterworth (the most recent major work in Butterworth's career), and The Long Goodbye by Raymond Chandler (the crime fiction companion to a murder and what it reveals about the world around it). Wiseguy by Nicholas Pileggi is the nonfiction companion to what organized crime actually looks like from inside." },
      { q: "What is the significance of the Soho setting in Mojo?", a: "Soho in the late 1950s was one of the most culturally significant neighborhoods in London — a center for jazz clubs, coffee bars, music venues, and a nascent rock-and-roll scene, as well as for criminal activity, sex work, and the kinds of underground economies that existed in postwar London. Colin MacInnes's 1959 novel Absolute Beginners captures this world in fiction, and Mojo is set in the same Soho. The setting allows Butterworth to examine the moment when British youth culture — which would produce the Beatles and the Rolling Stones within five years — was just forming, and when the entertainment and criminal worlds that surrounded it overlapped in ways that are central to Mojo's plot." },
      { q: "How did Mojo establish Jez Butterworth's career?", a: "Mojo received its world premiere at the Royal Court Theatre in London in 1995 and was an immediate critical success. It won the George Devine Award, the Evening Standard Award for Most Promising Playwright, and the Laurence Olivier Award for Best Comedy. It established Butterworth as a significant new voice in British theater — one who could combine menace and comedy in the manner of Harold Pinter and Joe Orton while creating work that was specifically rooted in English and British cultural history. The play was adapted into a film in 1997. Butterworth's subsequent plays — The Night Heron, The Winterling, Jerusalem, The River, The Ferryman — have confirmed his status as one of the major British playwrights of his generation." },
      { q: "What is the influence of Harold Pinter on Mojo?", a: "Harold Pinter's influence on Mojo is evident in several ways: the use of menace, comedy, and the withholding of information as dramatic tools; the way in which the characters' inability to fully communicate generates both humor and danger; the sense that something terrible has happened or is about to happen that the characters are circling around without being able to face directly; and the specific use of language — repetition, non-sequitur, and the gap between what characters say and what they mean. Butterworth has acknowledged Pinter as an influence, and Mojo is sometimes discussed as a work that both draws on and departs from Pinter's theatrical world: Butterworth is more interested in a specific historical moment and cultural setting than Pinter typically is, and his comedy is brasher and less austere, but the underlying theatrical vocabulary is recognizably Pinteresque." },
    ],
