    faq: [
      { q: "What is Milk Like Sugar by Kirsten Greenidge?", a: "Milk Like Sugar (2011) follows Annie Desmond, a teenager in a de-industrialized New England city, as she navigates pressure from her two best friends — Margie and Talisha — who are planning a pregnancy pact: the three of them will get pregnant at the same time and raise their babies together. The play is set against the backdrop of what Annie's world offers her: a mother who had her young, a community where economic opportunity is limited, and a friend group for whom a baby is the most imaginable form of hope and purpose. Annie also has a relationship with Malik, and the play follows what she does with the pressure she faces — from her friends, from her mother, and from herself — and what it means to want something different from what your world is offering." },
      { q: "What should I read after Milk Like Sugar by Kirsten Greenidge?", a: "After Milk Like Sugar, read Salvage the Bones by Jesmyn Ward (the fiction companion to a teenage girl in a poor Black community who discovers she is pregnant), The Bluest Eye by Toni Morrison (the fiction companion to a Black girl who has been taught to want what the world tells her to want), Random Family by Adrian Nicole LeBlanc (the nonfiction companion to young women in an underserved community and what their lives actually look like), Their Eyes Were Watching God by Zora Neale Hurston (the fiction companion to a Black woman's desire for a life of her own choosing), and Speak by Laurie Halse Anderson (the YA companion to what teenage girls cannot say). Dreamland by Sam Quinones is the nonfiction companion to de-industrialized communities and what fills the vacuum." },
      { q: "What is a pregnancy pact and what does it mean in the context of the play?", a: "A pregnancy pact is an agreement among a group of young women to get pregnant at the same time and raise their children together. Such pacts gained media attention in 2008 when reports emerged of a purported pregnancy pact at Gloucester High School in Massachusetts (though the details were disputed). Whether or not the Gloucester pact was real, Greenidge uses the premise to examine what drives young women in communities with limited options to make choices that the mainstream might view as self-defeating but that the women themselves might view as forms of hope, connection, and purpose. In Milk Like Sugar, the pact is not simply a mistake or a tragedy — it is an expression of what Annie's world makes available as a form of meaning, and the play takes seriously what that world has and has not offered these girls." },
      { q: "Who is Kirsten Greenidge?", a: "Kirsten Greenidge is an American playwright and educator. Her major works include Milk Like Sugar (2011) and Our Lady of Kibeho (2014). She has been a professor in the Department of Theatre and Dance at Boston University. Her work is known for examining the lives of Black women and girls — particularly in New England settings — with specificity, compassion, and without sentimentality. Milk Like Sugar received its world premiere at the Yale Repertory Theatre before its Off-Broadway run at Playwrights Horizons in New York." },
      { q: "What does the title Milk Like Sugar mean?", a: "The title Milk Like Sugar is drawn from what milk tastes like to a baby — sweet, satisfying, the thing the baby most wants — and it functions in the play as a metaphor for what Annie and her friends are being given instead of what they need. The world that Annie inhabits offers her things that taste like what she wants — friendship, love, a future — but that are substitutes, things made to taste like the real thing without actually being it. The title evokes the way that desire and deprivation can be confused with each other, and the way that communities that are systematically denied resources develop forms of hope and connection that look, from outside, like poor choices but are actually expressions of real need." },
    ],
