    slug: 'aleshea-harris-what-to-send-up-when-it-goes-down',
    intro: "What to Send Up When It Goes Down (2018) by Aleshea Harris is a ritual performance piece explicitly written for and about Black audiences — it begins by asking white audience members to acknowledge that this piece is not for them; then, through ritual, communal mourning, spoken word, and theatrical confrontation, it examines the ongoing violence against Black people in America and what it takes to survive it.",
    startWith: "What to Send Up When It Goes Down",
    books: [
      { title: "What to Send Up When It Goes Down", author: "Aleshea Harris", year: 2018, note: "Start here — the ritual performance piece about anti-Black violence and what it costs to survive it; the Moving Arts Theatre and Playwrights Horizons production as one of the most formally radical examinations of anti-Black violence in American theater" },
      { title: "Is God Is", author: "Aleshea Harris", year: 2016, note: "The essential companion — the predecessor in Harris's career; twins who have survived fire and the revenge they enact as the companion in Harris's examination of violence and survival" },
      { title: "Between the World and Me", author: "Ta-Nehisi Coates", year: 2015, note: "The essential companion in nonfiction — Coates's examination of what Black bodies mean in America as the companion to What to Send Up When It Goes Down's examination of the same" },
      { title: "The Fire This Time", author: "Jesmyn Ward", year: 2016, note: "The essential companion in nonfiction — the essay collection edited by Ward examining race in America as the companion to What to Send Up When It Goes Down" },
      { title: "Heavy", author: "Kiese Laymon", year: 2018, note: "The essential companion in memoir — Laymon's examination of what it costs to survive as a Black man in America as the companion to What to Send Up When It Goes Down's examination of the same" },
      { title: "Citizen: An American Lyric", author: "Claudia Rankine", year: 2014, note: "The essential companion in poetry — Rankine's examination of the experience of being Black in America as the companion to What to Send Up When It Goes Down's examination of the same in theatrical form" },
      { title: "The New Jim Crow", author: "Michelle Alexander", year: 2010, note: "The essential companion in nonfiction — Alexander's examination of mass incarceration and race as the companion to What to Send Up When It Goes Down's examination of the systems that produce anti-Black violence" },
      { title: "Homegoing", author: "Yaa Gyasi", year: 2016, note: "The essential companion in fiction — Gyasi's examination of the long history of anti-Black violence from slavery to the present as the fiction companion to What to Send Up When It Goes Down" },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: "What is What to Send Up When It Goes Down by Aleshea Harris?", a: "What to Send Up When It Goes Down (2018) is a theatrical ritual piece written explicitly for Black audiences. Before the performance begins, the piece addresses white audience members directly, asking them to acknowledge that what they are about to see is not for them — that they are guests in a space that is being held for Black people. The piece then moves through ritual, communal mourning, spoken word, direct address, and theatrical confrontation to examine the ongoing violence against Black people in America — the regular killings of Black men and women by police and civilians, the way this violence is covered, and what it does to the people who must live inside it. It is not a documentary; it is a ritual, and its form reflects its content." },
      { q: "What should I read after What to Send Up When It Goes Down?", a: "After What to Send Up When It Goes Down, read Between the World and Me by Ta-Nehisi Coates (the nonfiction companion to what Black bodies mean in America), Citizen: An American Lyric by Claudia Rankine (the poetry companion to the experience of being Black in America), Heavy by Kiese Laymon (the memoir companion to what it costs to survive as a Black man in America), The New Jim Crow by Michelle Alexander (the nonfiction companion to mass incarceration and race), and The Fire This Time edited by Jesmyn Ward (the essay collection companion to race in America). Is God Is by Aleshea Harris is the predecessor in Harris's career — the companion to Harris's examination of violence and survival." },
      { q: "What does it mean for a play to be written 'for Black audiences'?", a: "What to Send Up When It Goes Down is explicit that it is written for and about Black audiences — that the Black people in the audience are not witnesses but participants, the subjects of the ritual rather than observers of it. This is a formal and political choice. Most American theater has historically been produced by and for white audiences, with Black characters and Black experiences as content observed by white viewers. Harris's piece refuses this structure. White audience members are not excluded but they are repositioned: they are guests in a space being held for someone else, and they are asked to acknowledge that explicitly before the piece begins. This is part of what makes the piece formally radical — it changes the terms on which the audience encounters the work." },
      { q: "Who is Aleshea Harris?", a: "Aleshea Harris is an American playwright based in Los Angeles. Her major works include Is God Is (2016) and What to Send Up When It Goes Down (2018). Is God Is is a play about twin sisters who survive a catastrophic fire that their father set, then seek him out for revenge — it draws on Greek tragedy, the Western genre, and African American vernacular traditions. Both of Harris's major plays are formally inventive — they do not follow conventional dramatic structures but draw on ritual, music, oral tradition, and direct address. Her work has been produced at theaters including the Soho Rep and Playwrights Horizons in New York, and she has received significant recognition as one of the most original voices in contemporary American theater." },
      { q: "What theatrical form does What to Send Up When It Goes Down use?", a: "What to Send Up When It Goes Down is a ritual piece — it draws on traditions of communal ritual, mourning ceremony, and participatory performance rather than on the conventions of realistic drama. It includes spoken word, communal response, direct address, and movement, and it explicitly breaks down the fourth wall that separates audience from performance. The piece is structured as a ritual that the audience participates in rather than a story that the audience observes. This formal choice is connected to its content: the piece argues that anti-Black violence in America is not a story to be told but a trauma to be processed collectively, and that the theatrical form most adequate to this subject is not narrative drama but communal ritual." },
    ],
