    startWith: "Bad Dates",
    books: [
      { title: "Bad Dates", author: "Theresa Rebeck", year: 2003, note: "Start here — Haley Walker preparing for dates while telling the audience who she is, what she's hiding, and what a woman alone in New York is actually looking for; the Playwrights Horizons production as one of Rebeck's most beloved and widely produced plays" },
      { title: "Seminar", author: "Theresa Rebeck", year: 2011, note: "The essential companion — the companion in Rebeck's career; Leonard and the four young writers as the examination of ambition and what we will do for approval" },
      { title: "Fleabag", author: "Phoebe Waller-Bridge", year: 2013, note: "The essential companion in drama — Waller-Bridge's examination of a young woman who talks to the audience about her life while hiding the truth from herself as the companion to Bad Dates" },
      { title: "Bridget Jones's Diary", author: "Helen Fielding", year: 1996, note: "The essential companion in fiction — Fielding's examination of a woman dating in London and what she tells herself about what she wants as the prose comedy companion to Bad Dates" },
      { title: "The Commitments", author: "Roddy Doyle", year: 1987, note: "The essential companion in fiction — the ensemble comedy companion to Bad Dates' examination of working-class identity and what people do when they want more" },
      { title: "Shoe Dog", author: "Phil Knight", year: 2016, note: "The essential companion in nonfiction — Knight's examination of building a shoe business as the unexpected companion to Bad Dates' running motif of shoes as a system for understanding who a person is" },
      { title: "I Feel Bad About My Neck", author: "Nora Ephron", year: 2006, note: "The essential companion in nonfiction — Ephron's examination of what a woman's life in New York actually looks like as the comedy essay companion to Bad Dates" },
      { title: "The Lonely City", author: "Olivia Laing", year: 2016, note: "The essential companion in nonfiction — Laing's examination of what loneliness looks like in a city full of people as the companion to Bad Dates' examination of what Haley is actually looking for under the comedy" },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: "What is Bad Dates by Theresa Rebeck?", a: "Bad Dates (2003) follows Haley Walker, a single mother who is the manager of a somewhat shady New York City restaurant, as she prepares for a series of dates. The play is structured as a monologue: Haley addresses the audience directly, getting ready for each date while telling us about the date she is about to go on, the dates she has already been on, and — gradually — the truth about who she is, where she came from, and why she moved to New York. A running motif through the play is shoes: Haley is obsessed with shoes, owns hundreds of pairs, and describes her life in part through which shoes she is wearing and what that means. The play is famous for its warmth, its comedy, and for Rebeck's ability to make a one-person monologue feel like a conversation." },
      { q: "What should I read after Bad Dates by Theresa Rebeck?", a: "After Bad Dates, read Seminar (the companion in Rebeck's career — Leonard and the four young writers), I Feel Bad About My Neck by Nora Ephron (the comedy essay companion to what a woman's life in New York actually looks like), Bridget Jones's Diary by Helen Fielding (the prose comedy companion to a woman dating and what she tells herself about what she wants), Fleabag by Phoebe Waller-Bridge (the companion in drama — a woman who talks to the audience while hiding the truth from herself), and The Lonely City by Olivia Laing (the companion to what loneliness actually looks like under the comedy). Shoe Dog by Phil Knight is the unexpected companion to the play's motif of shoes as a system for understanding a person." },
      { q: "Who is Theresa Rebeck and what is her signature style?", a: "Theresa Rebeck (born 1958) is one of the most produced American playwrights of her generation. Her signature style combines sharp wit, precise observation of professional and social environments, and an ability to write female characters who are intelligent, funny, complicated, and unapologetic. Her major theatrical works include Bad Dates (2003), Omnium Gatherum (2003), Seminar (2011), Dead Accounts (2012), and Seared (2018). She has also written extensively for television, including NYPD Blue and creating Smash (NBC, 2012–2013). A consistent theme across her work is what women — in professional, romantic, and social contexts — have to do to be taken seriously, and what it costs them." },
      { q: "What is the significance of shoes in Bad Dates?", a: "Shoes in Bad Dates function as a system of meaning that Haley has developed to understand herself and other people. Each pair of shoes Haley considers or wears in the play tells the audience something about who she is, what she wants, and what she is afraid of. The motif also serves as a structural device: as Haley selects shoes for each date, the selection becomes an occasion to reveal something about herself that she might not say directly. The shoes motif also connects to Haley's past — what it meant to grow up without money, what it means to have money now, and what she is trying to buy when she buys shoes. Rebeck has described the shoe motif as a way into Haley's character that is simultaneously comic and emotionally revealing." },
      { q: "What restaurant does Haley run in Bad Dates?", a: "In Bad Dates, Haley Walker runs a restaurant in New York City that has a somewhat shady background — the original owner was connected to the Russian mob, and some of the establishment's operations are ethically ambiguous. Haley is the manager rather than the owner, and her position there is itself part of the play's backstory: how she came to be working at this restaurant, what her relationship to the establishment's murky past is, and what she is trying to do about it. The restaurant setting establishes Haley as a woman with practical knowledge, financial pressure, and a life that is more complicated than the comedy of her dating monologue initially suggests." },
    ],
