    faq: [
      { q: "What is What We're Up Against by Theresa Rebeck?", a: "What We're Up Against (2011) follows Eliza, a talented young architect who has just joined a small architecture firm. She is skilled and ambitious, and she has work to show for it. But when she shows her designs to the partner who hired her, she finds that a male colleague — Ben — has taken credit for them. The play follows what happens as Eliza tries to navigate this situation: the people who believe her, the people who don't, the people who believe her but won't say so, and what the institutional logic of the firm does to her complaint. The play is a precise and angry examination of how workplace sexism actually operates — not as overt discrimination but as a system of small advantages, credibility gaps, and institutional inertia that favors men." },
      { q: "What should I read after What We're Up Against by Theresa Rebeck?", a: "After What We're Up Against, read Invisible Women by Caroline Criado Perez (the nonfiction companion to how systems are designed by men for men and what women experience as a result), Lean In by Sheryl Sandberg (the nonfiction companion to what women face in professional environments), Seminar by Theresa Rebeck (the companion in Rebeck's career — power and what people do to get what they want), When Women Lead by Julia Boorstin (the nonfiction companion to what women with power do differently), and Know My Name by Chanel Miller (the memoir companion to what institutions do when a woman makes a complaint against a more powerful figure). The Devil Wears Prada is the fiction companion to what a young woman in a professional environment learns and loses." },
      { q: "How does What We're Up Against portray workplace sexism?", a: "Rebeck's play is specific and careful in how it portrays workplace sexism. The sexism in the play is not overt or cartoonish — no character admits to discriminating against Eliza on the basis of her gender. Instead, the play examines what might be called the plausibility architecture of workplace sexism: how it is always possible to explain each individual incident in a way that does not invoke gender, how the people who benefit from the system are invested in believing that the system is fair, and how the people who are harmed by the system face a double bind — if they complain, they are seen as difficult; if they don't complain, the behavior continues. The play is specifically about the credibility gap: what it takes for a woman's professional claims to be believed, and how that requirement is itself a form of discrimination." },
      { q: "What is the Women's Project Theater?", a: "The Women's Project Theater (now known as WP Theater) is a New York City-based theater company dedicated to developing and producing plays and musicals created by women. It was founded in 1978 by Julia Miles and is the longest-running professional theater organization in the United States dedicated to women artists. What We're Up Against received its New York production at Women's Project Theater, which was an appropriate home for a play about what women in male-dominated professional environments actually experience. WP Theater has produced works by numerous significant American playwrights and is an important institutional support for women's voices in the American theater." },
      { q: "Who are the other characters in What We're Up Against besides Eliza?", a: "The play's other major characters include Ben, the male colleague who takes credit for Eliza's designs; Stu and Theresa, partners at the firm; Janice, a woman at the firm who has found a way to function within the system but has made certain accommodations to do so; and Walter, a senior figure at the firm. The play uses this ensemble to examine different responses to the situation Eliza faces: Ben's entitlement (he barely seems aware that he has done something wrong), the partners' investment in believing the system is fair, Janice's complicated position as a woman who has survived in the firm by not making waves, and what each character's response reveals about how workplace sexism is maintained by a system rather than by any single bad actor." },
    ],
