    slug: 'theresa-rebeck-dead-accounts',
    intro: "Dead Accounts (2012) by Theresa Rebeck follows Jack, who has suddenly returned from a successful career in New York to his family home in Ohio — and as his wife, mother, and sister each demand to know why he's really back, the play examines what people run toward when they run away from the life they built, and what home actually means when you've left it.",
    startWith: "Dead Accounts",
    books: [
      { title: "Dead Accounts", author: "Theresa Rebeck", year: 2012, note: "Start here — Jack returning to Ohio from New York and the family who wants to know the real reason as the examination of what home means when you've left it and what you are actually looking for when you go back; the Broadway production at the Music Box Theatre as Rebeck's most recent Broadway play before Seared" },
      { title: "Seminar", author: "Theresa Rebeck", year: 2011, note: "The essential companion — the predecessor in Rebeck's career; what people will do for ambition and what they will do when it fails as the companion to Dead Accounts" },
      { title: "August: Osage County", author: "Tracy Letts", year: 2007, note: "The essential companion in drama — Letts' examination of a family gathered in a family home and what each person reveals about themselves as the companion to Dead Accounts" },
      { title: "Olive Kitteridge", author: "Elizabeth Strout", year: 2008, note: "The essential companion in fiction — Strout's examination of what it means to stay in the place you are from and what that life looks like from the outside as the companion to Dead Accounts' examination of what it means to leave and come back" },
      { title: "A Thousand Acres", author: "Jane Smiley", year: 1991, note: "The essential companion in fiction — Smiley's examination of what happens when a family secret surfaces when the family is gathered together as the prose companion to Dead Accounts" },
      { title: "Revolutionary Road", author: "Richard Yates", year: 1961, note: "The essential companion in fiction — Yates' examination of what people who left where they came from find when they get where they were going as the prose companion to Dead Accounts' examination of the same" },
      { title: "What I Talk About When I Talk About Running", author: "Haruki Murakami", year: 2007, note: "The essential companion in nonfiction — the examination of what a person does when the life they've built no longer feels like enough as the companion to Dead Accounts' examination of the same" },
      { title: "Rabbit, Run", author: "John Updike", year: 1960, note: "The essential companion in fiction — Updike's examination of a man who cannot stay in the life he has built as the prose companion to Dead Accounts' examination of what Jack is running toward when he goes home" },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: "What is Dead Accounts by Theresa Rebeck?", a: "Dead Accounts (2012) opens with Jack's unexpected return to his family's home in Cincinnati, Ohio. Jack has had a successful career in New York — apparently in finance — and his wife Jenny, who has stayed in Ohio, is glad to have him back and assumes he has returned permanently. His sister Lorna, who still lives with their mother, is suspicious of the real reason. Their mother is glad to see him. And as the play unfolds, the truth about why Jack has left New York becomes clear — with significant implications for everyone. The play examines what home means to someone who has left it, what people do when the life they built fails them, and what family is when it is simultaneously the place you escaped from and the only place that still knows who you are." },
      { q: "What should I read after Dead Accounts by Theresa Rebeck?", a: "After Dead Accounts, read August: Osage County by Tracy Letts (the dramatic companion to a family gathered in a home and what each person reveals), Olive Kitteridge by Elizabeth Strout (the fiction companion to what it means to stay in the place you are from), Revolutionary Road by Richard Yates (the fiction companion to what people who left where they came from find when they get where they were going), A Thousand Acres by Jane Smiley (the fiction companion to what happens when a family secret surfaces), and Rabbit, Run by John Updike (the fiction companion to a man who cannot stay in the life he has built). Seminar by Rebeck is the companion in Rebeck's career." },
      { q: "What is the significance of the Ohio setting in Dead Accounts?", a: "In American drama and fiction, the Midwest — and Ohio specifically — has a specific cultural geography: it is the place that people leave to pursue success in New York or Los Angeles, and the place they return to when something in the pursued life has failed or worn out. Rebeck uses this geography deliberately in Dead Accounts. Jack has built the New York life that someone from Cincinnati is supposed to want, and his return to Ohio is the sign that something has gone wrong — not just professionally but in his understanding of what he was pursuing and why. The play is partly about what 'home' means in this geography: whether you can go back, whether it is a refuge or a confession of failure, and what you find there when you do return." },
      { q: "Who is in the original Broadway production of Dead Accounts?", a: "Dead Accounts had its Broadway premiere at the Music Box Theatre in December 2012. The cast included Norbert Leo Butz as Jack, Katie Holmes as Jenny, Josh Hamilton as Phil (Jack's friend), Jayne Houdyshell as Jack's mother, and Judy Greer as Lorna. The production was directed by Jack O'Brien. Norbert Leo Butz had previously won two Tony Awards for Best Actor in a Musical (Dirty Rotten Scoundrels and Catch Me If You Can). The production received mixed critical reviews and had a limited run." },
      { q: "What does Dead Accounts say about success and the American Dream?", a: "Dead Accounts examines a specific American story: the person who grew up somewhere ordinary, worked extremely hard to build a successful professional life in a prestigious city, and then found that the life they built did not contain what they thought it would. Jack's return to Ohio is partly a flight from professional consequences, but it is also a flight from a version of success that turned out to feel hollow. The play asks what happens when the American Dream — leave home, work hard, make money, build a life — is actually achieved and turns out not to be what you wanted. It is also about what the people who did not leave — Jenny, Lorna, their mother — understand that Jack does not, and whether going home is a defeat or a recognition." },
    ],
