    startWith: "Seared",
    books: [
      { title: "Seared", author: "Theresa Rebeck", year: 2018, note: "Start here — Harry and the scallop dish and whether the restaurant becomes a business or remains an art as the examination of creative integrity and what it costs to hold onto it; the MCC Theater and San Francisco production as Rebeck's most recent examination of what happens when art meets commerce" },
      { title: "Seminar", author: "Theresa Rebeck", year: 2011, note: "The essential companion — the companion in Rebeck's career; Leonard and the four young writers as the examination of what ambition and creative power look like when they are filtered through an institution" },
      { title: "Kitchen Confidential", author: "Anthony Bourdain", year: 2000, note: "The essential companion in nonfiction — Bourdain's examination of what professional restaurant kitchens actually look like as the nonfiction companion to Seared's examination of the same" },
      { title: "The Bear", author: "Christopher Storer", year: 2022, note: "The essential companion in television — Storer's examination of a high-end chef running a Chicago restaurant as the TV companion to Seared's examination of what creative intensity in a kitchen looks like" },
      { title: "Blood, Bones and Butter", author: "Gabrielle Hamilton", year: 2011, note: "The essential companion in memoir — Hamilton's examination of building and running a restaurant from the chef's point of view as the memoir companion to Seared" },
      { title: "Setting the Table", author: "Danny Meyer", year: 2006, note: "The essential companion in nonfiction — Meyer's examination of the hospitality business and what it means to scale a restaurant into a brand as the companion to what Mike wants for Harry's restaurant in Seared" },
      { title: "Sweetbitter", author: "Stephanie Danler", year: 2016, note: "The essential companion in fiction — Danler's examination of what the restaurant world looks like from the front of house as the fiction companion to Seared's examination of what it looks like from the kitchen" },
      { title: "Dirt", author: "Bill Buford", year: 2020, note: "The essential companion in nonfiction — Buford's examination of what it takes to learn to cook at the highest level as the nonfiction companion to Seared's examination of what being a chef at that level demands" },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: "What is Seared by Theresa Rebeck?", a: "Seared (2018) centers on Harry, a Brooklyn chef whose small restaurant has developed a cult following for its food. Harry is brilliant and obsessive — particularly about a scallop dish that has become the signature of the restaurant. His business partner Mike wants to grow the restaurant into something more financially stable and scalable. A restaurant investor group has offered money and guidance. And a new front-of-house manager named Jenna arrives with the skills to transform the dining room and the business side of the operation. The play turns on Harry's relationship to his scallop dish — a refusal to change it or license it that becomes the ground on which the play examines whether art can coexist with commerce, and what it costs a person to protect creative integrity when everyone around them wants them to compromise." },
      { q: "What should I read after Seared by Theresa Rebeck?", a: "After Seared, read Kitchen Confidential by Anthony Bourdain (the nonfiction companion to what professional restaurant kitchens actually look like), Blood, Bones and Butter by Gabrielle Hamilton (the memoir companion to building and running a restaurant from the chef's point of view), Setting the Table by Danny Meyer (the nonfiction companion to the hospitality business and what scaling a restaurant into a brand actually involves), Sweetbitter by Stephanie Danler (the fiction companion to what the restaurant world looks like from the front of house), and Dirt by Bill Buford (the nonfiction companion to what it takes to learn to cook at the highest level). Seminar by Rebeck is the companion in Rebeck's career — what it looks like when creative power meets institutional pressure." },
      { q: "What does the scallop dish represent in Seared?", a: "The scallop dish in Seared functions as a symbol for creative integrity and the question of what an artist owes to their art versus what they owe to the institution that supports their art. Harry's refusal to change the scallop dish — or to let it be licensed, or to let it become part of a brand — is both completely understandable (it is his best work, and changing it or commercializing it would change what it is) and completely maddening to everyone around him (the dish is what makes the restaurant work, and if it could be scaled or replicated, it would make everyone's lives easier). The play uses the dish to examine what creative integrity actually costs and what it asks of the people around the creator." },
      { q: "What is MCC Theater?", a: "MCC Theater (formerly Manhattan Class Company) is a New York City-based off-Broadway theater company founded in 1986 by Robert LuPone, Bernard Telsey, and William Cantler. It is known for developing and producing new American plays and for its commitment to emerging and established playwrights. Seared received its world premiere at MCC Theater in 2018, before subsequent productions including a run at San Francisco's American Conservatory Theater and, later, a Broadway run. MCC has also produced works by other significant American playwrights including Jonathan Larson (Tick, Tick... Boom!), Neil LaBute, and many others." },
      { q: "What does Seared say about the relationship between art and commerce in the restaurant world?", a: "Seared examines a tension that is specific to the restaurant world but illuminates a broader question about any creative practice: what happens when the thing an artist makes becomes valuable enough that other people want to commercialize it? Harry's restaurant exists because of his food, and his food exists because of his refusal to compromise. But the restaurant also cannot survive indefinitely as a small cult restaurant with an obsessive chef who won't scale. The play does not resolve this tension in a simple way — it shows what is genuinely at stake on both sides, what Harry loses if he compromises and what everyone loses if he doesn't, and what Jenna's position in the middle of this conflict reveals about what the restaurant actually needs that neither Harry nor Mike can see." },
    ],
