    slug: 'anna-ziegler-boy',
    intro: "Boy (2016) by Anna Ziegler is based on the real case of David Reimer — a Canadian boy who, after a botched circumcision as an infant in 1966, was raised as a girl by his parents on the advice of psychologist John Money; the play examines what gender identity is, what it means to be assigned an identity that is not your own, and what the adults responsible for a child's care do and do not understand.",
    startWith: "Boy",
    books: [
      { title: "Boy", author: "Anna Ziegler", year: 2016, note: "Start here — Adam/Samantha's story as the examination of what gender is, what it means to be assigned an identity that is not your own, and what the psychologist's theory does when it meets a human life; the Ensemble Studio Theatre production as the companion in Ziegler's career to Photograph 51" },
      { title: "Photograph 51", author: "Anna Ziegler", year: 2015, note: "The essential companion — the predecessor in Ziegler's career; Rosalind Franklin as the examination of what a woman scientist loses when experts take credit for her work as the companion in Ziegler's examination of institutional and intellectual authority over individual lives" },
      { title: "As Nature Made Him", author: "John Colapinto", year: 2000, note: "The essential companion in nonfiction — Colapinto's examination of the David Reimer case and what happened to him as the direct nonfiction companion to Boy" },
      { title: "Gender Queer", author: "Maia Kobabe", year: 2019, note: "The essential companion in graphic memoir — Kobabe's examination of navigating a gender identity that does not fit assigned categories as the companion to Boy's examination of the same" },
      { title: "Man Enough", author: "Justin Baldoni", year: 2021, note: "The essential companion in nonfiction — the examination of what masculine identity demands and costs as the companion to Boy's examination of Adam/Samantha's relationship to masculine identity" },
      { title: "The Feminine Mystique", author: "Betty Friedan", year: 1963, note: "The essential companion in nonfiction — Friedan's examination of what femininity as an imposed identity costs women as the historical companion to Boy's examination of what an imposed gender identity costs Adam/Samantha" },
      { title: "Middlesex", author: "Jeffrey Eugenides", year: 2002, note: "The essential companion in fiction — Eugenides's examination of a person who is intersex and navigating identity as the prose companion to Boy's examination of gender identity and what is assigned versus what is known" },
      { title: "Shuggie Bain", author: "Douglas Stuart", year: 2020, note: "The essential companion in fiction — Stuart's examination of a boy who does not fit the masculine identity expected of him as the prose companion to Boy's examination of gender and identity" },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: "What is Boy by Anna Ziegler?", a: "Boy (2016) is based on the real case of David Reimer, a Canadian boy who in 1966, as an infant, suffered a botched circumcision that damaged his penis beyond repair. On the advice of psychologist John Money, his parents decided to raise him as a girl — a decision supported by Money's theory that gender identity was entirely socially constructed and that a child raised as a girl would identify as a girl. The child, initially named Bruce and then Brenda after the surgery and reassignment, never identified as female. In the play, the character is called Adam/Samantha. The play follows the character's life from childhood through adulthood and examines what gender identity is, what it means to be assigned an identity that conflicts with your own sense of yourself, and what the professionals who advised the parents did and did not understand about human gender." },
      { q: "What should I read after Boy by Anna Ziegler?", a: "After Boy, read As Nature Made Him by John Colapinto (the direct nonfiction companion to the David Reimer case), Photograph 51 (the predecessor in Ziegler's career — Rosalind Franklin and institutional authority over individual lives), Middlesex by Jeffrey Eugenides (the fiction companion to a person who is intersex and navigating identity), Gender Queer by Maia Kobabe (the graphic memoir companion to navigating a gender identity that does not fit assigned categories), and The Feminine Mystique by Betty Friedan (the historical companion to what feminine identity as an imposed category costs). Shuggie Bain by Douglas Stuart is the fiction companion to a boy who does not fit the masculine identity expected of him." },
      { q: "Who was David Reimer and why does his case matter?", a: "David Reimer (1965–2004) was a Canadian man whose case became one of the most significant in the history of gender studies. As an infant in 1966, a botched circumcision destroyed his penis. On the advice of psychologist John Money, his parents decided to raise him as a girl, and he underwent surgery. Money's theory of gender — that gender identity was entirely a product of socialization and that a child raised in a given gender role would identify with that gender — was at the time influential. But Reimer never identified as female. After learning the truth about his history as a teenager, he began living as a male. He married and became a stepfather. John Colapinto's book As Nature Made Him (2000) told his story publicly. Reimer died by suicide in 2004. His case has been cited as evidence against the theory that gender identity is entirely socially constructed." },
      { q: "What is John Money's theory of gender and why is it important in Boy?", a: "John Money (1921–2006) was an American psychologist and sexologist at Johns Hopkins University who developed influential theories about gender and sexuality. His theory of gender — which he called the 'gender role' — held that gender identity was primarily a product of socialization rather than biology: that a child raised in a given gender role would identify with that gender, provided the socialization was consistent and begun early enough. This theory was the basis for the advice he gave to David Reimer's parents. Money publicized the Reimer case as a success story supporting his theory — claiming that the child had successfully adapted to female identity — when in fact the child was deeply unhappy and was not identifying as female. When the truth emerged, it significantly damaged Money's reputation and contributed to a rethinking of how gender identity formation was understood." },
      { q: "Who is Anna Ziegler?", a: "Anna Ziegler is an American playwright. Her major works include Photograph 51 (2015), Boy (2016), Actually (2017), The Last Match (2016), and Dina (2019). She is known for plays that examine science, medicine, gender, and moral complexity through intimate, character-driven dramas. Boy received its world premiere at Ensemble Studio Theatre in New York. Both Boy and Photograph 51 examine cases in which institutional or scientific authority was exercised over individuals in ways that suppressed or denied the individuals' own experience of themselves — a theme that runs through much of Ziegler's work." },
    ],
