    faq: [
      { q: "What is Paradise Blue by Dominique Morisseau?", a: "Paradise Blue (2015) is the second play in Dominique Morisseau's Detroit Project. It is set in 1949 in Paradise Valley, a historically Black neighborhood in Detroit that was home to a thriving jazz and entertainment culture. Blue, a jazz trumpet player, owns a jazz club that has been the center of the community. The city of Detroit is planning to demolish Paradise Valley as part of urban renewal — the mid-century program of clearing 'blighted' neighborhoods that disproportionately targeted Black communities and often destroyed the very cultural and economic life of those communities. Blue is considering selling the club and leaving. Silver, a mysterious woman, arrives in Paradise Valley seeking lodging. The play examines what the neighborhood means, what Blue is carrying inside that is tearing him apart, and what Silver has come looking for." },
      { q: "What should I read after Paradise Blue by Dominique Morisseau?", a: "After Paradise Blue, read Detroit '67 (the first play in the Detroit Project — what Detroit became in the decades after Paradise Valley was demolished), Skeleton Crew (the third play in the Detroit Project — the auto plant as the Great Recession begins), Jazz by Toni Morrison (the fiction companion to a Black neighborhood and jazz music in the same era), The Warmth of Other Suns by Isabel Wilkerson (the nonfiction companion to the Great Migration that built Detroit), and Invisible Man by Ralph Ellison (the fiction companion to a Black man's inner life in the same historical period). A Raisin in the Sun by Lorraine Hansberry is the dramatic companion to a Black family and their dreams in the same historical moment." },
      { q: "What was Paradise Valley in Detroit?", a: "Paradise Valley was a historically Black neighborhood in Detroit, Michigan, that flourished from the 1920s through the late 1950s. It was the center of Black commercial, cultural, and entertainment life in Detroit — a neighborhood of jazz clubs, theaters, restaurants, barbershops, and businesses owned by and serving the Black community. The neighborhood emerged as a result of housing segregation: Black Detroiters, many of whom had migrated from the South during the Great Migration, were excluded from most of the city by discriminatory housing covenants and were concentrated in a specific area of the city. Within those constraints, the community built a thriving neighborhood. In the 1950s and 1960s, the city of Detroit used urban renewal programs to demolish Paradise Valley and its neighboring Hastings Street corridor, displacing thousands of residents and destroying one of the most vibrant Black cultural communities in the North." },
      { q: "What is urban renewal and how does it affect Paradise Blue?", a: "Urban renewal was a mid-twentieth-century federal and local government program that used public funds to demolish and redevelop urban neighborhoods deemed 'blighted.' In practice, urban renewal projects disproportionately targeted Black neighborhoods and communities of color — using the language of blight and improvement to justify the destruction of communities that were economically thriving by their own internal measures, and to clear land for highways, stadiums, universities, and developments that served white and wealthier populations. Paradise Valley, the Detroit neighborhood where Morisseau's play is set, was demolished in the 1950s and 1960s as part of urban renewal — the neighborhood was cleared for the Chrysler Freeway and other developments. The play's 1949 setting places it at the moment when the neighborhood is still intact but the threat of demolition is already present." },
      { q: "What role does jazz play in Paradise Blue?", a: "Jazz is central to Paradise Blue both as a setting element and as a thematic and structural device. Blue is a jazz trumpet player, and his relationship to jazz — what it allows him to express, what it demands of him, what it costs him — is central to his character and to the play's examination of what he is carrying inside. Morisseau draws on the tradition of jazz as a carrier of African American experience — the way jazz music has been understood as a form that contains and expresses things that cannot be said in words, including pain, loss, violence, and the aspiration toward freedom. Paradise Valley was historically a center of jazz in Detroit, and the play uses the jazz club as a setting that represents everything the neighborhood contains and what will be lost when urban renewal destroys it." },
    ],
