    faq: [
      { q: "What is Choir Boy by Tarell Alvin McCraney?", a: "Choir Boy (2013) is set at the Charles R. Drew Prep School for Boys — a fictional elite school for Black young men that prides itself on producing leaders and on a tradition of gospel and spiritual music. Pharus Jonathan Young is a student whose extraordinary singing voice has made him the lead of the school choir. He is also gay, which makes him a target for the bullying of Bobby, the nephew of the school's headmaster. When Pharus is taunted during a school assembly, the headmaster — who witnessed the exchange — must decide what to do. The play examines the school's investments: in Black excellence, in Christian faith, in the performance of masculinity, and in what happens when these values are used to police a young man who embodies Black excellence in a form the school cannot accommodate." },
      { q: "What should I read after Choir Boy by Tarell Alvin McCraney?", a: "After Choir Boy, read The Brothers Size (the predecessor in McCraney's career), Giovanni's Room by James Baldwin (the fiction companion to a man who must find a way to survive in a world that cannot accommodate what he is), Moonlight by McCraney (the original play that became the Academy Award-winning film — the examination of a young Black gay man growing up), Head of Passes (the companion play in McCraney's career), and The Price of the Ticket by Baldwin (the nonfiction companion to what it costs a Black gay man to be who he is). The Beautiful Room Is Empty by Edmund White is the prose companion to a young gay man's coming of age and what it costs." },
      { q: "What is the Broadway production of Choir Boy?", a: "Choir Boy had its world premiere at the Bush Theatre in London in 2012 and its American premiere at Manhattan Theatre Club in New York in 2013. The play then transferred to Broadway at the Samuel J. Friedman Theatre in 2019, directed by Trip Cullman, with choreography by Raja Feather Kelly. The Broadway production starred Jeremy Pope as Pharus and received Tony Award nominations including Best Featured Actor in a Play for Kyle Beltran. The production was praised for its use of live a cappella music — the choir sings throughout the play, and the music is central to the drama — and for the performances, particularly Pope's portrayal of Pharus." },
      { q: "How does faith function in Choir Boy?", a: "Faith — specifically Black Christian faith in the tradition of gospel and spiritual music — is central to Choir Boy. The Drew Prep School is built on a tradition of this faith, and the music that Pharus leads is gospel music: music that expresses and carries this tradition. But the play examines the contradiction at the heart of this faith as it is practiced at Drew: the same gospel tradition that affirms the dignity and worth of Black people is used to condemn Pharus for his sexuality. The play asks what a young Black man who loves God and loves gospel music does when the institution built on that love uses it to harm him. Pharus doesn't reject faith; he refuses to let faith be used as a weapon against him, while still believing in the music's power." },
      { q: "What is Tarell Alvin McCraney's film Moonlight based on?", a: "The 2016 film Moonlight, directed by Barry Jenkins, is based on McCraney's play In Moonlight Black Boys Look Blue (2003), which McCraney wrote while a student at the Theatre School at DePaul University. The play examines a young Black man — Chiron — at three stages of his life: as a boy, as a teenager, and as a young adult, and traces his relationship with masculinity, his mother's drug addiction, his sexuality, and the neighborhood in Miami where he grew up. The film won the Academy Award for Best Picture, and McCraney and Jenkins shared the Academy Award for Best Adapted Screenplay. McCraney has described the play as autobiographical — he grew up in Miami's Liberty City neighborhood, and his mother struggled with drug addiction." },
    ],
