    faq: [
      { q: "What is John by Annie Baker about?", a: "John (2015) is a play set in a bed-and-breakfast in Gettysburg, Pennsylvania, at Thanksgiving. Elias and Jenny, a couple in crisis, have come to the historic town to see the battlefield. They stay at a B&B run by Mertis, an elderly woman who lives with an enormous collection of dolls — some of which she attributes to a woman named Jenny who once lived in the house and whose presence Mertis seems to feel still. The play spans four days. Elias and Jenny fight, connect, and fail to connect. Mertis tends her dolls and tells her stories. A blind woman named Genevieve visits. The past of the town — the thousands of dead — hovers over everything. The play is Baker's most structurally and thematically ambitious: it is three hours long, it is a ghost story, and it examines what happens when the weight of historical violence presses on ordinary intimate relationships." },
      { q: "What should I read after John by Annie Baker?", a: "After John, read The Flick (the Pulitzer Prize-winning companion in Baker's career — the movie theater workers), Circle Mirror Transformation (the predecessor in Baker's career — the Vermont drama class), The Haunting of Hill House by Shirley Jackson (the prose companion to what a house does to the people inside it), The Turn of the Screw by Henry James (the prose predecessor — the ghost story that may or may not be real), and Fefu and Her Friends by María Irene Fornés (the dramatic predecessor — women in a house, ordinary conversation, and what haunts the space). Gettysburg by Michael Shaara is the historical fiction companion to the battle whose aftermath haunts John's B&B." },
      { q: "Who wrote John and how does it relate to Annie Baker's other work?", a: "Annie Baker (born 1981) wrote John (2015) after The Flick (2013, Pulitzer Prize). Like all of Baker's work, John is interested in the gap between what people say and what they mean, in the silences that reveal more than speech, and in the way ordinary American spaces carry the weight of everything that has happened in them. Baker's plays are often very long and very slow — not in a way that feels tedious but in a way that feels like deep attention. John is the most explicitly supernatural of her plays; the others (Circle Mirror Transformation, Body Awareness, The Flick) use naturalistic means. But even in John, the ghost is ambiguous — the play can be read as entirely naturalistic, with Mertis as an eccentric old woman and the ghostly presences as her fantasies." },
      { q: "What is the significance of the setting of John in Gettysburg?", a: "Gettysburg is the site of one of the bloodiest battles of the American Civil War (July 1–3, 1863), in which approximately 51,000 soldiers were killed, wounded, or went missing. The town has been defined by this history for over 150 years: it is one of the most visited historical sites in the United States, and the landscape — the fields, the ridges, the town itself — is layered with the memory of the dead. Baker's choice of Gettysburg as the setting for John places the disintegrating relationship of Elias and Jenny in the shadow of mass death and historical violence. The play asks what it means to have an ordinary crisis — a relationship ending — in a place where tens of thousands of people died. Mertis's dolls, with their connections to women who have lived and died in the house, extend this meditation on the dead who are still with us into the domestic space of the B&B." },
      { q: "What is the production history of John by Annie Baker?", a: "John received its world premiere at the Signature Theatre in New York City in July 2015. The production was directed by Sam Gold, who directed the premiere productions of The Flick and Circle Mirror Transformation. The play starred Georgia Engel (Mertis), Hong Chau (Jenny), Christopher Abbott (Elias), and Laine Kazan (Genevieve). The production ran for approximately three and a half hours. The play was subsequently produced at other regional theaters and internationally. Like Baker's other work, it generated a debate about theatrical length and patience — whether audiences were willing to give a play the time it demanded — and was recognized as one of the most significant American plays of the decade." },
    ],
