    faq: [
      { q: "What is A 24-Decade History of Popular Music by Taylor Mac?", a: "A 24-Decade History of Popular Music (2016) is a 24-hour live performance work by the artist Taylor Mac. Mac performs 246 popular American songs from 1776 to the present, covering one decade per hour. The performance is not merely a concert: Mac, wearing elaborate costume creations by Machine Dazzle, performs the songs while telling stories, involving the audience, and examining the political and social history that each decade's music carries. The full 24-hour version was first performed at St. Ann's Warehouse in Brooklyn in October 2016. Shorter versions — 6-hour 'epochs' — are more commonly produced. The work was a finalist for the Pulitzer Prize for Drama." },
      { q: "What awards did A 24-Decade History of Popular Music win?", a: "A 24-Decade History of Popular Music was a finalist for the Pulitzer Prize for Drama in 2017. Taylor Mac received the MacArthur Foundation Fellowship ('genius grant') in 2017, in part for this work. The work received numerous other recognitions, including the Doris Duke Artist Award and the Kennedy Center's Gold Medallion. The six-hour epoch performances have been widely produced in theaters and performance spaces across the United States and internationally." },
      { q: "What should I read after A 24-Decade History of Popular Music by Taylor Mac?", a: "After the 24-Decade project, read Hir (Mac's full-length play as the companion in Mac's work), Stomping the Blues by Albert Murray (the nonfiction companion to popular music as the carrier of American experience), Can't Stop Won't Stop by Jeff Chang (the nonfiction companion to hip-hop as American political history), The Rest Is Noise by Alex Ross (the companion examination of music as the cultural history of the twentieth century), and How Music Works by David Byrne (the companion in nonfiction — how music is produced, distributed, and heard). Please Kill Me by McNeil and McCain is the oral history companion to the American counterculture that the 24-Decade project examines." },
      { q: "Who is Taylor Mac?", a: "Taylor Mac (born 1973) is an American performer, playwright, and director who uses the pronoun 'judy' (lowercase). Mac is best known for A 24-Decade History of Popular Music (2016, Pulitzer finalist, MacArthur Fellowship), Hir (2014, widely produced nationwide), Gary: A Sequel to Titus Andronicus (2019, starring Nathan Lane and Julie White on Broadway), and The Lily's Revenge (2009). Mac is known for elaborate, highly theatrical performance works that combine music, drag, community ritual, and political commentary. Mac works regularly with the costume designer Machine Dazzle, whose elaborate constructions are central to the visual world of Mac's performances." },
      { q: "What is Taylor Mac's play Hir about?", a: "Hir (2014) is a full-length play by Taylor Mac. Isaac, a Marine, returns from combat in Afghanistan to his family's home and finds his family transformed: his abusive father Max has suffered a stroke and is now managed by Isaac's mother Paige, who has kept Max in a semi-stuporous state; and Isaac's younger sibling Maxine has come out as transgender and now goes by Max. The play is set in a house of filth and chaos that Paige has deliberately created as a rejection of the domestic order her husband imposed on her for decades. Isaac, traumatized by combat and by his own family, is unable to find his place in this new world. Hir examines gender, family violence, domestic space, and what happens to men when the world they were trained for no longer exists." },
    ],
