    faq: [
      { q: "What is Little Women about?", a: "Little Women (1868) follows the four March sisters — Meg, Jo, Beth, and Amy — growing up in Concord, Massachusetts during the Civil War while their father is serving as a chaplain. The novel covers approximately a year in their lives: Meg, the eldest, is sensible and domestic; Jo, the second, is passionate and tomboyish, a writer who wants to be independent and who is the novel's emotional center; Beth, the third, is gentle, musical, and sickly; Amy, the youngest, is artistic and social. Their neighbor is Laurie Laurence, a boy their own age, who becomes Jo's closest friend. The novel follows the sisters through domestic adventures — putting on a play, going to a dance, dealing with poverty, managing household crises while their mother manages without their father — and builds toward the question of what grown-up life will look like for each of them. Its sequel, Good Wives (originally published as volume two in America), continues the story with the sisters as young women." },
      { q: "Why did Jo March refuse Laurie's proposal?", a: "Jo March refuses Laurie Laurence's marriage proposal in one of the most discussed decisions in American fiction. Laurie is charming, affectionate, and devoted to Jo; many readers feel the refusal is wrong and the subsequent marriage of Laurie to Amy — Jo's younger sister — is a betrayal. Alcott's own explanation, in letters and interviews, was that she never intended Jo to marry Laurie because she based Jo largely on herself and she did not want to marry. The novel's explanation is that Jo loves Laurie as a friend and brother but not as a husband, and that she knows herself well enough to know the marriage would not be happy — that two passionate, ambitious, strong-willed people would fight each other rather than complement each other. Jo's eventual marriage to the older, calmer Professor Bhaer was a concession to readers and publishers who demanded that Jo marry someone; Alcott said she would have preferred Jo to remain single and independent, which may be the truest expression of what the character required." },
      { q: "Is Little Women autobiographical?", a: "Little Women is substantially autobiographical — the March family is the Alcott family thinly fictionalized. Louisa May Alcott was Jo; her father Bronson Alcott was the model for the absent Mr March (a Transcendentalist philosopher rather than a Civil War chaplain, but similarly impractical and idealistic); her mother Abigail May Alcott was the model for Marmee; her sisters Anna, Elizabeth, and May were the models for Meg, Beth, and Amy. The novel is set in the house in Concord where the Alcotts lived, and many of the domestic episodes it describes — the amateur theatricals, the poverty, the management of household crises — are drawn from Alcott's own memories. The departure from autobiography is that in the novel, Beth dies young (as Alcott's sister Elizabeth did in real life), but the other sisters' endings differ from their actual fates. The novel is not a memoir but it is deeply personal." },
      { q: "What is the significance of Jo March as a character?", a: "Jo March is one of the most significant characters in American fiction for reasons that go beyond her appealing personality. She is the first major female protagonist in American literature who is explicitly, unambiguously ambitious as a writer — who wants to write not as a pastime but as a profession, who submits her work to magazines, who receives rejections and keeps trying, and who ultimately succeeds. She is also the first major female protagonist who articulates a preference for independence over marriage: her refusal of Laurie's proposal is a statement that she knows what she wants and does not want, and what she wants is not what convention says she should want. She has served as a model for ambitious, independent girls for over a century — readers from Simone de Beauvoir to J.K. Rowling to Meryl Streep have cited Jo March as a formative influence. She also, unusually for a nineteenth-century heroine, loses: she does not get the independence she wants, and her marriage to Professor Bhaer is understood by most readers as a partial defeat. That complexity is part of what makes her lasting." },
      { q: "What should I read after Little Women?", a: "Good Wives (1869) — published as part two of Little Women in American editions — is the essential next read; it follows the March sisters as young women facing adult life, love, and loss. Little Men (1871) follows Jo as the head of Plumfield, her school for boys; Jo's Boys (1886) completes the sequence. For the tradition Little Women belongs to: Anne of Green Gables by L.M. Montgomery (1908) is the Canadian equivalent — another spirited, imaginative girl in a loving family who grows up through imagination and stubbornness. A Tree Grows in Brooklyn by Betty Smith (1943) is the American successor — Francie Nolan growing up in poverty in Brooklyn, with Little Women's combination of family warmth and honest economics. The Secret Garden by Frances Hodgson Burnett (1911) is the essential British companion. For adult literary successors to Jo March: The House of Mirth by Edith Wharton (1905) shows what happens to women who don't navigate the same social pressures successfully." },
    ],
