    faq: [
      { q: "What is Good Wives about?", a: "Good Wives (1869) continues the story of the four March sisters from Little Women, taking them from adolescence into adulthood over several years. Meg, the eldest, marries John Brooke and faces the domestic realities of married life in modest circumstances — the novel is quite honest about how marriage changes a woman's position and the adjustments required. Jo goes to New York as a boarder, writes sensation stories for newspapers, and meets the German professor Friedrich Bhaer, whose disapproval of her stories prompts her to stop writing them and write something better. Beth's health, always fragile, declines through the novel and she dies peacefully — the novel's most genuinely moving sequence. Amy goes to Europe as the companion of their Aunt March and encounters Laurie there after his rejection by Jo; their friendship deepens into a love that results in marriage. The novel ends with a double wedding (Meg's and Amy's) and Jo's establishment at Plumfield with Professor Bhaer, whom she has agreed to marry. Its contested decisions — particularly Jo's marriage to Bhaer and Amy's marriage to Laurie — have been debated by readers since publication." },
      { q: "Why do many readers object to Amy Laurence marrying Laurie in Good Wives?", a: "The marriage of Amy March to Theodore Laurence (Laurie) in Good Wives is one of the most contested decisions in nineteenth-century American fiction. Laurie has been, in Little Women, Jo March's closest friend and the character the reader has spent the most time with; when he proposes to Jo and is rejected, many readers hope he will either persist or find himself a better match. Instead he goes to Europe, meets Amy, and marries her. Many readers feel this is a betrayal — of Jo, of the friendship, and of the reader's investment in Laurie. Defenders of the ending argue that Amy and Laurie are actually a better match: both are social, interested in appearances and society, and capable of the compromise that their marriage requires. Jo is explicitly said to be too similar to Laurie for marriage to work between them — two passionate, strong-willed people would fight rather than complement each other. Alcott herself seemed to find the readers' objections irritating; she wrote to correspondents that she had never intended Jo to marry Laurie and that the readers were wrong to expect it." },
      { q: "Who is Professor Bhaer and why does Jo marry him?", a: "Friedrich Bhaer is a German professor of philosophy whom Jo meets in New York, where he is a boarder at the same house she is staying in. He is middle-aged, not conventionally handsome, and teaches at a small school. He and Jo form a friendship based on intellectual companionship — he gives her books, she reads German with him, they discuss literature and ideas. When he discovers she is writing sensation stories for newspapers, he disapproves and says so; Jo stops writing the stories and begins writing something that comes from her own experience. When she returns to Concord to nurse Beth, Bhaer eventually follows and proposes. They establish Plumfield, a school for boys, together. The objection to the marriage is that Bhaer seems not worthy of Jo — too old, too dull, too ready to disapprove of her work. The counterargument is that Alcott deliberately made him unprepossessing to make the point that Jo's choice is about companionship and intellectual respect rather than romantic excitement." },
      { q: "How does Beth die in Good Wives?", a: "Beth March's death in Good Wives is one of the most carefully handled deaths in American fiction — unhurried, peaceful, and prepared for through the entire second volume. Beth's illness begins in Little Women, when she contracts scarlet fever while nursing the poor Hummel family and nearly dies; she recovers but is left permanently weakened. In Good Wives, the reader gradually understands that Beth will not recover her full strength, that she is declining gently without much pain, and that she herself knows and accepts this. Jo understands first. The death itself takes place quietly, with Jo beside her; Alcott does not describe the moment of death directly, cutting away from it with the statement that 'the first break came in the family circle' on an early spring morning. The absence of sentimentality and the careful preparation make the death more moving than it would be if Alcott had dramatized it; by the time it arrives, the reader has been waiting for it and is not surprised, only sad." },
      { q: "What should I read after Good Wives?", a: "Little Men (1871) follows Jo and Professor Bhaer running Plumfield as a school for boys; it is a different kind of book — episodic rather than dramatic — but essential for readers who want the complete March world. Jo's Boys (1886) is the final volume, following the Plumfield boys and the March sisters in their later lives. For the literary tradition: Anne of Green Gables by L.M. Montgomery (1908) is the most obvious companion — another spirited girl growing into a woman; the Anne books follow a similar arc to the March books across several volumes. Jane Eyre by Charlotte Brontë (1847) is the novel Jo March admires most; it rewards reading alongside Good Wives. A Tree Grows in Brooklyn by Betty Smith (1943) is the American successor in the tradition of family and economic honesty. For an adult counterpart to the March sisters' world: The House of Mirth by Edith Wharton (1905) shows what the same social pressures look like from the outside, without the protection of a loving family." },
    ],
