    intro: "Excellent Women (1952) is Barbara Pym's most celebrated novel — the story of Mildred Lathbury, an unmarried woman in her early thirties in 1950s London, who moves in the world of church committees, anthropologists, and new neighbors, and who is constantly called upon to be what the title calls an 'excellent woman' — useful, undemanding, dependable — without anyone thinking to ask what she might want for herself. Pym's prose is precise, dry, and surprisingly funny; she is the English comic novelist most compared to Jane Austen, and Excellent Women is her most characteristic book.",
