    faq: [
      { q: "What is The Virgin Suicides about?", a: "The Virgin Suicides (1993) is narrated by an unnamed collective of middle-aged men who were teenagers in suburban Michigan in the mid-1970s, looking back at the year in which all five Lisbon sisters — Cecilia, Lux, Bonnie, Mary, and Therese — took their own lives. The novel opens with the youngest, Cecilia, attempting suicide and surviving, then succeeding a few months later at a party. The Lisbon parents respond by increasingly restricting the other four girls — finally imprisoning them in the house for months — and the novel follows the neighborhood boys' obsessive attempt to understand the girls' inner lives through the artifacts they collect (diaries, photos, letters) and their memories of glimpsed moments. The book is not about why the girls died — Eugenides deliberately withholds any definitive explanation — but about the impossibility of fully knowing another person and about the particular blindness of the male gaze when directed at female adolescence." },
      { q: "Why is The Virgin Suicides narrated by 'we'?", a: "The collective first-person plural narrator — the 'we' of neighborhood boys now grown to middle-aged men — is the novel's most significant formal decision. By making the narrator a group, Eugenides establishes from the first sentence that this is a story about what is seen from the outside rather than known from the inside. The boys' collective perspective is both sympathetic and limited: they genuinely loved the Lisbon girls, genuinely grieved for them, genuinely tried to understand them — and they never succeeded. The 'we' narrator is also a commentary on the male gaze more broadly: the boys' obsessive collection of artifacts, their construction of theories, their attempts to reconstruct the girls' inner lives — all of this is a form of possession that fails to reach its object. By the end of the novel, the men still don't know why the girls died, and their inability to know is Eugenides's point: the Lisbon sisters remain mysterious because the perspective from which they are seen cannot access their subjectivity." },
      { q: "Is The Virgin Suicides based on a true story?", a: "The Virgin Suicides is not based on a specific true story, though Eugenides grew up in suburban Detroit and has said that the novel draws on the atmosphere of suburban Michigan in the 1970s. He has been careful to distinguish the novel from any particular event, and the collective narrator's framing — men who have been trying to understand something for decades — is a deliberate device that distances the narrative from journalistic reconstruction. The novel has been compared to the Sylvia Plath tradition and to the broader literary history of female adolescence, but its direct sources are more aesthetic (John Cheever's suburban fiction, Nabokov's interest in the unreliable male narrator) than biographical." },
      { q: "How does The Virgin Suicides relate to Sofia Coppola's 1999 film?", a: "Sofia Coppola's film adaptation of The Virgin Suicides is widely regarded as one of the most successful literary adaptations in recent cinema — it preserves the novel's atmosphere, its collective narration (rendered in voice-over), and its refusal to explain the sisters' deaths while adding a visual beauty that complements rather than replaces Eugenides's prose. Kirsten Dunst plays Lux, the most vivid of the sisters, and Josh Hartnett plays Trip Fontaine, the boy who briefly penetrates the Lisbon house. Coppola's film is faithful to the novel's spirit if not always to its letter, and the two works are best understood as distinct but deeply related — the film a translation of the novel's concerns into a different medium rather than a substitute for the reading experience. Many readers come to the novel after the film, and many film fans find the novel adds texture and interiority that the film's visual surface cannot fully provide." },
      { q: "What should I read after The Virgin Suicides?", a: "Middlesex (2002), Eugenides's Pulitzer Prize-winning second novel, is the natural next step — a multigenerational Greek-American family saga and a novel about gender identity, narrated by the intersex Cal Stephanides; it is larger and more ambitious than The Virgin Suicides, and very different in form, but shares the first novel's intelligence and formal inventiveness. The Marriage Plot (2011) is his third novel, set at Brown University in the early 1980s, about a love triangle and a woman's relationship to the Victorian marriage-plot novels she studies. Within The Virgin Suicides' thematic territory — female adolescence, suburban repression, retrospective narration — Sylvia Plath's The Bell Jar (1963) is the essential companion; Jeffrey Eugenides himself has cited it as an influence. Atonement by Ian McEwan (2001) covers similar territory of retrospective narration and the limits of a narrator's perspective. And A Tree Grows in Brooklyn by Betty Smith (1943) is the American coming-of-age novel about female adolescence that most directly anticipates Eugenides's sympathetic attention to that subject." },
    ],
