    faq: [
      { q: "What is The Marriage Plot about?", a: "The Marriage Plot (2011) is set primarily in the early 1980s — at Brown University in 1982, then in New York, India, and Europe over the following year. Madeleine Hanna is an English major writing her senior thesis on the marriage plot in Victorian fiction (the convention by which a novel ends with a marriage, which resolves all social and emotional complications). She is in love with Leonard Bankhead, a charismatic biology major who reads Derrida and turns out to have a serious bipolar disorder; she is also the object of Mitchell Grammaticus's devoted but unrequited love, a religion major who is trying to understand what he believes and who goes to India to work with Mother Teresa as a way of finding out. The novel follows all three through their senior year and the year after graduation, asking whether the literary models of romantic love that Madeleine has internalized from Victorian fiction help or hinder her navigation of the real relationship she is in. It is a novel about the gap between what you read and what you live, and about the specific experience of loving someone who is genuinely ill." },
      { q: "Is The Marriage Plot about Roland Barthes?", a: "The Marriage Plot opens with Madeleine reading Roland Barthes's A Lover's Discourse and finding it a more accurate account of romantic experience than the novels she has been studying. Barthes's influence runs through the novel — his account of the stages of romantic feeling, his interest in the gap between what is felt and what can be said, and his skepticism about the conventions that organize romantic narrative are all present in Eugenides's approach. But the novel is also a critique of the kind of literary theory that was dominant at Brown and other elite universities in the early 1980s: the structuralism and deconstruction that Madeleine's professors believe has superseded the Victorian novel and that her boyfriend Leonard applies to everything. Madeleine's thesis argues that the marriage plot — the Victorian convention that seemed to have been killed by modernism and postmodernism — is alive and structuring people's lives whether they know it or not. The novel itself is a demonstration of that argument." },
      { q: "What is Leonard Bankhead's role in The Marriage Plot?", a: "Leonard Bankhead is the novel's most compelling and most difficult character — a brilliant, charismatic, sexually confident science student who reads theory, is physically attractive, and also has severe bipolar disorder. His manic episodes produce the person Madeleine falls in love with; his depressive episodes produce someone she cannot reach and does not fully know how to help. Eugenides writes Leonard's mental illness with clinical specificity — the lithium, the weight gain, the flatness of the medicated state, the seductiveness of going off medication to feel alive again — and the novel is notable for treating bipolar disorder as a medical reality rather than a romantic attribute. Leonard is not Byron; his illness is genuinely destructive to him and to the people who love him, and the novel resists the temptation to aestheticize it. The question of what Madeleine owes Leonard, and what Leonard owes himself, is the novel's central ethical problem." },
      { q: "What is Mitchell Grammaticus's spiritual journey about?", a: "Mitchell Grammaticus is the novel's third major perspective — a religious studies major from Detroit who genuinely believes in something and who goes to India partly to test that belief and partly to run away from his unrequited love for Madeleine. His India chapters, in which he works with Mother Teresa's Missionaries of Charity in Calcutta, are among the novel's most interesting sections: he does the work, finds it both more and less than he expected, and comes back changed in ways he cannot entirely account for. Mitchell represents the novel's interest in the spiritual dimension that Victorian fiction's marriage plot originally contained — the sense that marriage was a sacrament as well as a social arrangement — and his journey is Eugenides's way of asking what remains of that dimension when the formal religious context has been removed. Mitchell's story is quieter than Madeleine's and Leonard's, but it gives the novel its third and most important perspective on the question of what love is and what it asks of a person." },
      { q: "What should I read after The Marriage Plot?", a: "Fresh Complaint (2017), Eugenides's story collection, is the essential next step — years of stories that show his range beyond the three novels. The Virgin Suicides (1993) and Middlesex (2002) should both be read if they haven't been; the full picture of Eugenides's career requires all three novels. The Marriage Plot's explicit engagement with the Victorian marriage-plot tradition makes Middlemarch by George Eliot (1872) — the novel it is most directly in conversation with — an obvious companion, along with Jane Austen's Emma (1815) and E.M. Forster's A Room with a View (1908). For other novels that engage self-consciously with literary tradition: A.S. Byatt's Possession (1990) does something similar with Victorian poetry and romantic convention; David Lodge's academic comedies (starting with Changing Places, 1975) cover the same literary-theory milieu from a more comic angle. And Sylvia Plath's The Bell Jar (1963) is the ancestor for the specific experience of a brilliant young woman navigating the gap between her reading and her life." },
    ],
