    faq: [
      { q: "What is American Gods about?", a: "American Gods (2001) follows Shadow Moon, a large, quiet man who is released from prison to learn that his wife Laura has died in a car crash. He is recruited by Mr. Wednesday — later revealed to be Odin, the Norse god of wisdom, war, and death — to accompany him on a road trip across the American Midwest and South as Wednesday tries to recruit the other old gods for a war against the new gods of America (Media, Technology, the Internet, the stock market). The old gods — brought to America by the immigrants who worshipped them — are living diminished lives as mortals: Anansi runs a funeral home in Chicago, Czernobog is a retired slaughterhouse worker in Illinois, Czernobog's sister is a taxi driver. The new gods want to destroy the old ones because belief is a limited resource, and the new gods' devotees are more numerous. Shadow accompanies Wednesday through this strange America, learning its hidden landscape of sacred places and forgotten shrines, until the novel's central mystery — what Wednesday is actually planning — comes into focus." },
      { q: "What does American Gods say about America?", a: "American Gods is a meditation on what America does to the things it absorbs. The old gods — Anansi, Odin, Anansi, Bilquis, the slavic gods, the Polynesian gods, Anubis — were brought to America by the people who believed in them, but America has a way of wearing things down to nothing, of consuming belief and leaving only diminished remnants. The new gods — Media, Technology, Mr. World — are the gods America actually worships, and they are powerful but hollow, dependent on continuous attention in a way that the old gods, rooted in something deeper, are not. Gaiman is interested in the America that doesn't appear in guidebooks — the roadside attractions, the small towns with strange histories, the diners and motels where people stop on the way to somewhere else. The novel suggests that the sacred is hiding in this forgotten America, and that the most important things about American culture are not in the cities but in the places between them." },
      { q: "What are the mythological sources in American Gods?", a: "American Gods draws on an enormous range of world mythologies, rendered in the 'Coming to America' chapters interspersed through the main narrative. These chapters — some of the novel's most celebrated passages — follow different groups of people bringing their gods to America across centuries: Norsemen bringing Odin, Anansi, and the African trickster gods arriving with enslaved people, Slavic and Egyptian and Polynesian gods all making the crossing in different centuries and under different circumstances. Gaiman researched the mythological sources carefully — the Norse myths through the Eddas, the African religions through scholarly studies, the Native American traditions through consultation with indigenous scholars — and the gods he depicts are recognizable variations of their mythological originals rather than inventions. Norse mythology is the most central (Odin/Wednesday, Czernobog, Anansi/Loki), and readers who want to understand American Gods more deeply often read Norse Mythology, Gaiman's own retelling, alongside it." },
      { q: "What is the preferred text of American Gods?", a: "American Gods exists in two versions: the original 2001 edition and the Author's Preferred Text published in 2011, which includes about 12,000 words of material that was cut from the original for length. The two versions tell the same story with the same ending, but the Author's Preferred Text includes expanded scenes, additional backstory for Shadow and some secondary characters, and additional 'Coming to America' chapters. Most readers who have read both say that the Author's Preferred Text is the richer and more satisfying version, and it is the one most likely to be available in current printings. Readers who want the fullest experience of the novel should look for editions explicitly labeled 'Author's Preferred Text'; the TV series developed by Starz is based on the novel but diverges significantly in plot and tone." },
      { q: "What should I read after American Gods?", a: "Anansi Boys (2005) is the direct companion — a novel set in the same world that follows Anansi's two sons, one of whom knew their father was a god and one of whom did not; it is more comic and smaller in scale than American Gods, and it works well as a follow-up. The Ocean at the End of the Lane (2013) is Gaiman's most personal novel — a man remembering a summer from his childhood when the world turned out to be larger and stranger than he knew. Norse Mythology (2017) is Gaiman's retelling of the Norse myths that underlie American Gods, and it enriches the novel considerably to read it alongside or after. For mythological fiction in a similar vein: Rick Riordan's Percy Jackson series started the contemporary wave of mythology-for-fiction; but for adult readers, Madeline Miller's Circe (2018) and The Song of Achilles (2011) are the most celebrated recent literary treatments of classical mythology, and they cover some of the same territory as Gaiman's Norse material approached from Greek sources." },
    ],
