    faq: [
      { q: "What is That Old Cape Magic about?", a: "That Old Cape Magic (2009) follows Jack Griffin, a film screenwriter who spent most of his career as a literature professor before escaping to Hollywood. His parents — both failed academics who love literature and resent each other — are dead, and Jack has been driving around with their ashes in the trunk of his car, unable to scatter them. The novel takes place over two family weddings: his daughter Laura's wedding, and his wife Joy's brother's wedding, held a year later. Between the two weddings, Jack's marriage to Joy begins to unravel, partly because of his inability to let go of his parents and the person he was with them, and partly because of a brief affair with a colleague. The novel is about the relationship between the family you were born into and the family you build, and about the way the habits and patterns of the first generation work themselves out in the second. Russo draws explicitly on his own experience — he has spoken in interviews about the autobiographical elements — and the result is a quieter and more introspective novel than most of his work." },
      { q: "How does That Old Cape Magic compare to Russo's other novels?", a: "That Old Cape Magic is the Russo novel most readers find least satisfying on first encounter and most rewarding on reflection — it is smaller, more personal, and more concerned with the interior life of its protagonist than with the broad comic canvas of Nobody's Fool or the social scope of Empire Falls. Readers who come to it looking for the warm ensemble comedy of his best work often feel it is too narrow and too self-indulgent; readers who come to it as an autobiographical meditation on parents and marriage find it moving and precise. It is the Russo novel that most clearly shows the influence of John Updike's later work — the middle-aged professional man accounting for his life, the accumulated weight of small choices — rather than the Dickensian social comedy that characterizes his upstate New York novels. It is best approached as a companion to his larger work rather than as an entry point." },
      { q: "Why are Jack's parents so central to That Old Cape Magic?", a: "Jack's parents are the novel's true subject — the marriage he has been trying to understand and escape his entire life. Both of them were academics who wanted more than provincial university life gave them and who blamed each other (and sometimes Jack) for their disappointment. Their annual Cape Cod vacations — the title's 'old cape magic' — were the occasions when they were briefly the people they wanted to be, the people who quoted Keats on the beach and felt the romance of literature. Off the Cape, they were themselves again: competitive, bitter, mutually diminishing. Jack has absorbed this dynamic — the belief that somewhere else, one is a better version of oneself — and the novel is about his coming to understand how deeply that belief has shaped his own marriage and his own limitations. The ashes in the trunk are not just a comic device; they are the literal weight of his inheritance, and scattering them is the novel's central act of release." },
      { q: "What is the Cape Cod setting's significance in That Old Cape Magic?", a: "Cape Cod in That Old Cape Magic functions as a version of what the moon is in Moon Palace or the Midwest is in Nobody's Fool — a place that has been given a significance beyond its physical reality by the people who love it. For Jack's parents, Cape Cod is where they could briefly inhabit the lives they had imagined for themselves; for Jack, it is the place where his parents were almost happy and where he understood what a happy family might look like; for Joy, Jack's wife, it is a place she has heard about too many times to see clearly. Russo uses the Cape's specific geography — the old hotels, the beaches, the summer crowds — to ground the novel in a recognizable world, but the Cape's real function is as a figure for the gap between the life you imagine and the one you inhabit. The novel's title, from an old song, captures the nostalgic quality of the place as Jack carries it: something that had magic once, or seemed to, and that has left its residue in his life." },
      { q: "What should I read after That Old Cape Magic?", a: "Chances Are... (2019), Russo's most recent novel, is the closest companion — three male college friends reunited on Martha's Vineyard forty years after a fourth friend disappeared; it shares That Old Cape Magic's New England coastal setting and its interest in the relationship between the person you were in youth and the person you became. Bridge of Sighs (2007) is similarly meditative and retrospective, though much longer. For fiction that shares That Old Cape Magic's autobiographical quality and its interest in parents and marriage: John Updike's Too Far to Walk (1965) and Couples (1968) cover similar ground of academic/professional men examining their lives; Richard Yates's Easter Parade (1976) is the master work of a writer accounting for how a life failed to become what it was supposed to be; and Anne Tyler's Breathing Lessons (1988), a Pulitzer Prize winner about one day in a long marriage, shares the emotional precision of That Old Cape Magic without the Cape setting." },
    ],
