    faq: [
      { q: "What is The Risk Pool about?", a: "The Risk Pool (1988) follows Ned Hall from childhood to early adulthood in Mohawk, a declining upstate New York mill town. The novel opens with Ned living with his mother Jenny, a respectable and increasingly unstable woman whose inability to cope with Sam Hall's departure has destabilized her in ways she cannot admit. When Jenny has a breakdown and is institutionalized, Ned is sent to live with Sam — his father, who has been living at the periphery of Ned's life since leaving Jenny, drinking at Ward's bar, working intermittently, and being generally beloved by everyone who doesn't depend on him. The novel follows Ned's gradual understanding of his father — Sam's generosity and his irresponsibility, his charm and his self-destructiveness — and his own uncertain trajectory. He goes to college (the first in his family), has relationships, comes back to Mohawk, and tries to figure out whether he is his father's son in the ways that matter. Russo draws on his own childhood in Gloversville, New York, and The Risk Pool is the most personal of his novels, though 'autobiographical' understates how completely he has transformed the material." },
      { q: "How does The Risk Pool compare to Nobody's Fool?", a: "The Risk Pool and Nobody's Fool share the same upstate New York world — both are set in declining mill towns, both feature working-class men who are charming and irresponsible, both are interested in the relationship between fathers and sons in that milieu. The Risk Pool is slightly darker and more autobiographical; Nobody's Fool is funnier and more warmly comic. Sully Sullivan in Nobody's Fool is a fully realized comic character in a way that Sam Hall is not quite — Sam is more of a background presence, seen through his son's eyes, and the novel's emotional center is Ned's navigation between his two parents rather than Sam himself. Many readers who love Nobody's Fool find The Risk Pool valuable as a companion that shows Russo working through some of the same materials in a more personal register; others prefer The Risk Pool's more direct emotional confrontation with the father-son relationship. Neither is a sequel to the other, though they share a town (Mohawk) and a sensibility." },
      { q: "Who is Sam Hall in The Risk Pool?", a: "Sam Hall is one of Russo's great creations — a man of enormous personal charm and minimal responsibility, beloved by everyone who doesn't depend on him and a catastrophe for everyone who does. He drinks, he works when he feels like it, he has no savings and no ambition toward any, and he is nevertheless genuinely kind in ways that the more responsible people in Ned's life sometimes are not. Russo is not romanticizing irresponsibility — the novel is very clear about the damage Sam has done to Jenny and is doing to Ned — but he is also interested in why Sam is so appealing, why Ned gradually falls under his spell even knowing what Sam is. Sam's world at Ward's bar, his circle of similarly marginal friends, his cheerful refusal to be troubled by what troubles everyone else — these are rendered with affection even as the novel insists on the cost. Sam Hall is a prototype for Sully Sullivan, and readers who love one usually love the other." },
      { q: "What is the significance of Mohawk in The Risk Pool?", a: "Mohawk — like North Bath in Nobody's Fool and Empire Falls in the Pulitzer Prize-winning novel — is Russo's version of the American mill town in decline. Based loosely on Gloversville, New York, where Russo grew up, Mohawk is a place that was built around the tanning and glove-making industries, which began declining in the mid-twentieth century and continued to decline throughout the novel's period. The town and its people are shaped by this economic history — the good jobs are gone, the people who could leave mostly have, and the people who remain are doing so out of attachment rather than opportunity. Russo is not sentimental about this: Mohawk is cramped, limited, and full of people who have made their peace with diminished expectations. But he is also not contemptuous of it, and his central argument — present in The Risk Pool, Nobody's Fool, and Empire Falls — is that these places and these people deserve serious literary attention rather than the condescension of being treated as merely provincial." },
      { q: "What should I read after The Risk Pool?", a: "Nobody's Fool (1993) is the most natural companion — set in North Bath rather than Mohawk but featuring Sully Sullivan, who is a kind of development of Sam Hall into a full protagonist. Empire Falls (2001) is the Pulitzer Prize winner and Russo's most formally ambitious small-town novel. Straight Man (1997) is the most purely comic of his novels — an academic satire that shows a different side of his range. For fiction about the American Rust Belt in a similar vein: Bonnie Jo Campbell's American Salvage (2009) covers rural Michigan in story-collection form with a similar attention to working-class life at the economic margins; Stewart O'Nan's Snow Angels (1994) is set in western Pennsylvania and shares Russo's interest in the texture of ordinary small-town American life; and Andre Dubus's Stories of a Bad Boy (1964) and subsequent collections are the essential precedents for this kind of close, compassionate attention to working-class American men and their failures." },
    ],
