    faq: [
      { q: "What is Jesus' Son about?", a: "Jesus' Son (1992) is a collection of eleven loosely linked stories narrated by a young man known only as 'Fuckhead' — a drifter and heroin addict moving through Iowa City, Phoenix, and other Midwestern locations during the early 1970s. The stories follow him through overdoses and hospital visits, road accidents and bar fights, brief jobs and briefer relationships, and the kind of visionary moments that addiction and its aftermath can produce in the gaps between catastrophes. The collection is not organized by strict chronology — the stories loop and contradict each other slightly, as memory under the influence of drugs tends to — and the narrator's unreliability is part of Johnson's point: this is consciousness as it actually works when it is damaged, and the prose mimics the altered state it describes. The title comes from Lou Reed's lyric 'when I'm rushing on my run and I feel just like Jesus' Son' from the Velvet Underground's 'Heroin,' and the stories earn that reference: they are about addiction as a kind of religion, with its own ecstasies and its own damnations." },
      { q: "Is Jesus' Son a novel or a short story collection?", a: "Jesus' Son is typically described as a linked story collection — eleven individual stories that share a narrator and a loosely implied chronology but that were published separately before being collected. Some readers and critics describe it as a novel-in-stories because of the shared narrator and the sense of cumulative portrait that the collection builds; Johnson himself was somewhat vague about the distinction. The stories are short — several are only a few pages — and they do not add up to a conventional plot arc; instead they accumulate into a character study of the narrator and a portrait of a particular American subculture and era. Reading it in one sitting (it is very short — under 150 pages) is the recommended approach; the stories gain from their proximity to each other, and the hallucinatory effect of the prose intensifies as you move through them." },
      { q: "What makes Denis Johnson's prose style distinctive?", a: "Johnson's prose in Jesus' Son and throughout his career operates at an unusual pitch — it is close to the surface of consciousness without being stream-of-consciousness; it reports what the narrator perceives without fully explaining it; and it moves between the mundane and the visionary without warning. The sentences are often short and declarative, but they arrive at unexpected images: a man bleeding out on a road becomes an occasion for lyrical observation; a hospital waiting room contains a vision of terrible beauty. Johnson was a recovering addict, and his prose mimics the cognitive texture of that experience — the narrowed attention, the sudden clarity, the way meaning collapses and reconstitutes itself. He was also influenced by the religious traditions of Catholicism and American evangelicalism, and his work is full of fallen grace and the possibility of redemption without sentimentality. Raymond Carver is the most frequently cited comparison — minimalism, working-class settings, damaged people — but Johnson's spirituality and his willingness to go into visionary territory set him apart." },
      { q: "What is the best order to read Denis Johnson's books?", a: "Most readers start with Jesus' Son (1992) because it is short, immediately powerful, and gives you Johnson's voice in its most concentrated form. After that, the order depends on taste. For more short fiction, The Largesse of the Sea Maiden (2018), his final posthumous collection, is essential reading. For his novels, Angels (1983) is the most structurally direct; Tree of Smoke (2007) is the most ambitious; Train Dreams (2011) is the most spare and beautiful. Already Dead (1997) is the most genre-inflected (noir) and divides opinion. Fiskadoro (1985) is the most formally experimental — a post-apocalyptic novel that reads almost like magical realism — and is beloved by the readers who find it. The Pale King by David Foster Wallace is sometimes recommended alongside Johnson for readers who want another American writer who could render altered consciousness and spiritual yearning at the same time." },
      { q: "What should I read after Jesus' Son?", a: "The natural next step within Johnson's work is The Largesse of the Sea Maiden (2018), his final story collection, which shows how his voice developed over the thirty years since Jesus' Son. Train Dreams (2011), his Pulitzer-finalist novella, is perhaps his most formally perfect single work. For readers who want to go to other writers: Raymond Carver's Cathedral (1983) is the most direct comparison — similar minimalism, damaged characters, grace arriving unexpectedly. Denis Cooper's George Miles Cycle (beginning with Closer, 1989) covers some of the same drug-culture territory with a more extreme sensibility. Tom McGuane's Nobody's Angel (1982) shares Jesus' Son's Western landscapes and its interest in men who cannot fit the available lives. Barry Hannah's Airships (1978) is another essential American story collection from the same era that operates at a similarly high temperature. And Richard Price's The Wanderers (1974), for a different register of the same period's working-class American world." },
    ],
