    faq: [
      { q: "What is Tree of Smoke about?", a: "Tree of Smoke (2007) follows multiple characters whose lives are entangled with the Vietnam War from 1963 to 1983. Skip Sands is a young CIA operative assigned to assist his uncle, Colonel Francis Sands — a legendary intelligence figure who believes that psychological operations ('psy-ops'), not military force, are the key to winning the war. The Colonel's project, codenamed Tree of Smoke, involves a network of double agents and disinformation operations whose ultimate purpose Skip cannot quite determine. Also central are James and Bill Houston, brothers from Phoenix who enlist for different reasons and find their war nothing like what they expected; Kathy Jones, a Canadian missionary nurse who falls in love with Skip; and Nguyen Hao, a Vietnamese man whose allegiances are genuinely multiple and whose fate is the novel's most devastating thread. Johnson is not interested in the mechanics of combat — there are few battle scenes — but in the psychological and moral damage that the war produces in everyone who passes through it, and in the particular American tendency to wrap that damage in the language of mission and purpose." },
      { q: "Is Tree of Smoke hard to read?", a: "Tree of Smoke is Johnson's most demanding book by length (700 pages) and by narrative complexity. Unlike Jesus' Son, which is concentrated and immediate, Tree of Smoke is panoramic, multi-voiced, and deliberately resistant to the war-novel conventions that might make it easier to follow. The Colonel's Tree of Smoke project is kept deliberately obscure — Johnson does not fully explain it, because characters inside it don't fully understand it — and the novel's structure follows multiple characters across two decades without always signaling clearly where we are in time. Readers who come to it after Jesus' Son and Train Dreams are better prepared for Johnson's voice, but even experienced Johnson readers find Tree of Smoke a significant undertaking. Most who finish it consider it worth the effort — it is a serious reckoning with the American experience of Vietnam — but it is not the right starting point, and readers who want to test whether Johnson is for them should read Jesus' Son first." },
      { q: "How does Tree of Smoke compare to other Vietnam War novels?", a: "Tree of Smoke sits in a distinct tradition within Vietnam War fiction, closer to psychological and political novels like Graham Greene's The Quiet American (1955) — which is an obvious antecedent — than to combat narratives like Tim O'Brien's The Things They Carried (1990) or Karl Marlantes's Matterhorn (2010). Johnson is interested in the intelligence and psychological operations side of the war, in the civilian and Vietnamese perspectives (Nguyen Hao's sections are among the novel's most powerful), and in the aftermath — what the war made of the people who survived it. The Quiet American comparison is apt both because of the CIA/intelligence setting and because of the novel's interest in American innocence and its consequences. Other useful comparisons include Robert Stone's Dog Soldiers (1974), which also explores the corruption that the war sent back home, and Francis Ford Coppola's Apocalypse Now, which shares Tree of Smoke's Conrad-inflected interest in what happens when a mission becomes its own justification." },
      { q: "Who are the Houston brothers in Tree of Smoke?", a: "James and Bill Houston are two brothers from Phoenix who enlist at different points during the Vietnam era and whose stories ground the novel in a kind of American normalcy that the intelligence world of Skip Sands and the Colonel lacks. James enlists first, in 1963; Bill follows later. Their sections of the novel are in some ways the most conventional — they are the ordinary American soldiers whose experience is the war everyone knows — but Johnson gives them enough specificity and interiority that they feel fully realized rather than representative. Their post-war lives, particularly Bill's, are among the novel's most devastating threads: Johnson follows them into the 1970s and 1980s, tracking how the war continues to work on them after they have left it behind. The Houston brothers connect Tree of Smoke to the rest of Johnson's work — they inhabit the same America as the narrator of Jesus' Son, the same damaged Midwest and Southwest, and their fates feel continuous with the fates of characters in his other books." },
      { q: "What should I read after Tree of Smoke?", a: "Within Johnson's work, Train Dreams (2011) is the ideal next step — it is much shorter (a novella), set in the entirely different world of the early American West, and demonstrates his range by being his most restrained and beautiful work. The Largesse of the Sea Maiden (2018), his final story collection, is essential. For Vietnam War fiction comparisons: Tim O'Brien's The Things They Carried (1990) is the indispensable companion from the combat side; Graham Greene's The Quiet American (1955) is the antecedent for the CIA/intelligence thread; Karl Marlantes's Matterhorn (2010) provides the sustained combat perspective that Johnson deliberately avoids. Robert Stone's Dog Soldiers (1974) covers the drug-and-corruption aftermath of the war that Tree of Smoke gestures toward. And for American fiction of similar scope and ambition set in the Cold War era, Don DeLillo's Libra (1988) — about the Kennedy assassination and the intelligence world — is a natural companion." },
    ],
