    slug: 'cities-plain-mccarthy',
    intro: "Cities of the Plain (1998) is the third and final volume of Cormac McCarthy's Border Trilogy, completing the intertwined stories of John Grady Cole (introduced in All the Pretty Horses) and Billy Parham (introduced in The Crossing). Set in 1952 on a New Mexico cattle ranch near the Ciudad Juárez border, the novel brings the two young cowboys together as coworkers and friends, and follows John Grady's fatal pursuit of Magdalena, a sixteen-year-old epileptic prostitute from one of Juárez's brothels, whom he intends to marry and rescue. The novel is shorter and more concentrated than its predecessors, and it functions as a tragic coda to the trilogy — an ending to John Grady's story, to Billy's youth, and to the world both of them have loved.",
    startWith: "All the Pretty Horses",
    books: [
      { title: "All the Pretty Horses", author: "Cormac McCarthy", year: 1992, note: "Begin here — John Grady Cole's introduction and the trilogy's most accessible volume" },
      { title: "The Crossing", author: "Cormac McCarthy", year: 1994, note: "Introduces Billy Parham; read before Cities of the Plain for full context" },
      { title: "Cities of the Plain", author: "Cormac McCarthy", year: 1998, note: "The trilogy concludes here — both protagonists, a tragic ending" },
      { title: "Blood Meridian", author: "Cormac McCarthy", year: 1985, note: "The prequel in spirit — the same borderlands a century earlier, far darker" },
      { title: "No Country for Old Men", author: "Cormac McCarthy", year: 2005, note: "Returns to the Southwest; more thriller but shares the trilogy's moral gravity" },
      { title: "The Road", author: "Cormac McCarthy", year: 2006, note: "Pulitzer Prize winner; a very different McCarthy but essential for the full picture" },
      { title: "Suttree", author: "Cormac McCarthy", year: 1979, note: "The Appalachian masterpiece; rewarding for readers who want his full range" },
      { title: "The Passenger", author: "Cormac McCarthy", year: 2022, note: "Late career return — revisits some of the trilogy's elegiac concerns" },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: "What is Cities of the Plain about?", a: "Cities of the Plain (1998) is set in 1952 on a cattle ranch in New Mexico, a few miles from the border at El Paso and Ciudad Juárez. John Grady Cole and Billy Parham — the protagonists of the first two Border Trilogy novels — work together on the ranch as young cowboys. John Grady becomes obsessed with a sixteen-year-old Mexican prostitute named Magdalena, who suffers from epilepsy and is owned by Eduardo, the sinister and philosophically inclined manager of the brothel where she works. John Grady resolves to marry Magdalena and remove her from Juárez, a plan that Billy, Eduardo, and most of the novel's other characters recognize as catastrophically dangerous. The novel follows the working life of the ranch — cattle work, border crossings, card games, conversations between old men — alongside John Grady's doomed pursuit, ending in violence that closes the stories of both young men and the world they have inhabited. An epilogue set decades later shows Billy as an old man, still alive and still carrying the losses of his life." },
      { q: "Do I need to read the other Border Trilogy novels first?", a: "Technically Cities of the Plain can be read on its own, but it is significantly richer if you have read All the Pretty Horses (1992) and The Crossing (1994) first. John Grady Cole's history — the lost Texas ranch, the Mexico journey, the imprisonment at Saltillo — is part of who he is in Cities of the Plain, and readers who know it understand both his romanticism and his recklessness more clearly. Billy Parham's three Mexico journeys, and the losses that defined them, similarly inform his patience and his sadness in the later novel. Cities of the Plain is the shortest of the three and the most concentrated — it functions as a tragic ending rather than a self-contained story — and readers who come to it without the earlier volumes may find it thin where readers who come from them find it devastating. Most McCarthy readers recommend reading the trilogy in order." },
      { q: "What is the significance of the title Cities of the Plain?", a: "The title refers to Sodom and Gomorrah — the cities of the plain destroyed by God in Genesis — and it frames the novel's Ciudad Juárez, with its brothels, its violence, and its borderland corruption, as a latter-day version of that doomed landscape. John Grady's attempt to rescue Magdalena from the brothel takes on a quasi-biblical character: he is trying to bring someone out of a place that is constitutionally unredeemable, and the novel makes clear — through Eduardo's speeches, through the structure of the story, through the way the border itself works — that the cities of the plain cannot be escaped by individual will. The title also applies to John Grady himself: his code of honor, his romantic conviction that certain things can be saved if you want them badly enough and are brave enough, is a version of the same faith that turns out to be wrong. The cities burn; the people who love them are destroyed or exiled; only Billy survives, and the epilogue shows him as a very old man who has outlived everyone and everything he loved." },
      { q: "How does the epilogue of Cities of the Plain work?", a: "The epilogue — set in 2002, fifty years after the main story — shows Billy Parham as an old man, drifting and homeless in the American Southwest. He is taken in by a family and has a long, strange dream-conversation with a figure who may be Death or may be an aspect of himself, a conversation that echoes the philosophical exchanges embedded throughout the trilogy and in some ways provides the trilogy's final statement on what McCarthy has been arguing about fate, about loss, about the question of whether human life has meaning in a universe that does not seem to care. The epilogue has divided readers: some find it a fitting, haunting conclusion that gives the trilogy's elegiac tone its last word; others find it too abstract and too different in register from the human specificity of the main novel. It is also the site of a remarkable literary coincidence: the old man Billy meets in the epilogue has a speech that overlaps with a speech given by a character in The Crossing, suggesting a recursive, mythic quality to the trilogy's world." },
      { q: "What should I read after Cities of the Plain?", a: "Having completed the Border Trilogy, most readers next want either No Country for Old Men (2005) — McCarthy's return to the Southwest landscape in a more thriller-like form — or Blood Meridian (1985), the earlier novel set in the same borderlands in the 1840s and widely considered his masterwork. The Road (2006) offers a very different McCarthy but is essential for the full picture: postapocalyptic, spare, and emotionally direct in ways that the Trilogy is not. The Passenger (2022) is his late-career return, set partly in New Orleans and partly in an America defined by loss and by physics and by mathematical obsession — it shares the Trilogy's elegiac quality and its preoccupation with what survives. For readers who want to go back to the earlier McCarthy, Suttree (1979) is the Appalachian masterpiece: a long, shaggy, brilliant novel about a man living on a houseboat on the Tennessee River, and it is as different from the Border Trilogy as it is possible to get while remaining unmistakably the same author." },
    ],
