    slug: 'yellow-dog-amis',
    intro: "Yellow Dog (2003) by Martin Amis is his eighth novel — Amis's most divisive and most misunderstood novel, a work that operates simultaneously on several distinct registers (satirical journalism, criminal thriller, royal family pastiche, cosmic horror) and that was almost universally savaged on publication.\n\nThe novel follows several storylines: Xan Meo, a reformed criminal turned writer who suffers a brain injury and begins to regress to a state of violent masculinity; Clint Smoker, a journalist for a pornographic newspaper; Henry England IX, a fictional future king; and a gangster known as Threnody. The various plots intersect and comment on each other in ways that are not always easy to follow.\n\nYellow Dog is Amis's most experimental and formally ambitious novel since Time's Arrow, and it is also the novel that most clearly shows the risks of his method — the verbal energy and formal ambition that make his best work possible can, when applied to weaker material, produce something that is both difficult and unsatisfying. But it deserves more serious attention than it has received, and it is read with more sympathy when approached as a formal experiment than as a failed conventional novel.",
    startWith: "Yellow Dog is best read after Money, London Fields, and The Information as an Amis completist. Any Vintage or Cape edition works. Medium length (340 pages). Approach it as a formal experiment.",
