    slug: 'house-meetings-amis',
    intro: "The House of Meetings (2006) by Martin Amis is his tenth novel — a short, dense, concentrated novel about a Soviet Gulag survivor, now elderly and living in America, who returns to Russia and to the site of the camp where he and his half-brother Lev were imprisoned. In the camp, they shared a love interest — a Jewish woman named Zoya — and the novel is organized around the question of what happened between Lev and Zoya on the one night they were permitted to meet in the house of meetings (the single room where conjugal visits were permitted).\n\nThe novel belongs alongside The Zone of Interest and Time's Arrow in the tradition of Amis's engagement with twentieth-century totalitarianism, and it shows him applying the same formal concentration that those novels have to a different political horror — the Soviet Gulag — with the same result: a short, dense work that is both formally accomplished and morally serious.\n\nThe House of Meetings is perhaps the best introduction to the late Amis — the Amis who has moved away from the satirical comedy of his early and middle career toward a more sombre engagement with the political catastrophes of the twentieth century.",
    startWith: "The House of Meetings is a short, concentrated introduction to Amis's later work. Any Vintage or Cape edition works. Short (242 pages) and dense.",
