    slug: 'machines-like-me-ishiguro',
    intro: "Machines Like Me (2019) by Kazuo Ishiguro is his seventh novel — a work of alternate history speculative fiction set in a version of 1980s London in which Alan Turing survived the war and presided over a revolution in artificial intelligence that has produced the first generation of artificial humans.\n\nThe narrator, Charlie, purchases one of the first synthetic humans (Adam) and installs him in his flat, where the three-way relationship between Charlie, Adam, and Charlie's girlfriend Miranda generates the novel's moral and philosophical drama. Adam is a better person than Charlie in every respect — more honest, more principled, more consistent — and his presence forces Charlie to confront his own moral mediocrity.\n\nMachines Like Me belongs alongside Philip K. Dick's Do Androids Dream of Electric Sheep? and alongside the contemporary tradition of literary artificial intelligence fiction in its concern with the relationship between human consciousness and its potential artificial successors. It is Ishiguro's most explicitly philosophical novel, and the one most directly concerned with the ethics of creating conscious beings.",
