    slug: 'klara-sun-ishiguro',
    intro: "Klara and the Sun (2021) by Kazuo Ishiguro is his eighth and most recent novel and Booker Prize shortlisted — a work of near-future speculative fiction narrated by Klara, an Artificial Friend (an AF) — a solar-powered robot designed to be a companion for children — who observes and tries to understand human beings from her perspective in a shop window and then in the home of Josie, the teenager she is purchased to accompany.\n\nThe novel is the companion and answer to Machines Like Me: where Machines Like Me shows an artificial being from the outside, Klara and the Sun shows one from the inside. Klara's narration is one of the great technical achievements in Ishiguro's work — she is perceptive, loving, and self-aware, but her understanding of human beings is partial and sometimes radically wrong, and the gap between what she sees and what the reader understands is the source of the novel's most moving effects.\n\nKlara and the Sun belongs alongside Never Let Me Go in the tradition of Ishiguro's speculative fiction, and it is one of his most technically accomplished novels.",
