    slug: 'when-we-were-orphans-ishiguro',
    intro: "When We Were Orphans (2000) by Kazuo Ishiguro is his fifth novel and Booker Prize shortlisted — a novel about Christopher Banks, a celebrated English detective in the 1930s who returns to Shanghai, where he was orphaned as a child, to solve the mystery of his parents' disappearance.\n\nThe novel is told in first person by Banks, whose reliability as a narrator is one of the novel's central questions. Banks is charming, apparently competent, and entirely certain of his own importance and ability — and the reader slowly comes to understand that his self-image is radically different from reality, that the mystery he believes he is solving is a fantasy, and that his entire professional identity may have been constructed around a childhood trauma that he has never been able to confront directly.\n\nWhen We Were Orphans belongs alongside The Remains of the Day in the tradition of Ishiguro's great unreliable narrator novels, and it belongs alongside Graham Greene's work in the tradition of the English literary novel that uses the thriller genre's conventions while undermining them. It is Ishiguro's most formally playful novel, and the one in which the gap between the narrator's self-image and reality is most radical.",
    startWith: "When We Were Orphans is best read after The Remains of the Day as a companion to Ishiguro's unreliable narrators. Any Vintage or Faber edition works. Medium length (335 pages).",
