    slug: 'sparsholt-affair-hollinghurst',
    intro: "The Sparsholt Affair (2017) by Alan Hollinghurst is his sixth and most recent novel — a large, quietly devastating novel that follows the legacy of David Sparsholt, a beautiful young man who arrives at Oxford in 1940 and whose subsequent sexual scandal — the 'Sparsholt Affair' — is only partially glimpsed before the novel shifts forward in time to follow his son Freddie, a gay portrait painter in contemporary London.\n\nThe novel is organized in five sections spanning 1940 to the 2010s — the same long temporal structure as The Stranger's Child — and it is similarly concerned with the way that the past is constructed and reconstructed by the people who live in its shadow. David Sparsholt is the novel's absent center: we see him as a young man in 1940 Oxford, and then we see the consequences of his affair forty years later, but the affair itself is never fully shown. His son Freddie's contemporary life — his work as a portrait painter, his relationships, his family — is both a continuation and a consequence of the father's story.\n\nThe Sparsholt Affair belongs alongside The Stranger's Child and The Line of Beauty as Hollinghurst's great works of social panorama, and it shows him continuing to develop the century-spanning formal structure that he has made his own.",
