    slug: 'little-stranger-waters',
    intro: "The Little Stranger (2009) by Sarah Waters is her fifth novel and Booker Prize shortlisted — a slow-building, formally controlled Gothic horror novel set in Warwickshire in 1947-48, about Dr. Faraday, a country doctor who becomes involved with the Ayres family and their crumbling country house, Hundreds Hall.\n\nThe novel is narrated entirely by Dr. Faraday, whose reliability as a narrator is one of the novel's central uncertainties: he describes a series of strange events at Hundreds Hall — events that might be supernatural, or might be explained as the psychological disturbance of the Ayres family members — in a prose of utter flatness and matter-of-factness that is itself deeply unsettling.\n\nThe Little Stranger belongs alongside Daphne du Maurier's Rebecca and Shirley Jackson's The Haunting of Hill House in the tradition of the great English and American Gothic novel about a house that seems to have malevolent intentions. It is also a novel about the specific social moment of postwar England — the decline of the great houses, the dissolution of the old class structure, the specific atmosphere of austerity and cultural exhaustion — and about the specific figure of the outsider who is both attracted to and resentful of the world he is observing.",
    startWith: "The Little Stranger can be read independently as an introduction to Waters's Gothic work. Any Virago or Riverhead edition works. Long (466 pages) but the atmosphere is established immediately.",
