    slug: 'folding-star-hollinghurst',
    intro: "The Folding Star (1994) by Alan Hollinghurst is his second novel — a novel about Edward Manners, an English teacher in his early thirties who moves to a Belgian city to teach English and becomes obsessed with one of his students, a seventeen-year-old boy named Luc Altidore.\n\nThe novel is also about the city itself and the story of Edgard Orst, a fictional Symbolist painter who had a complex relationship with his own model, a boy named Luc — a story that Edward discovers through his work as a volunteer at the local museum. The parallel between Edward's obsession with his Luc and Orst's obsession with his Luc is the novel's formal conceit, and it uses the Symbolist painter's world to explore the relationship between art and obsession, between desire and its object.\n\nThe Folding Star belongs alongside The Swimming-Pool Library in the tradition of Hollinghurst's formally ambitious, psychologically intense explorations of gay male desire. It was shortlisted for the Booker Prize and shows Hollinghurst's developing mastery of the literary historical novel — his ability to render a foreign world (Belgium, the Symbolist movement) with the same precision he brings to the English world of his debut.",
