    intro: "The Spell (1998) by Alan Hollinghurst is his third novel — a shorter, lighter, and more purely comic novel than his first two, about four men in various states of romantic entanglement: Robin Woodfield, a middle-aged architect; his son Danny; Alex, Robin's former lover; and Justin, Danny's new boyfriend.\n\nThe novel is set in the 1990s and moves between London and a rented house in Dorset, and it is organized around the specific comedies of gay romantic life — the ex-lovers who remain friends, the new relationships that complicate old ones, the specific dynamics of a community where everyone seems to be connected to everyone else through chains of desire and ex-desire.\n\nThe Spell is Hollinghurst's most explicitly comic and least historically ambitious novel — it is the novel in which he is most purely a novelist of manners, in the tradition of Jane Austen and E.M. Forster, rather than a historical novelist or a social critic. It belongs alongside his other novels but shows a different face of his talent: the ability to render the specific comedy of social and romantic dynamics with precision and wit.",
