    slug: 'stranger-children-hollinghurst',
    intro: "The Stranger's Child (2011) by Alan Hollinghurst is his fifth novel — a large, formally ambitious novel that follows two families over a century of English social history (1913-2008), organized around the figure of Cecil Valance, a minor Edwardian poet who visits a suburban house in 1913 and leaves behind a poem, 'Two Acres,' that becomes famous after his death in the First World War.\n\nThe novel is organized in five sections, each set in a different decade, following the legacy of Valance's poem and the ways in which the myth of Valance himself is constructed and reconstructed by the people who knew him, studied him, and made careers from him. It is a novel about the literary biography industry, about the way that myths are made, about the specific social dynamics of each decade through which the story passes.\n\nThe Stranger's Child belongs alongside Hilary Mantel's Wolf Hall and A.S. Byatt's Possession in the tradition of the English literary novel that uses historical research and a long temporal span to explore how the past is constructed and reconstructed in the present. It is Hollinghurst's most formally ambitious novel and one of the outstanding English novels of the century so far.",
