    slug: 'colour-tremain',
    intro: "The Colour (2003) by Rose Tremain is her eighth novel — a large historical novel set in New Zealand in the 1860s during the gold rush, about Joseph Blackstone, an Englishman who has emigrated to New Zealand with his wife Harriet and his mother Lilian, and who is infected by the gold fever that sweeps through the settlements.\n\nThe novel follows Joseph's obsession with gold — his eventual abandonment of his wife and farm to go prospecting — and Harriet's response to being left, and it is also a novel about the specific experience of English emigration to New Zealand and the collision between English settlers and the Maori landscape and culture. The title refers to the colour of gold in water — the specific shimmer that gold has when it is found in a river — and to the way that gold fever is a kind of colour blindness that makes everything that is not gold invisible.\n\nThe Colour belongs alongside Tremain's Restoration and Music and Silence in the tradition of her large, researched, emotionally ambitious historical novels. It is one of her most fully realized novels and one of the most important English literary novels about the New Zealand experience.",
