    slug: 'trespass-tremain',
    intro: "Trespass (2010) by Rose Tremain is her tenth novel — a dark, intense novel set in the Cévennes region of southern France, about two parallel stories that eventually collide: Veronica and Anthony Verey, an English brother and sister who move to France — Veronica settling permanently, Anthony arriving to rest after a breakdown — and Aramon Lunel, a local farmer whose half-sister Audrun has been damaged by his cruelty and who is waiting for a reckoning.\n\nThe novel is about inheritance, violence, and the specific dynamics of families that have damaged their members — and it uses the specific landscape of the Cévennes (rocky, ancient, harsh) as a counterpart to the psychological landscape of its characters. The two stories — the Vereys and the Lunels — are brought into contact when Anthony wants to buy the old farmhouse that Aramon owns, and the novel's convergence has a quality of the inevitable.\n\nTrespass belongs alongside Tremain's Restoration and Sacred Country in the tradition of her novels that combine atmospheric setting with an unflinching examination of the damage that families do to their members. It is one of her darkest novels and one of her most formally controlled.",
    startWith: "Trespass can be read independently or after Restoration and Sacred Country. Any Chatto & Windus or Vintage edition works. Moderate length (278 pages) and immediately atmospheric.",
