    slug: 'merivel-tremain',
    intro: "Merivel: A Man of His Time (2012) by Rose Tremain is her eleventh novel and a sequel to her breakthrough Restoration (1989) — following Robert Merivel twenty years later, in the early 1680s, as he is now settled in his own estate, his daughter Margaret is growing up, and he is facing the late challenges of his life with the same combination of folly and goodness that characterized the earlier novel.\n\nThe novel is specifically a novel about old age — about what it means to be a person of a certain age who still has desires, who still makes mistakes, who is still capable of being surprised by what life offers. Merivel travels to France and to Switzerland, encounters the court of Louis XIV, and falls in love — in ways that are both comic and moving — with an aristocratic woman.\n\nMerivel: A Man of His Time belongs alongside Restoration in the tradition of Tremain's great comic historical fiction. It is in some ways a different kind of novel from its predecessor — darker, more meditative, more concerned with mortality — but it has the same warmth, the same intelligence, and the same generosity toward its characters that make Restoration such a pleasure to read.",
    startWith: "Merivel should be read after Restoration — it is a sequel and presupposes knowledge of the earlier novel. Any Chatto & Windus or Vintage edition works. Long (320 pages) but propulsive.",
