    slug: 'innocence-fitzgerald',
    intro: "Innocence (1986) by Penelope Fitzgerald is her sixth novel — a romantic comedy and social novel set in 1950s Florence, about the aristocratic Ridolfi family — specifically the young Chiara Ridolfi and her father Giancarlo — and their encounter with Salvatore Rossi, a doctor from the south of Italy who falls in love with Chiara.\n\nThe novel is about the collision between the Ridolfi family (Florentine aristocracy, slightly impoverished, living on the residue of their history) and Salvatore (southern Italian, self-made, fiercely proud, Communist in his politics) — a collision that generates both comedy and tragedy, and that Fitzgerald uses to explore the specific social landscape of Italy in the aftermath of World War II and the beginning of the economic miracle.\n\nInnocence is Fitzgerald's most romantically ambitious novel — it is her fullest engagement with the comedy and drama of love across social barriers — and it is also her most socially and historically complex. It belongs alongside E.M. Forster's A Room with a View in the tradition of the English literary novel about Italy and the English (or in this case, more fully Italian) comic romance.",
