    slug: 'blue-flower-fitzgerald',
    intro: "The Blue Flower (1995) by Penelope Fitzgerald is her final and most celebrated novel — a short, astonishing historical novel about Friedrich von Hardenberg, the German Romantic poet known as Novalis, and his love for Sophie von Kühn, a twelve-year-old girl who dies at fifteen before they can marry.\n\nThe novel is set in Germany in the 1790s, in the world of the early German Romantics — of Schiller and Goethe, of the philosophers and poets who were creating the Romantic movement that would transform European culture — and it renders this world with an economy and precision that seems miraculous given how foreign it is to contemporary English fiction. It is also a love story of a very strange kind: Hardenberg's love for Sophie is not easy to understand, since Sophie is a child, unintellectual, and in no obvious way a suitable object for a brilliant poet's devotion, and the novel refuses to explain it in conventional psychological terms.\n\nThe Blue Flower won the National Book Critics Circle Award and has been repeatedly cited as one of the finest English novels of the twentieth century. It is Fitzgerald's masterpiece.",
    startWith: "The Blue Flower is best read after The Bookshop and Offshore as Fitzgerald's culminating achievement, or it can be read first as an introduction. Any HarperCollins or Mariner edition works. Short (225 pages) but requires attention — the historical world is unfamiliar.",
