    slug: 'restoration-tremain',
    intro: "Restoration (1989) by Rose Tremain is her fifth novel and the breakthrough that made her international reputation — a large, entertaining historical novel set in the reign of Charles II, about Robert Merivel, a physician who becomes a court favourite of the king, is given a royal estate and made to marry one of the king's mistresses (on condition that he never actually love her), loses everything, and begins the long road of self-discovery that forms the novel's second half.\n\nThe novel is one of the great English historical novels of the twentieth century — brilliantly researched, written in a prose that captures the energy and excess of the Restoration court without being merely pastiche, and organized around a protagonist who is both comic and deeply moving. Merivel is a figure of a specific kind of English comedy: the good-natured fool who gets everything he wants and then has to learn what he actually needs.\n\nRestoration belongs alongside Hilary Mantel's Wolf Hall and John Fowles's The French Lieutenant's Woman in the tradition of the great English historical novel of the contemporary period that combines scholarly research with literary ambition and narrative energy. It is one of the most enjoyable serious English novels of recent decades.",
    startWith: "Restoration is an excellent standalone introduction to Rose Tremain. Any Sceptre or Vintage edition works. Long (375 pages) but propulsive — Merivel's voice makes the pages turn.",
