    intro: "The Road Home (2007) by Rose Tremain is her ninth novel and winner of the Orange Prize for Fiction — a large, warm, observant novel about Lev, a man from an unnamed Eastern European country who has come to England to find work after the post-Communist economic collapse, leaving behind his mother, his young daughter Maya, and the village where he has lived his whole life.\n\nThe novel follows Lev's first year in England — his journey from his village to London, his experiences working as a kitchen hand in a fashionable London restaurant, his relationships with the English people he meets (his landlady Lydia, his employer GK Ashe, his lover Sophie), and his gradual accommodation to the specific challenges of his position as a migrant worker in early twenty-first century Britain.\n\nThe Road Home belongs alongside Monica Ali's Brick Lane and Zadie Smith's White Teeth in the tradition of the English literary novel about the experience of immigration and the specific texture of early twenty-first century multicultural Britain. It is one of Tremain's most directly social and most humanely observed novels, and it won the Orange Prize deservedly.",
