    slug: 'bookshop-fitzgerald',
    intro: "The Bookshop (1978) by Penelope Fitzgerald is her second novel and the book that first brought her significant attention — a short, perfectly controlled novel about Florence Green, a widow who in 1959 decides to open a bookshop in the small Suffolk coastal town of Hardborough.\n\nThe novel is about the resistance that Florence's enterprise encounters from the local community — particularly from Violet Gamart, a socially powerful woman who wants the building for an arts center and who methodically undermines Florence until she succeeds in driving her out. It is a novel about the specific cruelty of small-town English society, about the way that social power operates through seemingly legitimate channels, and about the persistence and ultimate defeat of the individual who does not understand that the community does not want what she is offering.\n\nThe Bookshop belongs alongside Barbara Pym's novels and Elizabeth Taylor's domestic fiction in the tradition of the short, precise English literary novel that uses a small social setting to examine large questions about power, conformity, and the cost of independence. It is one of the great short English novels of the twentieth century, and it is the best introduction to Fitzgerald's particular genius for compression and for the implicit.",
