    slug: 'gate-angels-fitzgerald',
    intro: "The Gate of Angels (1990) by Penelope Fitzgerald is her eighth novel — a short, luminous, and deeply strange novel set in Cambridge in 1912, about Fred Fairly, a junior fellow in physics at St. Angelicus College, who becomes involved with Daisy Saunders, a young nurse who has recently survived a bicycle accident that has left her in a mysterious state between life and death.\n\nThe novel is set at the moment when physics is moving from the classical Newtonian understanding to the quantum world that will make classical physics obsolete, and Fred's professional world (the exact sciences, the rational, the male-only Cambridge of 1912) is colliding with Daisy's world (the female, the intuitive, the world of nursing and of illness and of the body) in ways that the novel uses to explore the limits of scientific explanation.\n\nThe Gate of Angels belongs alongside Offshore and The Bookshop in the small canon of Fitzgerald's compressed masterworks, and it shows her at her most historically and intellectually ambitious. It is a novel about the uncanny, about the limits of what science can explain, about love as something that defies rational explanation, and about the specific historical moment — 1912, the eve of the First World War — when the European culture of certainty was about to be destroyed.",
    startWith: "The Gate of Angels is best read after The Bookshop and Offshore as a third Fitzgerald. Any HarperCollins or Mariner edition works. Short (160 pages) and immediately readable despite its historical and scientific setting.",
