    slug: 'human-voices-fitzgerald',
    intro: "Human Voices (1980) by Penelope Fitzgerald is her fourth novel — a short, sharp, funny novel set in the BBC during the Blitz in 1940, drawing on Fitzgerald's own experience working at the BBC during the war.\n\nThe novel is about the specific culture of the BBC in wartime — its combination of high-minded public service culture, bureaucratic absurdity, and the specific drama of running a broadcasting organization while London is being bombed — and about the people who work there: the Recorded Programmes Director Sam Brooks, the Assistant Director of Programme Planning Jeff Haggard, and a series of young women who work in various capacities and who are the subjects of the various romantic complications that the novel follows.\n\nHuman Voices belongs alongside Evelyn Waugh's Sword of Honour trilogy and Henry Green's Caught in the tradition of the English comic and literary novel about the specific experience of the wartime institutions — the BBC, the army, the civil service — that organized British life during the Second World War. It is Fitzgerald's funniest novel and one of her most observationally precise.",
