    slug: 'beginning-spring-fitzgerald',
    intro: "The Beginning of Spring (1988) by Penelope Fitzgerald is her seventh novel and one of her most beloved — a short, strange, beautiful novel set in Moscow in 1913, about Frank Reid, an Englishman born in Moscow who runs a printing business and whose wife Nellie has inexplicably returned to England, leaving him with their three children and no explanation.\n\nThe novel is set in the specific social world of English people who have settled in pre-revolutionary Moscow — expatriates who have adapted to Russian life without entirely becoming Russian, who occupy an intermediate cultural position that is part of Fitzgerald's characteristic concern with people who belong fully to neither of two worlds. It is set in 1913, just before the revolution that will destroy the world the novel describes, and this historical positioning gives everything in it an additional resonance of impermanence.\n\nThe Beginning of Spring belongs alongside Offshore and The Gate of Angels in the small canon of Fitzgerald's compressed masterworks, and it is the novel that most fully demonstrates her ability to render an unfamiliar world — imperial Russia — with economy and precision, without either exoticizing it or domesticating it completely. It is also one of the finest English novels about Russia.",
