    slug: 'anti-death-league-amis',
    intro: "The Anti-Death League (1966) by Kingsley Amis is one of his most serious and least comic novels — a novel about a British army unit preparing for a secret operation, and about several of its officers' encounters with love, death, and the possible existence of God.\n\nThe novel follows James Churchill, an officer who falls in love with a young woman named Catharine Casement who has a terminal illness, and several of his fellow officers, including Max Hunter, a psychiatrist, and Alastair Ross, a cynical intelligence officer. The army context is rendered with procedural precision, and the novel is serious about military life in a way that Amis's other novels, which tend to treat institutional life as primarily comic, are not.\n\nThe Anti-Death League is Amis's most theologically engaged novel before The Green Man and his most emotionally earnest — it deals with the cruelty of random death, the question of whether God exists and is indifferent or malevolent, and the possibility of love in the face of terminal illness, all without the comic deflections that characterize his other fiction. It is one of his least-read novels and one of the most rewarding.",
    startWith: "The Anti-Death League stands alone and is best read after Lucky Jim and The Old Devils for context. Any Jonathan Cape, Vintage, or Penguin edition works. Moderate length (287 pages) and rewarding — the novel's unusual emotional seriousness is apparent from the opening chapters.",
    books: [
      { title: "The Anti-Death League", author: "Kingsley Amis", year: 1966, note: "Amis's most serious emotionally earnest novel — British army unit secret operation; James Churchill officer; Catharine Casement terminal illness love; Max Hunter psychiatrist; Alastair Ross intelligence officer; military life procedural precision; theological novel God indifference malevolence; random death cruelty; Jonathan Cape Vintage Penguin; 287 pages; Green Man comparison theologically engaged; least-read most rewarding." },
      { title: "The Green Man", author: "Kingsley Amis", year: 1969, note: "Amis's companion — the companion novel about God and death, approached through the ghost-story genre rather than the serious military novel." },
      { title: "Lucky Jim", author: "Kingsley Amis", year: 1954, note: "Amis's companion debut masterwork — the foundational comic novel; the companion that shows how different The Anti-Death League is from Amis's usual mode." },
      { title: "The Old Devils", author: "Kingsley Amis", year: 1986, note: "Amis's companion Booker Prize late masterwork — the companion that shows his most fully realized engagement with mortality and the approach of death." },
      { title: "From Here to Eternity", author: "James Jones", year: 1951, note: "Jones's companion — the great American novel about the prewar U.S. Army; the companion in the tradition of the literary novel that treats military life with procedural precision and emotional seriousness." },
      { title: "The Charterhouse of Parma", author: "Stendhal", year: 1839, note: "Stendhal's companion — Fabrizio among armies and aristocrats; the companion in the tradition of the literary novel about a young man finding love and meaning against a backdrop of military and political life." },
      { title: "The Death of Ivan Ilyich", author: "Leo Tolstoy", year: 1886, note: "Tolstoy's companion — the great short novel about dying; the companion that Amis's treatment of Catharine's terminal illness is in dialogue with." },
      { title: "Love Story", author: "Erich Segal", year: 1970, note: "Segal's companion — the short American novel about a young man's love for a woman who dies; the companion in the popular tradition of the love-and-terminal-illness story that The Anti-Death League treats in a literary mode." },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: "What is the secret operation?", a: "The army unit in The Anti-Death League is preparing for Operation Apollo — a secret military operation whose details are gradually revealed over the course of the novel. The operation's secret is partly a plot device and partly thematic: the novel is interested in the relationship between secrecy, institutional authority, and individual knowledge, and the gradual revelation of what Apollo involves is connected to the characters' gradual understanding of their own situations and of the larger questions the novel raises about death and meaning." },
      { q: "What is the theological question?", a: "The Anti-Death League is Amis's most direct engagement with the question of God before The Green Man — and his engagement here is angry rather than sardonic. The novel's title refers to a loose, informal protest against death itself: the sense that the random cruelty of terminal illness (and of the other deaths that occur in the novel) is evidence not of a benevolent or indifferent God but of a malevolent one. Several characters in the novel wrestle with the question of why, if God exists, the world contains the specific cruelties it does — including Catharine's illness, which has no narrative justification and exists simply as the kind of thing that happens." },
      { q: "Who is Catharine Casement?", a: "Catharine Casement is the young woman that James Churchill falls in love with — and her terminal illness is the novel's emotional center. Catharine is drawn with unusual sympathy and specificity: she is not a symbol or a plot device but a person, and her illness is depicted without sentimentality or consolation. The love between James and Catharine is one of the most emotionally serious relationships in Amis's fiction, and the novel's treatment of it is notable precisely for its refusal of the comic deflections that Amis usually employs when dealing with romantic emotion." },
      { q: "How does this novel compare to Amis's other work?", a: "The Anti-Death League is the most emotionally earnest of Amis's novels and the one most unlike his usual mode. Lucky Jim, That Uncertain Feeling, Take a Girl Like You, and most of his other fiction use sardonic comedy as their primary register; The Anti-Death League is primarily serious, and its moments of comedy feel like brief reliefs rather than the dominant mode. Readers who know Amis primarily through his comedies often find the novel a revelation — evidence of a writer capable of a different and more demanding register than his reputation suggests." },
      { q: "What should I read after The Anti-Death League?", a: "After The Anti-Death League, The Green Man by Amis is the companion novel about God and death approached through the ghost-story genre. The Old Devils by Amis is the companion late masterwork engaging with mortality. Lucky Jim by Amis is the companion debut that shows the opposite of this novel's emotional register. The Death of Ivan Ilyich by Tolstoy is the great companion short novel about dying. From Here to Eternity by Jones is the companion great American military novel with emotional seriousness. The Charterhouse of Parma by Stendhal is the companion literary novel about a young man finding love against a military backdrop." },
    ],
