    slug: 'buchi-emecheta-second-class',
    intro: "Second-Class Citizen (1974) by Buchi Emecheta is one of the most powerful and direct of African women's novels — a thinly veiled autobiographical novel about Adah Obi, a young Nigerian Igbo woman who marries Francis, emigrates to England with him in the early 1960s, and discovers that in England she is twice diminished: as a Black person in a racially hostile society and as a woman in a marriage in which her husband exercises absolute authority while she earns the money.\n\nEmecheta was born in Lagos in 1944 and emigrated to England in 1962 to join her student husband; by 1966, with five children and a husband who burned her manuscript, she had left him and was on her own in London. Second-Class Citizen is the novel she wrote from this experience, and it is both a devastating personal document and a social analysis of the specific intersection of racial and gender oppression that defined the experience of the West African woman immigrant to Britain in the 1960s.\n\nAdah is an extraordinary character — resourceful, intelligent, determined, and deeply honest about her own complicity in the marriage that has trapped her and her own desires for the life that she cannot quite manage to live. The novel's power comes partly from this self-honesty: Emecheta does not present Adah simply as a victim but as an agent whose agency is systematically undermined by the structures she inhabits, and who is gradually, painfully learning to understand those structures and to resist them.",
    startWith: "Second-Class Citizen is Emecheta's essential autobiographical novel. The Braziller or Heinemann edition works. Short (175 pages) and immediately engaging — best read alongside The Joys of Motherhood for the full portrait.",
