    faq: [
      { q: "What is Eight Men about?", a: "Eight Men is a collection of eight stories spanning Wright's career from the early 1930s to the late 1950s. The stories vary in setting and mode: some are set in the rural South of his childhood, some in the urban North, one in a foreign country. The most celebrated is 'The Man Who Lived Underground,' a novella about Fred Daniels, a Black man who is coerced by police into confessing to a murder he did not commit and who hides in the sewers beneath a city, from where he observes the surface world with a terrible, estranged clarity. Other notable stories include 'The Man Who Was Almost a Man' (a teenage boy in the rural South who accidentally kills a mule with the gun he has bought to prove his manhood) and 'Big Black Good Man' (about a large Black sailor and the white Danish hotel clerk whose fear of him reveals the unconscious racism beneath his self-image as a tolerant man)." },
      { q: "What is 'The Man Who Lived Underground' about?", a: "'The Man Who Lived Underground' is a novella about Fred Daniels, a Black man who is beaten by police until he confesses to a murder he did not commit. He escapes into the sewer system beneath the city and spends several days underground, living in the sewers and observing the surface world through gratings: a church service, a movie theater, a butcher's shop, a radio store, a jewelry store that he breaks into. Underground, he develops a strange, estranged relationship to money, to religion, to the rhythms of the world above. When he eventually tries to return to the surface and to confess his innocence, he is shot by the police. The story is Wright's most formally experimental and philosophically ambitious work, showing the influence of the existentialism he encountered in Paris." },
      { q: "Why did Wright live in Paris?", a: "Richard Wright moved to Paris in 1947, partly because of the racial violence and discrimination he faced in the United States and partly because of his friendship with the French existentialists — Jean-Paul Sartre, Simone de Beauvoir, and Albert Camus — who welcomed him as a major American writer. In Paris, Wright was free from the racial restrictions of American life, and he engaged deeply with existentialist philosophy, which shaped his later work including The Outsider (1953) and the stories in Eight Men. He died in Paris in 1960, and Eight Men was published posthumously in 1961. Wright's Paris years are less studied than his American years, but his late work shows a significant philosophical development beyond the naturalism of Native Son." },
      { q: "How does Eight Men compare to Uncle Tom's Children?", a: "Uncle Tom's Children (1938) and Eight Men (1961) represent the beginning and the end of Wright's short fiction career, and the comparison shows how far his thinking evolved over the decades. Uncle Tom's Children is a relatively unified collection — all five stories are about racial violence in the Deep South, and all are written in a naturalist mode. Eight Men is more various: the stories span several decades and several modes, from the naturalist Southern stories to the Kafkaesque 'Man Who Lived Underground' to the more philosophical stories of the Paris years. The later collection shows Wright's engagement with existentialism and with a more universal philosophy of consciousness, while the earlier collection shows his most direct confrontation with American racial violence." },
      { q: "What should I read after Eight Men?", a: "After Eight Men, Native Son (1940) is Wright's masterpiece and the essential companion. Uncle Tom's Children (1938) is the debut collection. Black Boy (1945) is his autobiography. The Outsider (1953) is the companion novel for readers interested in Wright's existentialist period. For short fiction in the same tradition of African American social realism, Ernest Gaines's collection Bloodline is an important companion." },
    ],
