    slug: 'olive-again-strout',
    intro: "Olive, Again (2019) by Elizabeth Strout is the companion to Olive Kitteridge — returning to Crosby, Maine and to Olive Kitteridge in her late 70s and early 80s, in the years after Henry's death and after her unexpected second marriage to the lawyer Jack Kennison. The novel in stories follows Olive and the Crosby community through the next stage of life: old age, the deaths of contemporaries, the continuing difficulty of the relationship with her son Christopher, and the new relationship with Jack, who is difficult in a different way from the way Olive is difficult.\n\nOlive, Again was published eleven years after Olive Kitteridge and confirms that Olive's later life has as much going on as her earlier life — she is not softened by age but she is deepened, and the novel traces her belated recognitions more explicitly than the original. The book is also formally similar to its predecessor: linked stories set in Crosby, each with its own cast of characters but all circling Olive and the passage of time in the community. Readers who loved Olive Kitteridge will find Olive, Again as emotionally intelligent and as precisely observed as the original.",
