    faq: [
      { q: "What is The Autobiography of an Ex-Colored Man about?", a: "The Autobiography of an Ex-Colored Man follows an unnamed mixed-race narrator who discovers his Black identity in childhood and moves through Black and white American society with relative freedom because of his light skin. He works in a cigar factory, becomes a ragtime pianist, travels to Europe as the companion of a wealthy white man, returns to the South to document Black folk music, and then — after witnessing a lynching — permanently adopts a white identity and becomes a successful white businessman. The novel ends with the narrator's acknowledgment that he has traded his birthright for a mess of pottage: his white success has cost him membership in what he now recognizes as 'a desperately great people.'" },
      { q: "Was The Autobiography of an Ex-Colored Man a real autobiography?", a: "The Autobiography of an Ex-Colored Man was published anonymously in 1912 and was initially received by many readers as a genuine memoir — evidence of the quality of Johnson's mimicry of autobiographical form and of the cultural plausibility of the story. Johnson revealed his authorship in 1927, when the novel was republished under his name. The novel's use of first-person anonymous confession and its realistic detail (particularly its account of Black musical culture and Southern racial violence) contributed to this confusion. Johnson used the ambiguity deliberately: by presenting the novel as potentially real, he forced readers to engage with its account of racial passing and racial shame as potential testimony rather than fiction." },
      { q: "What is the significance of ragtime in the novel?", a: "Ragtime is central to The Autobiography of an Ex-Colored Man as both subject and symbol. The narrator becomes a skilled ragtime pianist — Johnson himself was a Tin Pan Alley songwriter and understood the music deeply — and the novel's account of Black musical creativity (ragtime, spirituals, the folk songs the narrator goes South to document) is one of the richest in American literature. Ragtime also functions as the thing the narrator abandons when he chooses to pass: African American music is the cultural expression of the community he is turning away from, and his talent for it is the specific gift he is sacrificing. Johnson uses the narrator's musical gifts to make concrete what abstract appeals to 'racial heritage' might leave vague." },
      { q: "How does The Autobiography of an Ex-Colored Man relate to the tradition of the passing novel?", a: "The Autobiography of an Ex-Colored Man is the foundational text of the American passing novel — the tradition of fiction about African Americans who choose to live as white. Nella Larsen's Passing (1929) and Quicksand (1928) are the most important successors; more recent examples include Philip Roth's The Human Stain (2000) and Danzy Senna's Caucasia (1998). The passing novel engages the American racial system at its most absurd: if race is a biological fact, why does it need to be policed? If it is a social construction, what is the narrator passing as? Johnson uses the genre to expose the arbitrariness of the color line and its human cost — not just for those who pass, but for the communities they leave." },
      { q: "What should I read after The Autobiography of an Ex-Colored Man?", a: "After The Autobiography of an Ex-Colored Man, the immediate successors in the passing tradition are Nella Larsen's Quicksand (1928) and Passing (1929), both of which take up Johnson's themes with greater modernist psychological intensity. W.E.B. Du Bois's The Souls of Black Folk (1903) provides the intellectual context — the concept of double consciousness that the narrator's experience dramatizes. For the broader Harlem Renaissance context, Alain Locke's anthology The New Negro (1925) and Langston Hughes's poetry (The Weary Blues, 1926) are essential. Ralph Ellison's Invisible Man (1952) is the great mid-century extension of Johnson's project: the Surrealist version of what the unnamed narrator of The Autobiography of an Ex-Colored Man underwent in realist form." },
    ],
