    slug: 'el-llano-en-llamas-rulfo',
    intro: "El Llano en llamas (The Burning Plain, 1953) by Juan Rulfo is the short story collection that established the model for all subsequent Latin American fiction: seventeen stories set in the Jalisco countryside in a prose of absolute economy, where the dead and the living share the same landscape without distinction, where violence has no special emphasis, and where the land itself is a moral condition. Rulfo published only two books — this collection and Pedro Páramo (1955) — and together they represent the most concentrated and influential achievement in twentieth-century Spanish-language fiction.\n\nThe stories of El Llano en llamas are spare in a way that no subsequent summary or description quite captures: 'We were given Luvina' opens by establishing that Luvina is a town in the mountains where only the wind moves, and the story is entirely the account of one man telling another what Luvina is like before the second man goes there. 'Diles que no me maten' (Tell Them Not to Kill Me) is six pages in which a man who killed someone thirty-five years ago is about to be killed by that person's son, now a colonel. The violence is narrated as matter-of-factly as the landscape. The specific quality of Rulfo's prose — its flatness, its absence of emotional emphasis, its refusal of consolation — was entirely new in Spanish-language fiction and became the foundation on which García Márquez built magical realism, Fuentes built his historical novels, and Bolaño built his savage irony.",
