    slug: 'el-obsceno-pajaro-donoso',
    intro: "El obsceno pájaro de la noche (The Obscene Bird of Night, 1970) by José Donoso is one of the most formally radical novels in Latin American literature — a baroque nightmare of shifting identities, hallucinations, and monstrous bodies narrated by Humberto Peñaloza, a writer who has become a deaf-mute caretaker in an old women's retreat, and who may be the same person as the narrator of a manuscript he is supposedly writing, or may not be. The novel is set in a Chile that is simultaneously realistic and nightmarish: an aristocratic family's corrupt estate, an orphanage of deformed children, the ruins of a convent, and a world in which the division between the real and the hallucinatory has entirely broken down.\n\nDonoso was the Chilean member of the Boom generation — García Márquez, Vargas Llosa, Fuentes, Cortázar — but The Obscene Bird of Night is the darkest and most formally extreme of the Boom novels: where García Márquez uses magic to celebrate, and Cortázar uses formal experiment to create wonder, Donoso uses both to explore a world of absolute social degradation, religious corruption, and physical monstrosity. The novel's Chile is the Chile of the Chilean aristocracy in decline — the same world Donoso grew up in — and his treatment of it is grotesque, obsessive, and magnificent. The title comes from a Chilean folk belief: the obscene bird of night is the widower bird (chucao), whose call is an omen of death.",
