    slug: 'suenos-quevedo',
    intro: "Los Sueños (The Dreams, 1627 — written 1605–22) by Francisco de Quevedo are five satirical dream-visions in which Quevedo descends into Hell or witnesses the Last Judgment and encounters the great hypocrites, pretenders, and fools of Spanish society: venal doctors, corrupt judges, vain noblewomen, foppish courtiers, astrologers who cannot predict their own deaths. The satire is savage and magnificent — Quevedo is the greatest prose satirist of the Spanish Golden Age, and the Sueños are his most concentrated showcase: short, formally tight, linguistically dazzling prose fictions that operate simultaneously as social satire, theological comedy, and virtuoso language performance.\n\nQuevedo's language is the point: he is the master of conceptismo, the Spanish Baroque mode of dense conceptual wit in which every sentence is loaded with puns, paradoxes, syllepses, and conceits. A doctor in Hell is condemned to the deaths of all his patients, reversed — he dies forever from the diseases he failed to cure. A nobleman's coat of arms is revealed as heraldic fraud. An astrologer arrives at the Last Judgment having not predicted it. The humor is dark and exact, and the theological framework (hell, purgatory, judgment) is handled with a dryness that gives the satire its particular Quevedian edge — the cosmos is Quevedo's comic backdrop. The Sueños were enormously influential on Swift, Voltaire, and the whole European tradition of dream-vision satire.",
    startWith: "Sueño del infierno (Dream of Hell) — the longest and most fully developed of the five, and the best entry to Quevedo's satirical world.",
