    slug: 'l-oeuvre-au-noir-yourcenar',
    intro: "L'Œuvre au noir (The Abyss, 1968) by Marguerite Yourcenar won the Prix Femina and is her most ambitious historical novel after Mémoires d'Hadrien. Set in the sixteenth century across the religious wars that tore Europe apart — from the Low Countries to France, Germany, and the Mediterranean — it follows Zeno, a physician, philosopher, and alchemist, across a life lived in the shadow of the Inquisition and the Wars of Religion. Zeno is one of those extraordinary Renaissance figures who combined medical science, philosophical speculation, and esoteric learning in ways that made them permanently vulnerable to the religious authorities of their time — a doctor who performs dissections, a thinker who questions the tenets of both Christianity and emerging Protestantism, a man who is constitutionally incapable of limiting himself to what the age permits. The title comes from the alchemical term for the first stage of the Great Work — the blackening, or 'nigredo', in which the substance to be transformed is reduced to its essential blackness before it can be reconstituted. Zeno's life is described as this kind of 'work in the abyss': a sustained refusal of the comfortable positions available to him, a commitment to truth as he can discover it, and an eventual confrontation with the Inquisition that demands the same honesty under conditions that make it fatal. L'Œuvre au noir is a magnificent novel of historical scope and intellectual depth.",
