    slug: 'alexis-yourcenar',
    intro: "Alexis (1929) by Marguerite Yourcenar is the debut novel that launched one of the most distinguished careers in twentieth-century French literature. Written when Yourcenar was twenty-six, it is a brief, formally perfect epistolary novel: a letter from a musician named Alexis to Monique, the wife he is leaving, explaining why he cannot continue to live with her and their child. The explanation is the gradual, circling, deeply honest account of a homosexual man who has tried to live within the social and marital forms available to him and who has come to understand that this is a form of living death — not for want of affection for Monique, whom he loves and respects, but because the life he shares with her is not the life he is capable of living. Alexis draws explicitly on Rilke's Letters to a Young Poet in its style and on André Gide's L'Immoraliste in its subject — both acknowledged influences — but the voice Yourcenar achieves is entirely her own: measured, scrupulous, extraordinarily attentive to the nuance of a consciousness that is simultaneously honest with itself and unable to be fully honest with the person it is addressing. Alexis is a masterpiece of the epistolary form and one of the most beautiful short novels in French literature.",
