    slug: 'le-voyeur-robbe-grillet',
    intro: "Le Voyeur (The Voyeur, 1955) by Alain Robbe-Grillet won the Prix des Critiques and confirmed his position as the leading figure of the Nouveau Roman. Mathias, a travelling watch salesman, arrives by ferry at an island where he grew up and spends a day attempting to sell watches to the islanders. Somewhere in the middle of his rounds, a young girl named Jacqueline is murdered. Mathias may have killed her. The novel's most extraordinary formal feature is the gap in its narrative — a section of several hours during which the murder may or may not have occurred — which Robbe-Grillet renders not as a blank space but as an over-detailed description of what surrounds the gap: the objects Mathias sees before and after, the precise itinerary of his rounds with its impossible timing, the way he looks at the wall and the sea and the cigarette ash. The reader reconstructs the crime (or its absence) from these surrounding details, the way a forensic investigator would reconstruct a scene from physical evidence. Le Voyeur is a more concentrated formal experiment than Les Gommes — there is less plot and more pure Robbe-Grillet technique — and it remains one of the most formally innovative novels in French literature.",
