    slug: 'passage-de-milan-butor',
    intro: "Passage de Milan (1954) by Michel Butor is the debut that announced one of the most formally inventive careers in post-war French literature. A single night in a Parisian apartment building — the novel begins at seven in the evening and ends at dawn — unfolds through the consciousness and conversations of its various inhabitants: a retired missionary on the top floor, a young Egyptian woman on the first, a priest who plays the organ in the church across the street, families across several floors, the guests arriving for a party on the seventh floor. The party is the novel's centrepiece and its occasion for disaster: a young woman falls from a window (or is pushed), and the novel's second half traces the effect of this event on the building's community, the questions it raises, the silences it creates, and the way each inhabitant's response reveals their character and their position in the building's social geography. The title comes from a hawk (a Milan in French) that opens the novel by landing on the building's roof at dusk — a predatory bird that oversees the human drama below, returning at dawn when the novel ends. Passage de Milan is more conventional in its structure than the later Butor novels, but it already shows the interest in collective social space and the single night or day as a structural unit that would become central to the mature work.",
