    slug: 'la-route-des-flandres-simon',
    intro: "La Route des Flandres (The Flanders Road, 1960) by Claude Simon is the novel that established Simon as one of the major figures of the Nouveau Roman and the work for which he is still most widely read. It is a war novel unlike any other: three men — Georges, his cousin and commanding officer de Reixach, and a horse-racing jockey named Iglesia — are retreating through the Flemish countryside in the catastrophic collapse of the French army in May 1940. De Reixach is shot from his horse by a sniper, and the question of whether his death was suicide (a deliberate exposure to the sniper's bullet) haunts the novel's narrator Georges across a POW camp and into the aftermath of the war. But La Route des Flandres is not a story told in sequence: it is a series of recollections, obsessions, and sensory flashes, arranged in long, unpunctuated sentences that flow from one memory to another without clear transition, as consciousness actually moves. Simon's prose — which he compared to Faulkner's and which owes something to Proust's long sentences — creates a texture of time in which the present moment of narration, the wartime experience, and a much earlier event (the death of an ancestor, another de Reixach, in the Revolutionary wars, perhaps also a suicide) coexist without hierarchy. The novel's subject is the impossibility of recovering the past accurately and the way traumatic memory deforms and transforms rather than preserves what it cannot release.",
