    intro: "Les Géorgiques (The Georgics, 1981) by Claude Simon is widely regarded as his masterpiece — the novel in which all the themes, techniques, and preoccupations of the earlier work are brought together at the fullest scale and with the greatest formal ambition. It takes its title from Virgil's Georgics (poems about farming and rural labour) and its epigraph from a passage about the persistence of the past in the landscape, and it interweaves three narratives: that of L.S.M., a Revolutionary-era French general who is managing his estates while simultaneously waging wars across Europe (based on Simon's ancestor Jean-Pierre Lacombe Saint-Michel); that of a soldier in the Spanish Civil War (clearly autobiographical, returning to the territory of Le Palace); and that of a British writer called O., who was also in Spain and who wrote a book about it (clearly George Orwell). The three narratives never directly interact — they are held in parallel by the novel's formal organisation — but they rhyme across centuries: the same landscapes, the same military disasters, the same gap between revolutionary ideals and the reality of fighting, the same attempt to manage a farm while the world collapses. Les Géorgiques is a long and demanding novel, but it is also the most fully achieved Simon — the work that most completely realises the ambition of the Nouveau Roman to create a new kind of historical novel.",
