    slug: 'l-acacia-simon',
    intro: "L'Acacia (The Acacia Tree, 1989) by Claude Simon is the most directly autobiographical of his major novels and the one in which he most openly confronts the two events that shaped his life: the death of his father in the First World War, before Simon was born, and his own catastrophic experience in the retreat through Flanders in May 1940. The novel alternates between two temporal strands — the earlier one following Simon's mother as she travels to find the grave of her husband, killed in 1914, and traces the arc of her widowhood; the later one following the younger Simon through the opening disasters of the Second World War. The acacia tree of the title grows in the courtyard of the house Simon grew up in, and its seasonal rhythms provide the novel's only fixed natural point — everything else is movement, loss, war, and the attempt to recover a father who died before he could be known. L'Acacia is among Simon's most accessible novels, partly because the autobiographical investment is clear and partly because the narrative, while still using Simon's characteristic long sentences and non-linear memory structure, has a more explicit emotional arc than the earlier Nouveau Roman works. It is the natural entry point for readers who want to approach Simon through his personal history rather than through his formal ambition.",
