    slug: 'le-chiendent-queneau',
    intro: "Le Chiendent (The Bark Tree, 1933) by Raymond Queneau is the debut novel that began one of the most distinctive careers in twentieth-century French literature. Written under the influence of Descartes's Discourse on Method (which Queneau translated the structure of into the novel's seven-chapter organisation — seven parts corresponding to the six parts of the Discourse plus an introduction) and of Joyce's Ulysses (which Queneau had been reading in French translation with great attention), Le Chiendent follows a group of characters in the suburban Paris area of Puteaux over a period that begins with one of them, Étienne Marcel, being observed for the first time by Pierre Le Grand, a stranger who begins watching him with the same intense attention that, once applied, transforms the observed into a conscious being. The novel's plot is comic and digressive — it involves a treasure hunt, a love story, a war, and a series of escalating absurdities — but its philosophical structure is serious: the Cartesian question 'who am I?' is treated as a genuine uncertainty that watching and being watched can resolve or complicate. Le Chiendent is harder and more demanding than the later Queneau novels, but it is the foundation of everything — the linguistic experiments, the comic metaphysics, the love of spoken French — that makes Queneau one of the great originals of modern literature.",
