    slug: 'dans-un-mois-dans-un-an-sagan',
    intro: "Dans un mois, dans un an (Those Without Shadows, 1957) by Françoise Sagan is the third novel and the most socially expansive of her early work: where Bonjour Tristesse and Un certain sourire are tightly focused on a single narrator, Dans un mois, dans un an follows an ensemble of five or six characters in the Parisian publishing and cultural milieu — writers, editors, women who are loved and women who love, men who charm and men who are charmed — through a year of shifting attachments. The novel covers more social ground than its predecessors but maintains the same cool, precise, slightly mournful observation: these are people who live well, think clearly, and love without quite sufficient force to prevent themselves from being lonely. The title — 'in a month, in a year' — comes from a line that characters repeat to themselves as a consolation: whatever is unbearable now will be different in a month, in a year, time will pass and things will change, and this is both a comfort and a mild form of resignation. Sagan's third novel was received with somewhat less excitement than her first two — the ensemble form dilutes the intensity that made the single-narrator books so sharp — but it is a precise and characteristic work, and the milieu it describes (literary Paris in the mid-1950s) is rendered with great accuracy.",
