    slug: 'la-chamade-sagan',
    intro: "La Chamade (The Heartbeat, 1965) by Françoise Sagan is the novel that most clearly revisits the themes of Bonjour Tristesse at a more mature level: Lucile is a young woman in her twenties who has been kept, very comfortably, by Charles, a wealthy and kind older man — she has no money of her own, no career, no fixed identity beyond her relationship with Charles. When she falls in love with Antoine, a man of her own age, she must decide whether to exchange her comfortable existence for a life with someone she genuinely desires but who cannot provide what Charles provides. The novel follows the affair's beginning, middle, and eventual crisis, and the choice it forces on Lucile — between security and passion, between comfort and desire, between a life that is pleasant and a life that is alive — is presented with the same cool moral precision as Sagan's earlier novels. La Chamade is perhaps Sagan's most explicit treatment of the economic dimension of women's choices: Lucile's dependence on Charles is not incidental but the novel's structural condition, and the question of what a woman who has no independent income can actually choose is never far from the surface. The title refers to the drum signal in French military tradition indicating surrender — 'battre la chamade' — which is how Sagan describes the particular surrender of the heart in love.",
