    slug: 'la-peste-camus',
    intro: "La Peste (The Plague, 1947) by Albert Camus is the novel in which Camus moves from the solitary absurdism of L'Étranger to a collective study of how human beings respond when an epidemic strips away all illusions about the world's rationality and benevolence. Set in Oran, a French Algerian port city, the novel follows a group of characters — the doctor Bernard Rieux, the journalist Raymond Rambert, the mysterious Tarrou, the bureaucrat Grand, and the criminal Cottard — as the city is closed off by a bubonic plague outbreak and each must decide how to live under conditions of arbitrary mass death. Rieux tends the sick not from heroism or religious faith but from a simple professional commitment to fighting suffering wherever he finds it; Tarrou, who has spent his life trying not to be complicit in the systems that kill people, is the novel's moral centre; Rambert wants to escape to his love in Paris and gradually decides he cannot. The novel is Camus's allegory for the French experience under the German Occupation — the plague is the German presence, the different characters represent different responses to totalitarianism — and it is also a genuine exploration of the absurdist question: how do you live, act, and love in a world that will kill you arbitrarily? La Peste won Camus enormous international prestige and is still one of the most widely read French novels of the twentieth century, regularly revived in times of epidemic and political emergency.",
