    slug: 'le-mythe-de-sisyphe-camus',
    intro: "Le Mythe de Sisyphe (The Myth of Sisyphus, 1942) by Albert Camus is the philosophical essay that provides the intellectual framework for the fiction Camus was producing at the same time — it was published in the same year as L'Étranger and alongside his play Caligula. The essay begins with the assertion that there is only one truly serious philosophical question: whether life is worth living. Camus's answer is not nihilistic but rigorously argued: the absurd arises in the confrontation between the human demand for clarity and the world's unreasonable silence; the proper response to the absurd is not suicide (which accepts the absurd's terms) and not philosophical leap (which abandons reason in favour of religious consolation) but revolt — the permanent rebellion of a consciousness that refuses both despair and false hope. The essay ends with Camus's famous analysis of Sisyphus, condemned by the gods to roll a rock up a hill forever only to watch it roll back down: Camus argues that Sisyphus is happy, because the rock is his, the struggle is his, and the moment of watching the rock roll down — the moment of full consciousness — is the moment of revolt. The essay is remarkable for its combination of philosophical rigour and literary beauty; Camus writes about Kafka, Kierkegaard, Husserl, Heidegger, and Jaspers with the same vivid personal engagement that he brings to the figure of Sisyphus himself.",
