    intro: "La Vagabonde (The Vagabond, 1910) by Colette is a semiautobiographical novel narrated by Renée Néré, a music-hall mime and dancer who has rebuilt her life after a painful divorce from her dissolute husband Taillandy. Renée earns her own living, values her solitude, and has no desire to remarry — until she meets Maxime Dufferein-Chautel, a wealthy admirer who falls seriously in love with her. The novel is one of the first sustained fictional explorations of what female independence actually feels like from the inside: the freedom is real but also lonely, the pleasure in self-sufficiency is genuine but also partly a wound, and Renée's eventual decision (which the sequel L'Entrave begins to reopen) is a deliberate choice about what kind of life a woman can have. Colette draws on her own years as a music-hall performer to make the world of the provincial touring show — the smells, the exhaustion, the companionship, the tiny dressing rooms — entirely vivid. La Vagabonde is a short, intensely concentrated novel and one of the foundational texts of modern French women's fiction.",
