    slug: 'l-ile-mysterieuse-verne',
    intro: "L'Île mystérieuse (The Mysterious Island, 1874) by Jules Verne is his most complete and ambitious single novel — a Robinson Crusoe story elevated to a systematic demonstration of what human knowledge and ingenuity can build from nothing. Five American Civil War prisoners escape from a Confederate prison camp in Richmond by balloon during a storm and are deposited on an uncharted volcanic island in the Pacific. What they build, under the leadership of the engineer Cyrus Harding, is a complete civilization: they smelt iron, manufacture nitroglycerin, build a brick forge, cultivate crops, domesticate animals, and eventually make themselves entirely self-sufficient. Verne's achievement is the combination of the Robinson Crusoe appeal — humans surviving alone against nature — with a systematic practical education: the reader who follows Harding's explanations learns how iron ore becomes steel becomes tools. The novel is also the resolution of the Captain Nemo story: the Nautilus is moored in an underwater cave beneath the island, and Nemo — dying, his true identity now revealed as the Indian Prince Dakkar — emerges to save the castaways several times before his death. L'Île mystérieuse is the novel in which Verne brings together the optimistic faith in human knowledge of his Voyages extraordinaires and the melancholy Romantic rebellion of his greatest character.",
