    slug: 'vipere-au-poing-bazin',
    intro: "Vipère au poing (1948) by Hervé Bazin is the most celebrated and most unsparing portrait of a monstrous mother in French literature — a work that made 'Folcoche' (from 'folle cochonne,' mad pig) a permanent fixture of the French cultural vocabulary for the bad mother. The novel follows Jean Rezeau, known as Brasse-Bouillon (Stirrer of the Pot), and his brothers Ferdinand and Marcel, who are sent to live with their paternal grandmother in Brittany when their parents return from China — and who are retrieved from the grandmother's warm household by their mother Paule, who proceeds to subject them to a systematic regime of cold, hunger, humiliation, and psychological cruelty. Bazin was himself the third son of a cold and domineering mother, and Vipère au poing is barely disguised autobiography: the children's rebellion against Folcoche, their small victories and crushing defeats, their strategies for survival, have the specificity of lived experience. The novel is not sentimental: Bazin refuses to rescue his protagonists through external intervention, and the 'victory' at the end — Jean eventually breaks free — is qualified by what the struggle has cost him. The novel invented a vocabulary (Folcoche, Brasse-Bouillon) that entered French and became available for discussion of maternal cruelty, which is part of why it is taught in schools: it gave French students a language for a domestic reality that was previously nameless.",
