    intro: "Pêcheur d'Islande (1886) by Pierre Loti is the novel that made Loti famous and remains his most celebrated work — a story of Breton fishermen who sail annually to the waters off Iceland to fish for cod, and of the women who wait for them on shore. The novel alternates between the grey, violent, magnificent Atlantic — one of the most sustained evocations of the sea in French fiction — and the village of Paimpol in Brittany, where the fishermen's wives and mothers count the months until their return. At the center is the love between Yann Gaos, the proudest and most beautiful of the fishermen, and Gaud Mével, the daughter of a newly wealthy Paimpol family; Yann resists marriage for most of the novel out of a combination of pride, attachment to the sea, and a sense that he belongs to the ocean rather than to any woman. The novel ends with Yann's death at sea — he disappears during a storm, and the final pages describe the sea as the lover who has finally claimed him. Pêcheur d'Islande is written in a style of lyrical melancholy that was immediately recognized as distinctive — not Naturalism, not Romanticism, but something in between, a prose poetry of sea and loss and the simple, heroic lives of working people — and it influenced the regionalist tradition in French fiction as well as writers as different as Colette and Hemingway.",
