    slug: 'poil-de-carotte-renard',
    intro: "Poil de Carotte (1894) by Jules Renard is a series of short linked sketches about a red-haired, freckled boy — the youngest son of the Lepic family — who is openly unloved by his mother, Madame Lepic, and who has learned to survive her cruelty through an internal stoicism that is simultaneously moving and alarming. The title is the boy's nickname: carrot-top. Madame Lepic is one of the great maternal villains of French literature — not a monster in any obvious sense, but a woman who simply does not like her youngest child and does not pretend to, assigning him the most unpleasant tasks (sleeping in the dark, catching the chickens, emptying the chamber pots) while favoring his older siblings. Poil de Carotte responds not with rebellion but with a learned self-erasure: he internalized the view that he is worthless and that suffering is his natural condition. The sketches are very short, very dry, and written with a control that keeps the pathos from becoming sentimentality. Renard was a sharp journalist and playwright as well as a novelist, and the economy of the Poil de Carotte sketches influenced Colette, Jules Vallès, and the tradition of French autobiographical fiction that uses brevity as a form of emotional compression. The book has never been out of print in France and is taught in schools, where its portrait of childhood without adult protection speaks directly to children's experience of powerlessness.",
