    slug: 'lettres-de-mon-moulin-daudet',
    intro: "Lettres de mon moulin (1866, collected 1869) by Alphonse Daudet is a collection of short stories and sketches set in Provence, ostensibly written from an old windmill near Fontvieille that Daudet rented and immortalized. The book established the literary myth of Provence — its light, its cicadas, its garrulous peasants, its goatherds, its curé, its Papal Avignon — and gave French readers a Provençal world that felt simultaneously exotic and deeply French. The individual pieces range from gentle comedy (the tale of Maître Cornille, who pretends his mill still works, scattering flour-flour to fool the villagers) to near-tragedy (the story of the goat who chose one night of freedom over a lifetime of safety, only to be eaten by the wolf at dawn) to historical pageant (the Avignon of the Popes). The 'Chèvre de M. Seguin' — the little goat who escapes, dances all night under the stars, and is eaten at dawn — is arguably the most famous individual piece in French short fiction after Mérimée, taught in French schools for generations and summarizing a particular Romantic view of freedom and its price. The collection helped create the French regionalist tradition and anticipated the Provençal writing of Jean Giono a half-century later.",
