    slug: 'flaubert-leducation-sentimentale',
    intro: "L'Éducation sentimentale (1869) by Gustave Flaubert is the anti-novel of French Realism — the story of Frédéric Moreau, a young provincial who comes to Paris to make his fortune, falls in love with the bourgeois wife of an art dealer, and spends the entire novel failing to do anything decisive in love, in politics, or in his career, while history (the revolution of 1848, the June massacre, the coup of 1851) happens around him without his participation. The novel is the most systematic defeat of the Bildungsroman in French literature: nothing develops, no education occurs, the Sentimental Education is entirely ironic. Flaubert described it as the novel of modern life, meaning a novel in which modern life renders the development of the self impossible. The famous ending — in which Frédéric and Deslauriers agree that the best time of their lives was an unsuccessful visit to a brothel when they were teenagers — is the most devastating conclusion in French fiction: the education has produced nothing, and the best thing in a life is its first failure. Henry James called it 'one of the great books of the century' and it has been increasingly recognized as the novel that most accurately predicted the political and emotional texture of the 20th century.",
