    slug: 'malraux-l-espoir',
    intro: "L'Espoir (Man's Hope, 1937) by André Malraux is the novel of the Spanish Civil War written while the war was still being fought — Malraux participated directly as the commander of the España squadron, a volunteer air force that flew for the Republic, and the novel is based on this experience. It is the most important documentary novel of the Spanish Civil War from the Republican perspective, covering the period from July 1936 to the Battle of Guadalajara in March 1937. The novel is deliberately documentary rather than classical — it has a large cast of characters, no single protagonist, and a narrative that follows the actual events of the war rather than imposing a dramatic structure. The title, L'Espoir (Hope), is ironic in context: the novel was written as the Republican cause was visibly failing, and the hope of the title is the hope that revolutionary solidarity can be maintained even in defeat. Malraux also adapted the novel into a film (Sierra de Teruel, completed 1939) which was one of the most important films of the Spanish Civil War period.",
