    slug: 'poemes-saturniens',
    intro: "Poèmes saturniens (Saturnine Poems, 1866) by Paul Verlaine is the first collection of one of the greatest French lyric poets — published when Verlaine was twenty-two, it already contains the characteristic qualities that will define his mature work: the musicality of the verse, the vagueness and tentativeness of mood, the preference for suggestion over statement, the fascination with the melancholy and the evanescent. The collection is dedicated to Gautier and explicitly acknowledges the Parnassian tradition as its starting point, but it already contains the seeds of the reaction against Parnassianism that Verlaine would develop in Romances sans paroles — the emphasis on music over sculpture, on mood over statement, on the ephemeral over the permanent. The most celebrated poems in the collection — 'Mon rêve familier,' 'Chanson d'automne,' 'Soleils couchants' — are already fully characteristic: languorous, musical, suggestive, marked by a sense of melancholy loss that cannot be precisely articulated but is perfectly conveyed. The collection is the beginning of one of the most important transitions in French poetry: the movement from the Romantic and Parnassian tradition toward Symbolism.",
    startWith: "Begin with 'Chanson d'automne' (the most famous poem in the collection, with its extraordinary opening rhythm), 'Mon rêve familier' (the most personal), and 'Soleils couchants' (the most characteristic). Then read the 'Mélancolie' section for the most developed statement of the collection's dominant mood.",
