    slug: 'fetes-galantes-verlaine',
    intro: "Fêtes galantes (Elegant Entertainments, 1869) by Paul Verlaine is the collection that established him as a major poet and one of the defining works of the Symbolist movement — twenty-two short poems set in the world of the eighteenth-century fête galante, the outdoor entertainment in which costumed aristocrats play at love against a backdrop of parklands and fountains. Verlaine takes this subject matter from the painter Watteau, whose canvases of Pierrot, Harlequin, and Columbine provided the dreamlike Rococo world that the collection inhabits, and transforms it into something entirely his own: the characters are not the aristocrats of the historical eighteenth century but figures in a dream, at once real and theatrical, their love play always on the edge of melancholy because the dream is always on the edge of dissolving. The collection is formally extraordinary — each poem is technically accomplished while appearing effortless, the language is simple while the effect is complex — and it represents the fullest early realization of Verlaine's characteristic combination of musical verse, theatrical setting, and pervasive melancholy. 'Clair de lune,' 'Mandoline,' and 'À Clymène' are among the finest short lyrics in French.",
    startWith: "Read Fêtes galantes complete — it is short (22 poems) and works best as a whole. 'Clair de lune' (the opening poem) sets the dreamlike tone; 'Pantomime,' 'Sur l'herbe,' and 'À Clymène' show the range. The collection rewards rereading.",
