    slug: 'romances-sans-paroles',
    intro: "Romances sans paroles (Songs Without Words, 1874) by Paul Verlaine is the collection in which his poetry reaches its most characteristic perfection — the work of a poet who has pushed the musicality of verse to an extreme previously unknown in French, the vagueness of mood to a point where precise statement has become almost impossible, and the suggestion of meaning to a point where the poem evokes rather than says. The collection was written during the most turbulent period of Verlaine's life: the years of his relationship with Rimbaud, the journey to Belgium and England, and the shooting incident in Brussels (1873) for which Verlaine was imprisoned. The biographical turbulence is not directly expressed — the poems are often about rain, gray landscapes, and the indeterminate emotional states of the traveler in Belgium and England — but it is perfectly transmitted through the poems' refusal to be precise about anything, their insistence on the suggestive over the stated. The 'Ariettes oubliées' sequence that opens the collection contains the most celebrated of all Verlaine's poems ('Il pleure dans mon cœur' — 'It rains in my heart as it rains in the city') and the most complete realization of his aesthetic ideal: 'De la musique avant toute chose' (music above all else).",
    startWith: "Begin with the 'Ariettes oubliées' sequence, particularly 'Il pleure dans mon cœur' and 'Dans l'interminable / Ennui de la plaine' (no. III). Then the 'Paysages belges' poems for the specifically English and Belgian landscape register. Read the collection complete — it is short and works as a unity.",
