    slug: 'jadis-et-naguere',
    intro: "Jadis et naguère (Long Ago and Not So Long Ago, 1884) by Paul Verlaine is the collection that established him as the leader of the Symbolist movement, not because it is his most characteristic work but because it contains the 'Art poétique' — the theoretical poem that became the Symbolist manifesto — and several other poems that appeared in the little magazines and anthologies through which the movement constituted itself in the early 1880s. The collection is the most varied of Verlaine's major works: it gathers poems from across twenty years of writing, including pieces from the pre-Saturniens period, pieces connected to the years with Rimbaud, and more recent work, and its range is correspondingly wide. The 'Art poétique' is the most famous text in the collection, but other poems are equally important: 'Crimen amoris,' the long symbolist fantasia about Rimbaud as a demonic figure, and 'À Albert Mérat,' the address to a fellow poet that connects Jadis et naguère to the wider world of Symbolist literary culture. The collection's historical significance is greater than its aesthetic unity: it is the book that made Verlaine the official precursor of the Symbolist generation.",
    startWith: "Read 'Art poétique' first — it is the most influential text in the collection and the theoretical framework for everything else. Then 'Crimen amoris' for the Rimbaud material. The rest of the collection rewards selective exploration; the 'Jadis' section is the most historically significant.",
