    slug: 'les-paradis-artificiels',
    intro: "Les Paradis artificiels (Artificial Paradises, 1860) by Charles Baudelaire is the great essay on the psychology and aesthetics of intoxication — a study of hashish and opium that is simultaneously a literary analysis of De Quincey's Confessions of an English Opium-Eater (which Baudelaire translates and annotates) and a philosophical meditation on the relationship between intoxication and the ideal. The work is organized in two parts: 'Le Poème du haschisch,' Baudelaire's original essay on the psychological states produced by hashish, and 'Un mangeur d'opium,' his translation and expansion of De Quincey. Baudelaire's position in Les Paradis artificiels is more complex than a simple celebration of artificial paradises: he is simultaneously fascinated by the states of heightened perception that intoxication produces (which he describes with extraordinary precision and beauty) and suspicious of them as substitutes for the genuine spiritual life that he believes the artist must pursue through suffering and discipline rather than through chemical shortcut. The work is the most systematic expression of Baudelaire's aesthetic theory and the one that most directly connects his poetry to the tradition of mysticism and transcendence.",
