    slug: 'curiosites-esthetiques',
    intro: "Curiosités esthétiques (Aesthetic Curiosities, 1868 — posthumous) by Charles Baudelaire is the great collection of his art criticism — essays on the Salon of 1845, the Salon of 1846, the Salon of 1859, his essays on Ingres, Delacroix, Daumier, Constantin Guys, and other artists of the period. The work is essential reading for anyone interested in nineteenth-century French painting and one of the most influential documents in the history of art criticism. Baudelaire's critical position is developed most fully in 'Le Peintre de la vie moderne' (The Painter of Modern Life, 1863), which defines the modern artist as the flâneur who moves through the city extracting its fleeting beauty — 'la modernité, c'est le transitoire, le fugitif, le contingent' (modernity is the transitory, the fugitive, the contingent) — and in the Salon essays, which establish Delacroix as the great Romantic colorist and opponent of the classical line-based tradition of Ingres. The collection contains some of the most brilliant art criticism in any language and develops the aesthetic theory that is implicit in Les Fleurs du mal into a systematic account of the relationship between art, beauty, and modernity.",
