    slug: 'actes-et-paroles',
    intro: "Actes et Paroles (Acts and Words, 1875-1876) by Victor Hugo is the great documentary record of his political life — a four-volume collection of his speeches, letters, manifestos, proclamations, and political actions from 1841 to 1876, organized chronologically and annotated by Hugo himself. The work is an unusual hybrid: part personal archive, part political autobiography, part manifesto for the republican cause that Hugo had championed since the coup of 1851. The speeches in Actes et Paroles are among the finest in the French political tradition — Hugo was one of the great orators of the nineteenth century, and his speeches before the Legislative Assembly, his proclamations during the exile, and his addresses after the return to France in 1870 have the same rhetorical power as Les Châtiments in verse. The work is rarely read complete today, but the individual speeches and political documents are frequently anthologized, and Actes et Paroles provides the most complete picture of Hugo as a political actor rather than a poet.",
