    slug: 'la-fin-de-satan',
    intro: "La Fin de Satan (The End of Satan, posthumous 1886) by Victor Hugo is the unfinished philosophical epic that was to be the companion to La Légende des siècles — where the Légende narrates the moral progress of humanity, La Fin de Satan narrates the cosmic drama of evil and its ultimate redemption. The poem begins with Satan's fall from heaven and his creation of the material world as a realm of darkness, and it follows Satan's relationship with human history through a series of great episodes: the prison, the sword (the invention of war and oppression), and the final sections on the American and French Revolutions as the beginning of Satan's ultimate defeat. The poem is unfinished — Hugo worked on it across the exile period and never completed it to his satisfaction — but what exists is among the most extraordinary poetry Hugo wrote, including the great invocation of Satan at the opening ('Satan est mort, refaites-lui des ailes' — Satan is dead, give him wings again) and the episodes of Nimrod, Caïn, and the Crucifixion that develop into the most ambitious philosophical poetry in the French tradition.",
    startWith: "La Fin de Satan is best read after La Légende des siècles, which provides the philosophical framework. Begin with the Prologue (the Fall of Satan) and the section on Nimrod; then the Crucifixion section, which is the most sustained passage of visionary poetry in the work. The editorial apparatus in the Pléiade edition explains the manuscript situation.",
