    slug: 'dieu-hugo',
    intro: "Dieu (God, posthumous 1891) by Victor Hugo is the most systematically philosophical of his poems — a long dialogue between a Voice and a series of embodied worldviews (Atheism, Skepticism, Manichaeism, Paganism, Judaism, Christianity, Free Thought) about the nature of God and the universe, culminating in the vision of Light that transcends all partial perspectives. The poem was written during the exile period, revised sporadically across Hugo's later years, and published posthumously in an incomplete state. It is the most direct expression of Hugo's personal theology — a pantheistic, universalist, and ultimately mystical vision in which God is not a personal deity but the infinite Light that underlies all existence, and in which the various religions of humanity are partial glimpses of this Light, each capturing something true and each making the error of mistaking its partial truth for the whole. The poem is demanding — the philosophical arguments are more systematic than in any of Hugo's published poetry — but it is also the work that most fully explains the philosophical ambitions of Les Contemplations, La Légende des siècles, and La Fin de Satan.",
    startWith: "Dieu is best read after Les Contemplations and La Légende des siècles, which develop its central ideas in a more accessible form. Read the section on Free Thought first for the clearest statement of Hugo's own position; then the earlier sections for the tradition of religious thought that he is engaging with.",
