    intro: "La Légende des siècles (The Legend of the Centuries, 1859-1883) by Victor Hugo is the most ambitious poetic project of the nineteenth century — a three-volume series of poems that narrates the moral history of humanity from Genesis to the future, organized as a sequence of autonomous episodes rather than a continuous narrative. The project is explicitly philosophical: Hugo believes that history is the story of humanity's slow and painful progress from darkness toward light, from oppression toward freedom, from the bestial toward the divine. Each poem takes an episode from myth, history, or legend — the story of the Sultan Mourad, the episode of Roland at Roncevaux, the medieval poem Booz endormi, the futurist fantasy Le Satyre — and treats it as a moment in this larger narrative of progress. The most celebrated individual poems — 'Booz endormi' (the most perfect poem Hugo ever wrote, many readers argue), 'Le Satyre' (the visionary finale of the first series), 'Les Pauvres gens' (a narrative poem of human generosity) — are among the greatest in the French language. The project was too large for any single organizing form and has a necessarily uneven quality, but at its best La Légende des siècles is Hugo's most extraordinary achievement in poetry.",
