    slug: 'william-shakespeare-hugo',
    intro: "William Shakespeare (1864) by Victor Hugo is one of the greatest critical works in the French language — a philosophical essay on the nature of genius, ostensibly about Shakespeare but in reality a sustained argument about the role of the poet in human history. The work was written as the preface to Hugo's son François-Victor Hugo's complete translation of Shakespeare, but it far exceeds the occasion: it develops into a meditation on what Hugo calls the 'génies' (geniuses) of human history — Homer, Aeschylus, Isaiah, Ezekiel, Job, Dante, Rabelais, Cervantes, Shakespeare — and argues that these figures are not merely literary but prophetic, that they are the conscience and the voice of humanity at its highest moments of self-understanding. The work is not literary criticism in the modern sense — Hugo is not interested in close reading or historical context — but it is one of the most powerful statements of the Romantic theory of genius and its social function, and it is written in Hugo's most sustained and magnificent prose. The work also contains, in passing, some of the most memorable critical phrases in French — 'Shakespeare est un génie' is the whole argument distilled, and the surrounding elaboration is a masterpiece of critical prose.",
    startWith: "William Shakespeare can be read independently of any knowledge of Shakespeare's works — it is about the nature of genius rather than about specific Shakespeare plays. Read Part I ('Génies') for the theory, then the middle sections for the application. The work is available in complete French editions and in English translation.",
