    slug: 'l-art-d-etre-grand-pere',
    intro: "L'Art d'être grand-père (The Art of Being a Grandfather, 1877) by Victor Hugo is the most surprising of his major works — a collection of lyric poems about his grandchildren, Jeanne and Georges, that combines domestic charm with the full range of the late Hugo's philosophical ambition. The collection was written during Hugo's last decade in Paris, after his return from the nineteen years of exile, and it documents with extraordinary tenderness and humor the relationship between the old man and the two small children who became the center of his domestic life. The poems range from the purely playful (poems about the children's games, their misbehavior, their chatter) to the philosophical and elegiac, in which the presence of the children prompts meditations on childhood, age, death, and regeneration that connect to the large philosophical themes of Les Contemplations and La Légende des siècles. The collection is often underrated because of its subject matter, but it is in many ways the most formally experimental of Hugo's late work — the poems use syntax, rhythm, and imagery in ways that anticipate Symbolism and even Surrealism — and the love for Jeanne and Georges that pervades it is the most immediate and unguarded of any emotion in his work.",
