    slug: 'les-quatre-vents-de-l-esprit',
    intro: "Les Quatre Vents de l'esprit (The Four Winds of the Spirit, 1881) by Victor Hugo is one of the great late collections — published when Hugo was seventy-nine years old, two years before his death, it gathers poems from across the late period in four books organized by mode: satirical (Le Livre satirique), dramatic (Le Livre dramatique), lyric (Le Livre lyrique), and epic (Le Livre épique). The collection is both a retrospective and a continuation: it contains some of the most extraordinary late poems in any language, alongside earlier work that had not been collected, and its four-part organization represents Hugo's final attempt to demonstrate the full range of what he called the four winds of the spirit — the four modes in which the poet's voice can speak. The most celebrated poems in the collection are in the lyric and epic books: the elegy 'À Albert Dürer' (a meditation on the relationship between art and nature), the great philosophical poem 'Dieu' (expanded here from earlier drafts), and the epic poems that continue the project of La Légende des siècles. The collection is less well known than Les Contemplations and La Légende des siècles but is essential to any complete reading of Hugo's poetry.",
