    slug: 'les-feuilles-d-automne',
    intro: "Les Feuilles d'automne (Autumn Leaves, 1831) by Victor Hugo is the lyric collection that marks his transition from the public Romantic leader to the private poet of intimate feeling. Published in the year of his political disillusionment after the July Revolution of 1830, it is the first of the great lyric collections in which Hugo withdraws from the political arena and the literary battlefield of the Préface de Cromwell to speak in a different register: the domestic, the personal, the elegiac. The collection opens with the poem 'Ce siècle avait deux ans' ('This century was two years old'), which establishes the autobiographical framework — Hugo born at the same moment as the nineteenth century, his childhood and adolescence the childhood of the age — and continues with poems about his children, his home, childhood memory, and the melancholy of the passing of time. The title is deliberate: these are the autumn leaves of the Romantic spring of the 1820s, the meditative withdrawal of the poet from public noise into private music.",
