    slug: 'les-rayons-et-les-ombres',
    intro: "Les Rayons et les ombres (Rays and Shadows, 1840) by Victor Hugo is the last and most accomplished of the four lyric collections of the 1830s — the collection that fully resolves the tension between the private and the prophetic that has been productive across the decade, and that establishes the model for the great later work. The title — rays and shadows, light and dark — is the fundamental opposition of Hugo's mature poetry: the contrast between the light of truth, beauty, and justice and the shadow of ignorance, suffering, and oppression. The collection's first poem, 'Fonction du poète' (The Poet's Function), is one of the great programmatic poems in the French tradition: it argues that the poet's role is not to entertain or to express private feeling but to illuminate the way for humanity, to be 'a living flame' in the darkness of history. Les Rayons et les ombres contains some of Hugo's most celebrated individual poems, including 'Oceano Nox' (the great elegy for sailors lost at sea) and 'Tristesse d'Olympio' (the most famous of his personal lyrics, a meditation on the passage of time and the erasure of memory).",
