    slug: 'les-chatiments',
    intro: "Les Châtiments (The Punishments, 1853) by Victor Hugo is the great political poem of nineteenth-century France — the long collection of satirical and lyric poems written in exile after the coup d'état of 2 December 1851, in which Louis-Napoléon Bonaparte (Napoleon III) dissolved the French Republic and declared himself Emperor. Hugo escaped to Jersey and then Guernsey, where he remained for nineteen years, and Les Châtiments is the product of the first two years of this exile: a sustained and magnificent denunciation of the coup, the usurper, and the political crime of December 2nd. The collection is named after the punishments that Hugo prophesies for Napoleon III and his accomplices — he does not merely denounce the crime but predicts, with prophetic conviction, its ultimate failure and the restoration of the Republic. The collection is both the most partisan and the most artistically ambitious of Hugo's political works: the satirical poems reach the heights of classical invective (and are compared to Juvenal and Swift), the lyric poems achieve the visionary intensity of his best work, and the whole collection is unified by a prophetic conviction that the tide of history is on the side of the Republic.",
