    slug: 'pierre-et-camille-musset',
    intro: "Pierre et Camille (1844) by Alfred de Musset is the most unusual of his prose works — a novella about Pierre, a brilliant deaf-mute boy who grows up as the best friend and closest companion of Camille, the girl who lives with his family, and who eventually realizes that his friendship has become love but cannot speak it. The story is remarkable for the way it makes the absence of voice both a literal condition and a metaphor for the Romantic difficulty of expressing love — Pierre can communicate everything except the one thing he most needs to say, and the story is built around the question of whether the thing that cannot be spoken can nevertheless be understood. The novella was written at the same moment as the other late prose works (Mimi Pinson, Il faut qu'une porte soit ouverte ou fermée) and shares their economy and restraint — Musset's late prose is a long way from the irony and self-consciousness of the Confession, and Pierre et Camille is perhaps the furthest from his characteristic manner: simple, direct, and deeply sympathetic to its characters.",
