    intro: "Margot (1838) by Alfred de Musset is one of the shortest and most charming of his prose stories — a brief tale set in the Vendée during the Revolutionary Wars, in which a young royalist soldier finds himself sheltered by a peasant girl named Margot, whose simple-hearted loyalty and warmth provide a moment of grace in the middle of a brutal conflict. The story is unusual in Musset's prose fiction for its historical setting — the other prose works are set in contemporary Paris — and it shows a different range: the story of Margot and the soldier is written with the same economy and direct feeling as Mimi Pinson and Frère et Sœur, but the historical backdrop gives it a different quality, something between a short story and a prose poem. Margot herself is one of Musset's most likeable minor characters — practical, warm-hearted, unself-conscious — and the story's brief glimpse of human warmth against a background of violence is the kind of effect that the best of Musset's minor prose achieves.",
