    slug: 'emmeline-musset',
    intro: "Emmeline (1837) by Alfred de Musset is the earliest of his full prose novellas — a story of a young woman, Emmeline, who is genuinely in love with a man named Burner but who allows herself to be maneuvered by the coquettish social world she inhabits into a series of misunderstandings that eventually drive Burner away. The story is an examination of the gap between genuine feeling and social performance — Emmeline's coquetry is not malicious but a learned social behaviour that she cannot switch off even when she genuinely wants to express her real feeling, and the result is that Burner reads her performance as her real self and takes her at social face value. The story is more straightforwardly realistic than the other Musset novellas and more interested in social mechanism — how the social world creates the gap between feeling and expression — and it anticipates some of the psychological realism of Flaubert's portraiture, though with a lighter touch and a less sardonic perspective.",
