    slug: 'histoire-d-un-merle-blanc',
    intro: "L'Histoire d'un merle blanc (The Story of a White Blackbird, 1842) by Alfred de Musset is the most whimsical and most obviously self-referential of his prose works — an animal fable in which a white blackbird (a freak of nature in a world of black birds) searches for a white blackbird like himself to love, and in the course of his search meets a sequence of women who claim to be exceptional but who turn out to be painted or disguised. The story is a transparently autobiographical satire on the Romantic literary marketplace and the search for an exceptional woman worthy of an exceptional man — the white blackbird is a version of Musset himself, the exceptional black birds who paint themselves white are versions of the literary women who pretended to be as singular as they were merely fashionable. The tone is light and self-mocking, and the fable is one of the most delightful things Musset wrote — a relief from the earnestness of the Romantic manner, including Musset's own, and a demonstration that he could treat his own Romantic ambitions with genuine humour.",
