    slug: 'a-quoi-revent-les-jeunes-filles',
    intro: "À quoi rêvent les jeunes filles (What Young Women Dream Of, 1832) by Alfred de Musset is the lyric comedy that completes the Un Spectacle dans un fauteuil volume — a verse play in two acts about twin sisters Ninon and Ninette, who dream of the ideal lover, and the father who decides to find out what they dream of by hiring two young men to play their ideal lovers. The play is deliberately light and whimsical — almost a fairy-tale — and it represents a different side of the 1832 Musset from the tragic aspiration of La Coupe et les Lèvres and the ironic Byronic digression of Namouna. It is the version of the Romantic dream of love from the woman's side: what the young girl dreams of when she imagines the ideal, and how that dream relates to the real world that will eventually have to satisfy it. The play ends happily, with both sisters engaged to the young men their father hired, and the dream turning out to be closer to reality than anyone expected.",
