    slug: 'rolla-musset',
    intro: "Rolla (1833) by Alfred de Musset is one of the most famous and most controversial of his poems — a long narrative poem about Jacques Rolla, a young man who has spent three years dissolving his inheritance in dissipation in order to die on the night when he can no longer pay for it. On his last night alive, Rolla pays for one final night with Marie, a fifteen-year-old prostitute, and in the early morning, contemplating her sleeping innocence, he drinks poison. The poem is simultaneously an indictment of the Romantic generation's spiritual desolation (the famous opening passage, which develops the 'Rolla' diagnosis from Namouna, argues that Voltaire killed the God who made life meaningful and left the Romantic generation with nothing to believe in) and a lyric celebration of the beauty that persists in the midst of desolation — Marie's sleeping form as the last beautiful thing Rolla sees before he dies. The poem was controversial when published (George Sand defended it against conservative critics who objected to its subject matter) and is now seen as the defining text of French Romantic libertinage and its moral and aesthetic contradictions.",
    startWith: "Read the opening passage on Voltaire and the death of God first — it is one of the most famous passages in French Romantic poetry and establishes the argument. Then read the whole poem in order; the final scene with the sleeping Marie is the emotional center and only works after the opening establishes what Rolla has lost.",
