    slug: 'mimi-pinson',
    intro: "Mimi Pinson (1845) by Alfred de Musset is the most popular of his prose short stories — a short, charming tale of the seamstresses and students of the Latin Quarter, in which Mimi Pinson, a free-spirited grisette (working-class young woman), sells her only dress to pay for a friend's medicine, and the student Rodolphe who loves her learns about her generosity too late to help. The story is set in the world that Murger would make famous in Scènes de la vie de bohème (1851) and Puccini would make immortal in La Bohème (1896), and it is one of the founding texts of the literary mythology of Bohemian Paris — the world of young artists, grisettes, poverty, and generosity that became the essential backdrop for Romantic fiction. Mimi Pinson herself (named for a French folksong) gave her name to a type: the cheerful, generous, free-spirited grisette who lives fully in the moment and whose poverty is redeemed by her emotional richness.",
