    slug: 'les-deux-maîtresses-musset',
    intro: "Les Deux Maîtresses (The Two Mistresses, 1837) by Alfred de Musset is one of his most psychologically subtle short stories — a novella in which Valentin (the same name as the hero of Il ne faut jurer de rien, possibly the same character) finds himself simultaneously in love with two women: the aristocratic, intellectual Mme de Parnes and the simple, warm-hearted young Cécile. He pursues both, oscillates between them, discovers that he cannot give up either, and eventually loses both through the indecision that his double attachment creates. The story is a study of the Romantic temperament that desires more than one thing simultaneously — that finds in the ideal woman both the intellectual stimulus that Mme de Parnes provides and the emotional warmth that Cécile provides, and cannot choose between them because choosing would mean accepting the incompleteness of what the chosen one offers.",
