    slug: 'frere-et-soeur-musset',
    intro: "Frère et Sœur (Brother and Sister, 1842) by Alfred de Musset is the most moving of his prose novellas — a story of two orphaned young people, Gaspard and Marguerite, who grow up together in poverty, are separated, and reunite years later when Gaspard is dying; the reunion reveals the depth of the bond between them and raises (with great delicacy) the question of whether their feeling for each other has always been something more than sibling affection. The story is written with extreme economy and restraint — Musset says very little explicitly and trusts the reader to understand from what is not said — and it has the concentrated emotional power of the best of his dramatic work. It is less ironic, less comic, and less self-referential than most of what Musset wrote; it is simply and directly about the capacity of human beings for deep, enduring, and ambiguous attachment. The story is now one of the least-read of Musset's works but is regarded by French critics as one of his finest achievements in any genre.",
    startWith: "Frère et Sœur is short (under 60 pages) and should be read in one sitting. Read it after the comedies and dramas to experience the full range of Musset's register — the restraint and emotional directness of Frère et Sœur is very different from the wit and irony of the plays.",
