    slug: 'le-chandelier-musset',
    intro: "Le Chandelier (The Candlestick, 1835) by Alfred de Musset is the most formally brilliant of his Proverbes dramatiques and the one most often described as a comedy of manners — a play in which the young clerk Fortunio is introduced as the false lover to distract a suspicious husband from his wife Jacqueline's real affair with the officer Clavaroche. The device (the 'chandler,' i.e. the candle-holder who provides light for someone else's love) is a classic comic tradition, but Musset subverts it: Fortunio genuinely falls in love with Jacqueline, his love is so pure and so moving that she cannot continue to use him cynically, and by the end it is Clavaroche (the calculating officer who devised the scheme) who has been replaced by the pure feeling he dismissed as naive. The play is a defense of genuine Romantic feeling against the calculating cynicism that the world rewards — Fortunio's naive love, which everyone around him finds ridiculous, turns out to be the only force capable of disturbing the self-satisfied mechanics of the arranged affair.",
