    slug: 'la-coupe-et-les-levres',
    intro: "La Coupe et les Lèvres (The Cup and the Lips, 1832) by Alfred de Musset is one of his most ambitious early dramatic poems — a verse play in the Romantic mode that he described as 'a spectacle in an armchair' and never intended for the stage, exploring the doomed aspirations of Frank, a young Tyrolean hunter who rejects the peaceful village life and the love of Déidamia in pursuit of fame, power, and a freer, more turbulent existence. The play is a study of the Romantic temperament that desires something without being able to name it precisely — Frank wants 'more,' but the 'more' is never specified, and the play shows the havoc that unspecified romantic aspiration wreaks on the specific, real human beings who love him. La Coupe et les Lèvres belongs to the German Romantic tradition in its Alpine setting and its use of fantasy elements, but it is distinctively French in its analytical self-awareness: Frank is presented as a type (the Romantic hero), and the play is partly a critique of the type even as it sympathizes with it.",
    startWith: "La Coupe et les Lèvres is usually published with Namouna and À quoi rêvent les jeunes filles as one of Musset's early verse dramatic works. Read them in order — the three together form a picture of Musset's range in 1832, from the lyric idyll to the mock-heroic epic to the Romantic tragedy.",
