    slug: 'on-ne-saurait-penser-a-tout',
    intro: "On ne saurait penser à tout (One Cannot Think of Everything, 1849) by Alfred de Musset is one of his later Proverbes dramatiques — lighter and shorter than the major early comedies, but characteristic of the form at its most elegant. The Marquis de Valberg, at a country house party, forgets to ask his host for a horse and finds he must borrow one; in the process of arranging the loan he encounters his host's daughter Laure and falls into an easy, charming conversation that becomes the occasion for the play's central comic reversal. The Proverbe is a miniature — one act, two characters, a single situation — and its particular quality is the precision of its social observation: the way that small social awkwardnesses can become the occasion for genuine contact, the way that what begins as a practical problem (no horse) becomes an emotional encounter (mutual attraction). It belongs to the later Musset who has moved away from the Romantic tragedy of the early work and settled into a more comfortable accommodation with social comedy.",
    startWith: "Read On ne saurait penser à tout in the context of the complete Proverbes collection — it is a miniature and makes most sense as part of the range, from the one-act perfection of Il faut qu'une porte to the more elaborate comedy of Il ne faut jurer de rien.",
