    intro: "La Vénus d'Ille (1837) by Prosper Mérimée is the most celebrated of his fantastic tales — a short story in which the narrator, visiting a small town in the south of France to examine a recently unearthed bronze statue of Venus, witnesses a sequence of events that may or may not demonstrate that the statue is alive: the statue appears to grasp the wedding ring of the young man who jokingly places it on her finger during a game of tennis, and the morning after his wedding the young man is found dead with his chest crushed. La Vénus d'Ille is a masterpiece of the fantastic mode that Mérimée made his own: the narrator is skeptical, rational, and entirely unable to explain what he has observed; the events are narrated with scrupulous care; and the story refuses to resolve the ambiguity between the natural and the supernatural explanation. It belongs to the tradition of the conte fantastique that runs from Nodier through Gautier to Maupassant and that parallels the German tradition of Hoffmann and Kleist — and among the French examples, La Vénus d'Ille is the most economical and the most perfectly controlled.",
