    slug: 'son-excellence-eugene',
    intro: "Son Excellence Eugène Rougon (His Excellency Eugène Rougon, 1876) by Émile Zola is the sixth novel in the Rougon-Macquart cycle and one of the most politically direct — a study of the mechanics of Second Empire political power through the career of Eugène Rougon, the eldest Rougon son, who becomes a minister and senator under Napoleon III and who treats politics as a purely technical exercise in the manipulation of interest and favor, without principles or convictions of any kind. Zola researched the novel by attending sessions of the National Assembly and studying the political figures of the Second Empire, and the result is the most anatomically precise account of political cynicism in French Naturalist fiction. Rougon's political theory — that power is its own justification, that principles are decorations that powerful men put on for public occasions and take off in private, that the only real thing in politics is the calculation of advantage — is presented not as a corruption of some better ideal but as the true nature of all political life under any regime.",
