    slug: 'la-conquete-plassans',
    intro: "La Conquête de Plassans (The Conquest of Plassans, 1874) by Émile Zola is the fourth novel in the Rougon-Macquart cycle and the cycle's study of provincial Catholicism and clerical power — a novel about the conquest of Plassans (the fictional city modeled on Aix-en-Provence that appears throughout the cycle) by the Abbé Faujas, a priest who arrives from outside and systematically takes over the city's bourgeois society, eventually driving the hapless householder François Mouret (Marthe's husband) to madness. La Conquête de Plassans is Zola's most sustained meditation on the mechanics of ecclesiastical power: the Abbé Faujas is an operator as cynical as Eugène Rougon in the political world, but working through the specifically Catholic instruments of confession, moral influence, and the direction of female conscience. The novel is also remarkable for its portrait of the female characters — Marthe Mouret, who is gradually taken over by the Abbé's spiritual direction, and the various bourgeois women who fall under his influence — as the primary agents through whom clerical power penetrates the family and the community.",
    startWith: "La Conquête de Plassans (1874) — the novel is moderate length (about 400 pages) and self-contained; the Helen Constantine Penguin Classics translation is the best available in English. Read after La Fortune des Rougon to understand the Plassans setting, and alongside Son Excellence Eugène Rougon (1876) to see the parallel treatment of ecclesiastical and political power in the Second Empire.",
