    slug: 'beggars-opera-gay',
    intro: "The Beggar's Opera (1728) by John Gay is the most successful theatrical work of the eighteenth century — a three-act ballad opera that ran for an unprecedented sixty-two consecutive performances in its first season and transformed English theatrical culture. Gay's innovation was to adapt the conventions of Italian opera seria (which dominated London's fashionable theatres) by substituting familiar English ballad tunes for operatic arias and by making the characters not classical heroes but highwaymen, prostitutes, thieves, and a corrupt prison keeper, who speak and sing in the language of London's criminal underworld. The central character, Macheath, is simultaneously a charming highwayman, a figure satirizing the political corruption of Sir Robert Walpole (whose nickname 'The Great Man' Macheath echoes), and a mock-heroic lover pursued by two wives (Polly Peachum and Lucy Lockit) whose fathers are rival criminal businessmen. The ballad opera format — in which the dialogue is spoken prose and the songs are set to pre-existing tunes rather than original compositions — was Gay's invention, and it influenced everything from the music hall tradition to Brecht's Threepenny Opera (1928), which is a direct reworking. The opera's satire on the aristocracy, the government, and Italian opera was immediately recognized as politically radical, and its sequel Polly was banned before performance.",
    startWith: "The Beggar's Opera (1728) — the Oxford World's Classics edition edited by Hal Gladfelder (2013) is the best modern edition with excellent notes on the tunes and political satire. The Penguin Classics edition edited by Bryan Loughrey and T.O. Treadwell (1986) is also reliable. Listening to a recording while reading is strongly recommended — knowing the original tunes transforms the song lyrics.",
    books: [
      { title: "The Beggar's Opera and Polly (Oxford World's Classics)", author: 'John Gay (ed. Hal Gladfelder)', year: 2013, note: "The best modern scholarly edition — includes The Beggar's Opera and its banned sequel Polly; Gladfelder's notes identify every tune, every political target, and every allusion to Italian opera; essential reading." },
      { title: 'John Gay: A Profession of Friendship', author: 'David Nokes', year: 1995, note: "The standard biography — Nokes's account of the opera's genesis, its relationship to Gay's friendship with Swift and Pope, and the political furore over the banned Polly is essential context." },
      { title: "The Threepenny Opera", author: 'Bertolt Brecht (trans. Marc Blitzstein)', year: 1928, note: "Brecht and Weill's 1928 reworking of The Beggar's Opera, updated to Weimar Germany — the most influential adaptation and a masterpiece in its own right; read alongside Gay to see how the satire was transformed for the twentieth century." },
      { title: "English Theatre Music in the Eighteenth Century", author: 'Roger Fiske', year: 1973, note: "The standard scholarly study of ballad opera and its musical context — situates Gay's innovation in the tradition of English theatrical music and its relationship to Italian opera seria." },
      { title: "Macheath: A Novel", author: 'Malachi Whitaker', year: 1956, note: "A modern prose retelling of the Macheath story — useful as a reading companion for those who find the ballad opera format unfamiliar; shows the narrative's continuing vitality." },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: "What is The Beggar's Opera about?", a: "The Beggar's Opera (1728) by John Gay is a three-act ballad opera set in London's criminal underworld. The central figure is Captain Macheath, a charming highwayman who has secretly married Polly Peachum, the daughter of a criminal 'businessman' who organizes thieves and collects a percentage of their takings. Polly's father, furious at the marriage, arranges to have Macheath arrested and hanged; Macheath is pursued also by Lucy Lockit, daughter of the prison keeper, whom he has also promised to marry. The opera is simultaneously a comedy of criminal manners, a satire on the corruption of Prime Minister Walpole and the aristocracy, and a parody of Italian opera seria." },
      { q: "How did The Beggar's Opera influence later theatre?", a: "The Beggar's Opera invented the ballad opera format — spoken prose dialogue alternating with songs set to pre-existing popular tunes — which became the dominant form of English popular theatre for decades. Its satirical use of low-life characters to comment on high politics influenced the music hall tradition and the Victorian burlesque. Most directly, Brecht and Weill's Threepenny Opera (1928) adapted it wholesale for Weimar Germany, making Peachum and Macheath icons of modern anti-capitalist satire. The show has been in continuous revival since 1728 and is one of the most performed works in the English theatrical tradition." },
    ],
