    slug: 'the-divine-comedy-dante',
    intro: "The Divine Comedy (c. 1308-1320) by Dante Alighieri is the greatest work of medieval literature and one of the supreme achievements of world literature — a three-part journey through Hell (Inferno), Purgatory (Purgatorio), and Heaven (Paradiso) that combines personal allegory, political satire, theological investigation, and lyric poetry of extraordinary intensity. The poem is organized as a first-person journey: Dante the Pilgrim, having lost his way in the middle of his life, is guided through Hell and Purgatory by the Roman poet Virgil, and through Heaven by his idealized love Beatrice. The Inferno is the most famous part — its nine circles of Hell and their punishments (the murderers in the river of blood, the sodomites in the burning sand, the traitors in the ice of Cocytus) are organized around the principle of contrapasso (the punishment mirrors the sin). The Purgatorio is widely considered the most humane and the most lyrical. The Paradiso is the most philosophically ambitious and the most technically difficult. The Divine Comedy has had an unparalleled influence on European culture: it shaped the Italian literary language (Dante made his Florentine dialect into the literary Italian through the force of his example), established many of the conventions of the afterlife that persist in Western imagination, and has been the continuous companion of European writers from Boccaccio through Eliot to Borges.",
    startWith: "The Divine Comedy — Inferno first, then Purgatorio, then Paradiso, in a good modern translation with commentary. The best current translations: Robert Hollander and Jean Hollander (Doubleday, with facing-page Italian and exceptional notes); Robin Kirkpatrick (Penguin Classics, three volumes); John Ciardi (NAL, accessible verse). The Singleton commentary (Princeton) is the definitive scholarly apparatus.",
    books: [
      { title: 'The Divine Comedy', author: 'Dante Alighieri', year: 1320, note: "The complete three-canticle poem (Inferno, Purgatorio, Paradiso) — in the Hollander or Kirkpatrick translation, with facing-page Italian if possible. All three parts are essential; the Purgatorio and Paradiso are no less important than the Inferno, though the Inferno is the most famous" },
      { title: 'The Aeneid', author: 'Virgil', year: -19, note: "Virgil's companion — the founding of Rome, and Dante's guide through Hell. The companion as the primary classical predecessor: Dante uses Virgil as his guide through Hell and Purgatory, acknowledging the Aeneid as the founding epic that the Divine Comedy is in dialogue with throughout" },
      { title: "The Iliad", author: 'Homer', year: -750, note: "The companion — the Trojan War. The companion as the primary classical epic that Dante knows at second hand (through Latin summaries and the Aeneid, since medieval Europe had little direct access to Greek): the Divine Comedy's journey structure and its investigation of heroic virtue and vice are organized partly in relation to the Homeric tradition" },
      { title: 'Paradise Lost', author: 'John Milton', year: 1667, note: "Milton's companion — the Fall of Man. The companion as the primary English epic that is in sustained dialogue with the Divine Comedy: Milton uses the same three-part cosmological structure (Hell, middle region, Heaven) and the same sustained theological investigation that Dante developed" },
      { title: "The Wasteland", author: 'T.S. Eliot', year: 1922, note: "Eliot's companion — the fragmented modern poem. The companion as the modernist heir: Eliot saturates The Waste Land with Dantean allusions (particularly from the Inferno) and explicitly organized his investigation of the modern spiritual wasteland in relation to Dante's investigation of the medieval spiritual journey" },
      { title: 'Ficciones', author: 'Jorge Luis Borges', year: 1944, note: "Borges's companion investigations. The companion as the South American heir: Borges was one of the great Dante critics and scholars of the twentieth century, and his fictions are saturated with Dantean references, structures, and theological investigations — reading the two together is one of the most illuminating experiences in world literature" },
      { title: 'The Odyssey', author: 'Homer', year: -720, note: "Homer's companion — the journey home. The companion as the primary predecessor of the journey-structure: the Divine Comedy's three-part journey (through the realms of the dead) is the Christian transformation of the Odyssey's journey (through the Mediterranean world), and Dante meets Ulysses in Inferno 26 to acknowledge and transform the Homeric tradition" },
      { title: 'The New Life', author: 'Dante Alighieri', year: 1295, note: "Dante's companion early work — the love poems organized around his first sight of Beatrice. The companion as the predecessor: the New Life (Vita Nuova) establishes Beatrice as Dante's idealized love and poetic inspiration, and reading it before the Divine Comedy illuminates why Beatrice is the guide through Paradise" },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'What is the Divine Comedy about?', a: "The Divine Comedy is about Dante the Pilgrim's journey through the three realms of the afterlife: Hell (Inferno), Purgatory (Purgatorio), and Heaven (Paradiso). The poem begins 'Midway in the journey of our life I came to myself within a dark wood,' and Dante finds himself lost in a spiritual crisis in the middle of his life. He is guided through Hell and Purgatory by the Roman poet Virgil (who can go no further than Purgatory because he was not a Christian), and through Heaven by Beatrice, the Florentine woman Dante loved from childhood and who died young. In Hell, he sees the punishments of the damned (organized around the principle of contrapasso, where the punishment mirrors the sin) and meets many Florentine contemporaries and historical figures. In Purgatory, he sees the souls being purged of their sins and ascending toward God. In Paradise, he ascends through the nine celestial spheres to the Empyrean, where he has a vision of God that he can only partially describe in words." },
      { q: "Why is the Inferno more famous than the Purgatorio and Paradiso?", a: "The Inferno is more famous than the other two canticles for several reasons. Its subject matter — Hell, sin, punishment — is more immediately dramatic and more universally accessible than the subtle theological investigations of the Purgatorio and Paradiso. Its characters are often more vivid: Francesca da Rimini, Farinata, Ugolino, Ulysses, and Brutus and Cassius are among the most memorable portraits in Dante. Its punishments are concrete and visual in ways that the spiritual transformations of Purgatory and the beatific visions of Paradise are not. And it has been more extensively illustrated and adapted: Doré's engravings, Rodin's Gates of Hell, and countless other works have fixed the Inferno in the popular imagination. But most serious readers of Dante, including the most distinguished scholars and critics of the poem (T.S. Eliot, Jorge Luis Borges, Charles Williams), have argued that the Purgatorio is the most humane and the most lyrical, and that the Paradiso is Dante's greatest technical achievement — the attempt to describe the indescribable in poetry of extraordinary beauty." },
    ],
