    slug: 'wives-and-daughters-gaskell',
    intro: "Wives and Daughters (1864-66) by Elizabeth Gaskell is her last and fullest novel — an unfinished masterpiece (Gaskell died before completing the final chapters, and the novel ends with Molly and Roger's relationship still unresolved) that investigates the social world of the country town of Hollingford in the early nineteenth century through the eyes of Molly Gibson, the motherless daughter of the local doctor. Molly's world is upended when her father remarries — his new wife Hyacinth (a widow of great charm and no depth) brings her own daughter Cynthia into the household, and Cynthia's dazzling beauty and genuine warmth (combined with her moral carelessness) become both Molly's greatest friendship and her greatest source of suffering. The novel is organized around the parallel between Molly's steady, reliable, self-forgetful love (for the scientist Roger Hamley, who initially falls for Cynthia) and Cynthia's brilliant, charming, fundamentally uncommitted relationship to everyone around her. Wives and Daughters is Gaskell's closest approach to a Tolstoyan or Eliot-level social investigation — the character comedy of Miss Phoebe and Miss Browning, the tragedy of Squire Hamley and his son Osborne, and the portrait of the provincial scientific world (Roger is a naturalist who goes to Africa) all contribute to a picture of a whole society in transition.",
