    slug: 'the-myth-of-sisyphus-camus',
    intro: "The Myth of Sisyphus (1942) by Albert Camus is the essay that established the philosophical framework of the absurd — the confrontation between the human desire for clarity and the world's silence in response to that desire. Camus begins with the question of whether suicide is the only honest philosophical response to the absurd, and he answers: no. The absurd hero, like Sisyphus condemned to roll his boulder up the hill forever, must revolt against his condition — must continue living and acting in the face of the absurd without recourse to either suicide (which eliminates the contradiction) or philosophical suicide (religion, which resolves the contradiction by asserting a transcendent meaning). 'One must imagine Sisyphus happy.' The essay is inseparable from the novels — The Stranger and The Plague are its fictional companions — and from the postwar French philosophical climate of existentialism and absurdism. Camus rejected the label 'existentialist' (he preferred 'absurdist') but The Myth of Sisyphus and Being and Nothingness are the defining philosophical texts of the same moment.",
    startWith: "The Myth of Sisyphus (1942) — the complete essay. Best Camus reading order: The Stranger → The Myth of Sisyphus → The Plague → The Fall → The Rebel.",
    books: [
      { title: 'The Myth of Sisyphus', author: 'Albert Camus', year: 1942, note: "The complete essay — the absurd, the revolt against suicide, and the imagination of Sisyphus as happy. The companion to The Stranger: where the novel dramatizes the absurdist hero, the essay provides the philosophical framework; together they form a single philosophical statement" },
      { title: 'The Stranger', author: 'Albert Camus', year: 1942, note: "The companion novel — Meursault after the murder on the beach. Published the same year as The Myth of Sisyphus; Meursault is the absurdist man without explanation, and the essay is the theoretical companion to the novel's practice" },
      { title: 'The Plague', author: 'Albert Camus', year: 1947, note: "The companion novel — Rieux and the plague in Oran. The companion investigation of the absurdist condition in a collective rather than individual situation: where The Stranger is about Meursault's individual revolt, The Plague is about the collective response to a situation in which the human desire for meaning encounters the brute fact of epidemic disease" },
      { title: 'Being and Nothingness', author: 'Jean-Paul Sartre', year: 1943, note: "Sartre's companion philosophical masterwork. The companion text from the other side of the absurdism/existentialism divide: Sartre and Camus are investigating the same philosophical territory (the absence of inherent meaning; the necessity of human choice) but with different conclusions and different temperaments" },
      { title: 'The Fall', author: 'Albert Camus', year: 1956, note: "Camus's companion novel — Jean-Baptiste Clamence in Amsterdam. The companion investigation of the limits of the absurdist revolt: where The Myth of Sisyphus describes the possibility of authentic revolt, The Fall is about what happens when the revolt becomes self-serving" },
      { title: 'The Rebel', author: 'Albert Camus', year: 1951, note: "Camus's companion essay — the historical and philosophical investigation of revolt. The companion philosophical text to The Myth of Sisyphus: where The Myth of Sisyphus investigates the individual relationship to the absurd, The Rebel investigates the collective and political dimension of revolt — and its limits" },
      { title: 'The Genealogy of Morals', author: 'Friedrich Nietzsche', year: 1887, note: "Nietzsche's companion investigation of the origins of moral values. The companion philosophical text to The Myth of Sisyphus as an investigation of the same question: what does it mean to say yes to life in the face of suffering and meaninglessness? Camus's absurdist revolt is a response to Nietzsche's nihilism that refuses Nietzsche's solution (the Superman) while accepting his diagnosis (the death of God)" },
      { title: 'The Trial', author: 'Franz Kafka', year: 1925, note: "Kafka's companion novel — Josef K arrested without explanation. The companion literary exploration of the absurdist condition: Camus discusses Kafka extensively in The Myth of Sisyphus, arguing that Kafka stops short of the absurdist revolt by allowing hope (the possibility of divine meaning behind the incomprehensible process) to survive" },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'What is the philosophy of the absurd in Camus?', a: "The absurd, for Camus, is not a quality of the world or of the human being but of the confrontation between them: the human desire for clarity, meaning, and unity encounters a world that is fundamentally silent, indifferent, and irrational. The absurd is not the discovery that life is meaningless (which would be nihilism) but the discovery that there is no correspondence between the human need for meaning and the world's capacity to provide it. Camus's response to this discovery is the absurdist revolt: the refusal to allow the confrontation to be resolved either through suicide (which eliminates the human side of the equation) or through philosophical suicide, by which Camus means the leap of faith — the assertion of a transcendent meaning (God, History, Progress) that resolves the tension by importing meaning from outside the human condition. The absurdist hero lives with the confrontation, in revolt, in freedom, in passion — one must imagine Sisyphus happy." },
      { q: 'How does The Myth of Sisyphus relate to The Stranger?', a: "The Myth of Sisyphus and The Stranger were published in the same year (1942) and are companion texts. The essay provides the philosophical framework that the novel dramatizes: Meursault is Camus's absurdist man without explanation — he acts without the framework of conventional motivations (love, grief, ambition, guilt), he is condemned for this absence rather than for the murder, and in the face of his execution he achieves something like the absurdist revolt when he opens himself to the 'gentle indifference of the world.' The essay explains what Meursault's indifference means philosophically: it is not stupidity or psychopathy but the refusal of the bad faith that requires us to pretend that the world has the meanings we have projected onto it." },
    ],
