    slug: 'la-bete-humaine-zola',
    intro: "La Bête Humaine (1890) by Émile Zola is the seventeenth novel in the Rougon-Macquart cycle and one of Zola's most gripping — a crime novel and a psychological study of inherited violence, set in the world of the French railway system. Jacques Lantier, a locomotive driver and the son of Gervaise Macquart from L'Assommoir, is haunted by a hereditary impulse to kill women — an urge he has suppressed through willpower and the sublimating passion of his locomotive, La Lison. When he witnesses a murder on a train and falls in love with the murderer's wife Séverine, the repressed violence begins to surface. Zola uses the railway — its speed, its iron discipline, its capacity for destruction — as the central metaphor for the hereditary forces that drive his characters toward violent ends.",
