    slug: 'the-europeans-james',
    intro: "The Europeans (1878) by Henry James is a short, comic novel — one of the lightest and most accessible things James ever wrote — that reverses his usual American-in-Europe situation by bringing two cultivated Europeans, the Baroness Eugenia Münster and her artist brother Felix Young, to visit their American cousins, the Wentworths, at their Boston-area estate in the 1840s. The novel is James's most Austenian: its comedy is generated by the contrast between the worldly, adaptable Europeans and the scrupulous, morally earnest New Englanders, and James keeps the sympathy balanced between them — the Baroness's manipulation is finally exposed, but the Wentworths' rigidity is gently satirized, and the happy marriages at the end reward the people who manage to combine European sophistication with American integrity.",
