    slug: 'lost-illusions-balzac',
    intro: "Lost Illusions (Illusions perdues, 1837-43) by Honoré de Balzac is a three-volume novel published over six years that many critics consider the greatest novel in La Comédie Humaine — a portrait of the literary and journalistic marketplace of Paris in the 1820s organized around two young men from the provinces, Lucien Chardon (who wants to be a poet) and his friend David Séchard (a printer who wants to be an inventor). Lucien's story dominates the novel: his arrival in Paris, his transformation from poet to journalist, his discovery that the literary marketplace has no interest in art and every interest in money and politics, his rise through journalism, his spectacular fall, and his departure from Paris in disgrace. The novel is both a Bildungsroman about disillusionment and a sociological analysis of how culture is produced and corrupted in a commercial society — Balzac's portrait of journalism, publishing, criticism, and theatrical life in 1820s Paris is as comprehensive as any media criticism written since.",
    startWith: 'Lost Illusions (1837)',
    books: [
      { title: 'Lost Illusions', author: 'Honoré de Balzac', year: 1837 },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: "Do I need to read Père Goriot before Lost Illusions?", a: "No — though both novels are part of La Comédie Humaine and share the general social world of post-Napoleonic France, they are set in different social spheres (Père Goriot is about Parisian boarding-house society and social ambition; Lost Illusions is about the provincial bourgeoisie, the publishing world, and the literary marketplace) and have no overlapping major characters. Either can be read first. If you have already read Père Goriot, Lost Illusions extends your portrait of Balzac's Paris into the world of culture and media; if you have not, Lost Illusions is an equally valid entry point into La Comédie Humaine." },
      { q: "What does Lost Illusions say about journalism and publishing?", a: "Balzac's portrait of the Parisian press in the 1820s is one of the most devastating critiques of media in literary history. He shows journalism as an industry organized entirely around political allegiance and financial interest — newspapers exist to support political parties and to extort money from authors and theater managers (by threatening negative reviews) rather than to report events or judge art honestly. A critic in Lost Illusions does not read books to evaluate them; he receives them and decides whether to attack or praise based on who is paying. Balzac's analysis is specific to the Restoration period, but it has struck readers in every subsequent era as a recognizable portrait of how cultural industries actually work." },
      { q: "Is Lost Illusions one novel or three?", a: "Lost Illusions was published in three parts over six years: 'The Two Poets' (1837), 'A Distinguished Provincial at Paris' (1839), and 'The Sufferings of an Inventor' (1843). The three parts are collected and published as a single novel — they were planned together and their separation was a publishing accident rather than a literary design. Most editions present all three parts as one work. The middle part, 'A Distinguished Provincial at Paris,' is the most celebrated section and is sometimes published separately, though reading it without the first and third parts misses the novel's architecture." },
      { q: "What happens to Lucien Chardon at the end of Lost Illusions?", a: "Lucien leaves Paris in disgrace, having spent all the money he brought, accumulated debts, been abandoned by his aristocratic patron Madame de Bargeton, and compromised whatever literary integrity he arrived with through his journalism. He returns to the provinces on foot, nearly commits suicide by throwing himself into a river, is rescued by the Spanish priest Carlos Herrera (who is Vautrin, the escaped convict from Père Goriot in disguise), and agrees to return to Paris under Vautrin's protection. This ending leads directly into the sequel novel, A Harlot High and Low (Splendeurs et misères des courtisanes), in which Lucien's second Parisian career ends in catastrophe." },
    ],
