    intro: "Italo Svevo's Confessions of Zeno (La coscienza di Zeno, 1923) is the great Italian modernist novel — the fictional autobiography of Zeno Cosini, a Trieste businessman and hypochondriac who enters psychoanalysis and whose memoir becomes a monument to self-deception, irony, and unreliable narration. Svevo, born Aron Hector Schmitz to a Jewish-Italian family in Trieste, had published two earlier novels (A Life and As a Man Grows Older) to near-silence before writing Confessions of Zeno with the encouragement of his English teacher James Joyce, who championed the book in Paris after its publication and helped bring it to wider European attention. The novel — organized around Zeno's obsessions with cigarette-quitting, his father's death, his marriage, and his business partnership — reads as a foundational text of psychological realism and of the unreliable narrator as a formal device. Svevo died in a car accident in 1928, five years after publication. There are no sequels; the novel stands alone, though Svevo's two earlier novels and his story collections are well worth reading afterward.",
