    slug: 'the-ghost-writer-roth',
    intro: "Philip Roth's The Ghost Writer (1979) is the first and best of the Nathan Zuckerman novels — twenty-three-year-old Zuckerman visiting the New England farmhouse of the elder writer E. I. Lonoff, discovering a young woman named Amy Bellette who may or may not be Anne Frank alive and in America. Roth published nine novels featuring Nathan Zuckerman: The Ghost Writer (1979), Zuckerman Unbound (1981), The Anatomy Lesson (1983), The Prague Orgy (1985 novella, completing the first tetralogy), and then the later American trilogy (American Pastoral, I Married a Communist, The Human Stain) with Zuckerman as narrator. The Ghost Writer is the best entry point — short, perfectly constructed, and containing in concentrated form the themes of Jewish-American identity, literary ambition, and moral seriousness that run through all the Zuckerman novels. The first tetralogy is collected as Zuckerman Bound (1985).",
