    slug: 'pnin',
    intro: "Vladimir Nabokov's Pnin (1957) is among the most tender and subtly devastating of his novels — a portrait of Timofey Pnin, a Russian émigré professor of Russian literature at Waindell College, a fictional New England liberal arts college, who navigates American academic culture with a mixture of scholarly dignity and comic helplessness. Pnin is the least Nabokovian of Nabokov's English novels in style — less pyrotechnically demanding than Lolita (1955) or Pale Fire (1962) — but no less artistically controlled. The novel's six chapters follow Pnin through a series of disasters and misunderstandings — getting off the train at the wrong stop, the bowl that doesn't break at the party — while gradually revealing, with delicate indirection, the tragedy of his exile: his failed marriage to Liza, his lost Russia, his permanent outsider status in a culture that can't quite see him. Nabokov, himself an émigré, was writing about a man who is always almost comprehended and never quite, and the novel achieves an emotional complexity — grief beneath comedy — that makes it one of the most quietly heartbreaking of his books.",
