    slug: 'american-pastoral',
    intro: "Philip Roth's American Pastoral (1997), which won the Pulitzer Prize, is the first and most celebrated novel in his 'American Trilogy' and one of the great American novels of the late twentieth century — a devastating examination of the American Dream's disintegration through the story of the 'Swede,' Seymour Levov, a high school sports hero who builds the perfect American life and watches it destroyed when his daughter becomes a domestic terrorist during the Vietnam era. Roth narrates the novel through Nathan Zuckerman, his alter ego, who imagines and reconstructs the Swede's story at their forty-fifth high school reunion; the meditation on what American innocence costs and what destroys it unfolds with the structure of a Greek tragedy. American Pastoral belongs to Roth's late great period, which also includes I Married a Communist (1998) and The Human Stain (2000).",
