    slug: 'weep-not-child',
    intro: "Ngũgĩ wa Thiong'o's Weep Not, Child (1964) was the first novel published in English by an East African writer and remains one of the most important accounts in fiction of the Mau Mau uprising — the Kenyan anti-colonial revolt of the 1950s. The novel follows Njoroge, a young Kikuyu boy whose family is torn apart by the violence of the uprising, through a childhood in which faith in education and in God is gradually destroyed by the reality of colonial power. Ngũgĩ wrote it while a student at Makerere University in Uganda, drawing on his own childhood in the Limuru district north of Nairobi during the Emergency. It belongs to the tradition of African novels about colonialism and independence that Achebe's Things Fall Apart had inaugurated in 1958 — the same concerns applied to the East African experience.",
