    slug: 'invisible-cities',
    intro: "Italo Calvino's Invisible Cities (1972) is the most beautiful and most purely imaginative of his books — the fictional dialogue between Marco Polo and Kublai Khan, in which Polo describes the cities of the Khan's empire, each organized around a different principle: memory, desire, signs, the dead, time. The cities are not real cities but meditations on what cities are: what it means to live in a shared space, what we project onto the built environment, what we lose when we move through the world without attention. Invisible Cities is a work of philosophy disguised as poetry disguised as fiction, and it can be entered anywhere and read in any order without losing its effect.",
