    intro: "Stefan Zweig's The World of Yesterday (1942) is the greatest memoir of the early twentieth century — Zweig's account of his life from his comfortable Viennese Jewish bourgeois childhood through the First World War, the Weimar Republic, and the rise of Hitler to his exile in Brazil, written in the weeks before his suicide in 1942. Zweig was one of the most famous writers in the world in the 1930s, and his memoir is above all an elegy for the European culture that produced him — a culture of cosmopolitan humanism, of the free movement of ideas and people across national boundaries, of the belief that civilization was cumulative and irreversible. The World of Yesterday is required reading for understanding what the twentieth century destroyed.",
