    slug: 'we-have-always-lived-in-the-castle',
    intro: "Shirley Jackson's We Have Always Lived in the Castle (1962) is a gothic novel narrated by eighteen-year-old Merricat Blackwood, who lives in isolation with her older sister Constance and their Uncle Julian in the family mansion after most of their family has died from arsenic poisoning at dinner six years earlier. Told in Merricat's strange, obsessive, and unreliable voice, the novel is about exclusion, female power, and the violence done to those who refuse to conform — one of the great American novels and Jackson's final work before her death in 1965.",
    startWith: 'We Have Always Lived in the Castle',
    books: [
      { title: 'We Have Always Lived in the Castle', author: 'Shirley Jackson', year: 1962, note: "The complete standalone novel; reads in an afternoon. A natural companion to Jackson's The Haunting of Hill House (1959), which shares the gothic atmosphere and isolated female protagonist." },
      { title: 'The Haunting of Hill House', author: 'Shirley Jackson', year: 1959, note: "Jackson's horror novel about a haunted house and the four people who investigate it — the foundational American psychological horror novel and the direct companion to We Have Always Lived in the Castle.", isOptional: true },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'What happened to the Blackwood family?', a: "Six years before the events of We Have Always Lived in the Castle, most of the Blackwood family — John Blackwood (the father), Helen Blackwood (the mother), Aunt Dorothy, and two uncles — died at the dinner table from arsenic poisoning in the sugar. The survivors were Merricat Blackwood (who had been sent to her room without supper as punishment), her older sister Constance (who never ate sugar and so was not poisoned), and their Uncle Julian (who ate a small amount and survived but was permanently disabled and mentally impaired). Constance was tried for the murders and acquitted, but the town assumed her guilt. The novel never definitively answers who poisoned the family, but Merricat's account — unreliable, strange, and increasingly revealing — makes the truth gradually apparent. Jackson withholds the confirmation as long as possible, requiring the reader to piece together Merricat's gradual self-incrimination from her peculiar narration." },
      { q: 'What is the significance of Merricat\'s rituals and beliefs?', a: "Merricat Blackwood is one of the great unreliable narrators in American fiction, and her system of protective rituals — burying talismans, nailing books to trees, marking territories — is both the clearest sign of her psychological damage and her mode of maintaining the isolated world that she and Constance have built. The rituals work within the logic of the novel: they are the grammar of Merricat's power, her attempt to control a world that has always treated her as monstrous. Jackson presents them with perfect deadpan seriousness, never confirming or denying their efficacy, maintaining the ambiguity between Merricat's belief and the reader's skepticism. The rituals are also about the desire for an enclosed, sufficient world — a world in which the two sisters are enough for each other and the outside with its cruelty can be kept at bay." },
      { q: 'How does We Have Always Lived in the Castle relate to Jackson\'s life?', a: "Shirley Jackson suffered from agoraphobia and social anxiety throughout much of her adult life, and We Have Always Lived in the Castle is her most direct fictional treatment of the desire to withdraw from a hostile social world into an enclosed domestic space. The novel's portrait of the sisters' life in the Blackwood mansion — their rituals, their sufficiency to each other, their contempt for the village that has shunned them — reflects Jackson's own experience of feeling excluded and monstrous in her Vermont community. Jackson died in 1965, a year after the novel's publication, at the age of forty-eight, from a heart attack while sleeping; she left several unfinished manuscripts. We Have Always Lived in the Castle is widely considered her masterpiece and one of the finest American novels of the twentieth century." },
    ],
